window.CHAPTER_DATA = {
  "1": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch1\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">1</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 1: Chemical Reactions and Equations</h2>\n      <p>Balancing Equations, Types of Reactions (Combination, Decomposition, Displacement, Redox) & Everyday Effects — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 6, 10, 13) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 20)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 6 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why should a magnesium ribbon be cleaned before burning in air?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scientific Reason:</strong></p>\n<p>Magnesium is an active metal. When exposed to atmospheric air, it slowly reacts with oxygen to form a thin, stable, and protective inert layer of <strong>basic magnesium oxide ($MgO$)</strong> and magnesium carbonate on its surface:</p>\n$$2\\text{Mg}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{MgO}(s)$$\n<p>This protective layer acts as a barrier that prevents direct contact between underlying magnesium metal and oxygen, hindering combustion. Rubbing with sandpaper removes this inert oxide coating, exposing fresh reactive magnesium so that it ignites smoothly with a dazzling white flame.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identification of basic magnesium oxide (MgO) protective layer</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining sandpaper removal exposes fresh metal for smooth combustion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 6 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write the balanced equation for the following chemical reactions: (i) Hydrogen + Chlorine &rarr; Hydrogen chloride, (ii) Barium chloride + Aluminium sulphate &rarr; Barium sulphate + Aluminium chloride, (iii) Sodium + Water &rarr; Sodium hydroxide + Hydrogen.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Balanced Chemical Equations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Hydrogen + Chlorine &rarr; Hydrogen chloride:</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{Cl}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{HCl}(g)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Barium chloride + Aluminium sulphate &rarr; Barium sulphate + Aluminium chloride:</strong><br>\n    $$3\\text{BaCl}_2(aq) + \\text{Al}_2(\\text{SO}_4)_3(aq) \\longrightarrow 3\\text{BaSO}_4(s)\\downarrow + 2\\text{AlCl}_3(aq)$$\n    <em>Note:</em> Barium sulphate is formed as an insoluble dense white precipitate.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Sodium + Water &rarr; Sodium hydroxide + Hydrogen:</strong><br>\n    $$2\\text{Na}(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{NaOH}(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow + \\text{Heat}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (i): H2 + Cl2 -> 2HCl</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (ii): 3BaCl2 + Al2(SO4)3 -> 3BaSO4 + 2AlCl3</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (iii): 2Na + 2H2O -> 2NaOH + H2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 6 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write a balanced chemical equation with state symbols for the following reactions: (i) Solutions of barium chloride and sodium sulphate in water react to give insoluble barium sulphate and the solution of sodium chloride. (ii) Sodium hydroxide solution in water reacts with hydrochloric acid solution to produce sodium chloride solution and water.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Balanced Equations with Physical State Symbols:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Precipitation Reaction:</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{BaCl}_2(aq) + \\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4(s)\\downarrow + 2\\text{NaCl}(aq)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Neutralisation Reaction:</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{NaOH}(aq) + \\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{NaCl}(aq) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced precipitation equation with (aq) and (s) state symbols</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced neutralisation equation with (aq) and (l) state symbols</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 10 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A solution of a substance 'X' is used for white-washing. (i) Name the substance 'X' and write its formula. (ii) Write the reaction of the substance 'X' named in (i) above with water.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Identification and Reaction of Substance 'X':</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Name and Formula:</strong><br>\n    Substance 'X' is <strong>Calcium oxide</strong> (commonly called <strong>Quicklime</strong>).<br>\n    <strong>Chemical Formula:</strong> $\\text{CaO}$.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Reaction with Water:</strong><br>\n    Quicklime reacts vigorously with water in a highly exothermic reaction to produce slaked lime:\n    $$\\text{CaO}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(aq) + \\text{Heat}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Name (Calcium oxide / Quicklime) and formula (CaO)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equation with water forming Ca(OH)2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 10 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the amount of gas collected in one of the test tubes in Activity 1.7 (electrolysis of water) double of the amount collected in the other? Name this gas.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electrolysis of Water Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Water molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom in a $2:1$ ratio by volume ($H_2O$). During electrolysis of acidulated water:</p>\n$$2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Electricity}} 2\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g)$$\n<p>By stoichiometric mole ratio and Avogadro's Law, 2 volumes of hydrogen gas are liberated at the cathode for every 1 volume of oxygen gas liberated at the anode. Hence, the gas collected in double the volume is <strong>Hydrogen gas ($\\text{H}_2$)</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced electrolysis equation showing 2:1 stoichiometric mole ratio</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying the gas as Hydrogen collected at cathode</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 13 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the colour of copper sulphate solution change when an iron nail is dipped in it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Displacement Reaction Mechanism:</strong></p>\n<p>Iron ($\\text{Fe}$) is more reactive than copper ($\\text{Cu}$) in the reactivity series. When an iron nail is dipped in blue copper sulphate solution, iron displaces copper:</p>\n$$\\text{Fe}(s) + \\text{CuSO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{FeSO}_4(aq) + \\text{Cu}(s)$$\n$$\\text{(Grey)} + \\text{(Blue)} \\longrightarrow \\text{(Light Green)} + \\text{(Reddish Brown)}$$\n<p>The blue colour fades and changes to pale green due to formation of $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$ (ferrous sulphate) ions, and displaced copper deposits as a reddish-brown coating on the iron nail.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Iron is more reactive than copper and displaces it</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation and colour change: blue to light green (FeSO4)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 13 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give an example of a double displacement reaction other than the one given in Activity 1.10 (between barium chloride and sodium sulphate).</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Example of Double Displacement Reaction:</strong></p>\n<p>Reaction between aqueous Lead(II) nitrate and Potassium iodide:</p>\n$$\\text{Pb(NO}_3)_2(aq) + 2\\text{KI}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{PbI}_2(s)\\downarrow + 2\\text{KNO}_3(aq)$$\n<p><strong>Observation:</strong> Exchange of ions takes place, resulting in the instantaneous formation of a bright <strong>yellow precipitate of Lead iodide ($\\text{PbI}_2$)</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct reactants: Lead nitrate and Potassium iodide</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation with yellow precipitate PbI2 identified</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 13 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Identify the substances that are oxidised and the substances that are reduced in the following reactions: (i) $4\\text{Na}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{Na}_2\\text{O}(s)$, (ii) $\\text{CuO}(s) + \\text{H}_2(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{Cu}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Oxidation and Reduction Analysis:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) $4\\text{Na}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{Na}_2\\text{O}(s)$:</strong><br>\n    - <strong>Substance Oxidised:</strong> Sodium ($\\text{Na}$) gains oxygen to form $\\text{Na}_2\\text{O}$.<br>\n    - <strong>Substance Reduced:</strong> Oxygen ($\\text{O}_2$) is reduced.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) $\\text{CuO}(s) + \\text{H}_2(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{Cu}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$:</strong><br>\n    - <strong>Substance Oxidised:</strong> Hydrogen ($\\text{H}_2$) gains oxygen to form water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$).<br>\n    - <strong>Substance Reduced:</strong> Copper(II) oxide ($\\text{CuO}$) loses oxygen to form copper ($\\text{Cu}$).\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Part (i): Na oxidised, O2 reduced</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Part (ii): H2 oxidised, CuO reduced</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q1 (MCQ)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the statements about the reaction below are incorrect? $2\\text{PbO}(s) + \\text{C}(s) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{Pb}(s) + \\text{CO}_2(g)$<br>(a) Lead is getting reduced.<br>(b) Carbon dioxide is getting oxidised.<br>(c) Carbon is getting oxidised.<br>(d) Lead oxide is getting reduced.<br>Choices: (i) (a) and (b), (ii) (a) and (c), (iii) (a), (b) and (c), (iv) all.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Answer: (i) (a) and (b) are incorrect.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> In this redox reaction:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>$\\text{PbO}$ loses oxygen &rarr; Lead oxide ($\\text{PbO}$) is being reduced (statement (d) is correct, (a) is incorrect because lead oxide, not lead, is being reduced).</li>\n  <li>$\\text{C}$ gains oxygen &rarr; Carbon ($\\text{C}$) is being oxidised (statement (c) is correct, (b) is incorrect because carbon, not carbon dioxide, is being oxidised).</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Selecting option (i) with scientific reasoning regarding reactants oxidised/reduced</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q2 (MCQ)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">$\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 + 2\\text{Al} \\longrightarrow \\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3 + 2\\text{Fe}$. The above reaction is an example of a:<br>(a) combination reaction, (b) double displacement reaction, (c) decomposition reaction, (d) displacement reaction.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Answer: (d) displacement reaction.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Aluminium ($\\text{Al}$) is more reactive than iron ($\\text{Fe}$). It displaces iron from ferric oxide to form aluminium oxide and molten iron (Thermite reaction).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) displacement reaction with reactivity reasoning</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q3 (MCQ)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What happens when dilute hydrochloric acid is added to iron filings? Tick the correct answer.<br>(a) Hydrogen gas and iron chloride are produced.<br>(b) Chlorine gas and iron hydroxide are produced.<br>(c) No reaction takes place.<br>(d) Iron salt and water are produced.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Answer: (a) Hydrogen gas and iron chloride are produced.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reaction:</strong> $\\text{Fe}(s) + 2\\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{FeCl}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (a) with balanced chemical equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is a balanced chemical equation? Why should chemical equations be balanced?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Definition:</strong> A chemical equation in which the total number of atoms of each element is equal on both the reactant side and the product side is called a <strong>balanced chemical equation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Reason for Balancing:</strong> Chemical equations must be balanced to satisfy the <strong>Law of Conservation of Mass</strong> (formulated by Antoine Lavoisier), which states that mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. Therefore, the total mass of reactants must equal the total mass of products.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of balanced chemical equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Application of Law of Conservation of Mass</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Translate the following statements into chemical equations and then balance them:<br>(a) Hydrogen gas combines with nitrogen to form ammonia.<br>(b) Hydrogen sulphide gas burns in air to give water and sulphur dioxide.<br>(c) Barium chloride reacts with aluminium sulphate to give aluminium chloride and a precipitate of barium sulphate.<br>(d) Potassium metal reacts with water to give potassium hydroxide and hydrogen gas.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Balanced Equations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a)</strong> $3\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{N}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{NH}_3(g)$</li>\n  <li><strong>(b)</strong> $2\\text{H}_2\\text{S}(g) + 3\\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + 2\\text{SO}_2(g)$</li>\n  <li><strong>(c)</strong> $3\\text{BaCl}_2(aq) + \\text{Al}_2(\\text{SO}_4)_3(aq) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{AlCl}_3(aq) + 3\\text{BaSO}_4(s)\\downarrow$</li>\n  <li><strong>(d)</strong> $2\\text{K}(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{KOH}(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equation (a) balanced correctly</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equation (b) balanced correctly</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equation (c) balanced correctly</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equation (d) balanced correctly</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Balance the following chemical equations:<br>(a) $\\text{HNO}_3 + \\text{Ca(OH)}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{Ca(NO}_3)_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$<br>(b) $\\text{NaOH} + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\longrightarrow \\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$<br>(c) $\\text{NaCl} + \\text{AgNO}_3 \\longrightarrow \\text{AgCl} + \\text{NaNO}_3$<br>(d) $\\text{BaCl}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\longrightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4 + \\text{HCl}$</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Balanced Equations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a)</strong> $2\\text{HNO}_3 + \\text{Ca(OH)}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{Ca(NO}_3)_2 + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$</li>\n  <li><strong>(b)</strong> $2\\text{NaOH} + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\longrightarrow \\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4 + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$</li>\n  <li><strong>(c)</strong> $\\text{NaCl} + \\text{AgNO}_3 \\longrightarrow \\text{AgCl}\\downarrow + \\text{NaNO}_3$ (Already balanced)</li>\n  <li><strong>(d)</strong> $\\text{BaCl}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\longrightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4\\downarrow + 2\\text{HCl}$</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equation (a) with coefficient 2 for HNO3 and H2O</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equation (b) with coefficient 2 for NaOH and H2O</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equation (c) verified balanced stoichiometry</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equation (d) with coefficient 2 for HCl</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write the balanced chemical equations for the following reactions:<br>(a) Calcium hydroxide + Carbon dioxide &rarr; Calcium carbonate + Water<br>(b) Zinc + Silver nitrate &rarr; Zinc nitrate + Silver<br>(c) Aluminium + Copper chloride &rarr; Aluminium chloride + Copper<br>(d) Barium chloride + Potassium sulphate &rarr; Barium sulphate + Potassium chloride</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Balanced Chemical Equations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a)</strong> $\\text{Ca(OH)}_2(aq) + \\text{CO}_2(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{CaCO}_3(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$</li>\n  <li><strong>(b)</strong> $\\text{Zn}(s) + 2\\text{AgNO}_3(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{Zn(NO}_3)_2(aq) + 2\\text{Ag}(s)$</li>\n  <li><strong>(c)</strong> $2\\text{Al}(s) + 3\\text{CuCl}_2(aq) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{AlCl}_3(aq) + 3\\text{Cu}(s)$</li>\n  <li><strong>(d)</strong> $\\text{BaCl}_2(aq) + \\text{K}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4(s)\\downarrow + 2\\text{KCl}(aq)$</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (a) balanced</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (b) balanced</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (c) balanced</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (d) balanced</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write the balanced chemical equation for the following and identify the type of reaction in each case:<br>(a) Potassium bromide(aq) + Barium iodide(aq) &rarr; Potassium iodide(aq) + Barium bromide(s)<br>(b) Zinc carbonate(s) &rarr; Zinc oxide(s) + Carbon dioxide(g)<br>(c) Hydrogen(g) + Chlorine(g) &rarr; Hydrogen chloride(g)<br>(d) Magnesium(s) + Hydrochloric acid(aq) &rarr; Magnesium chloride(aq) + Hydrogen(g)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Balanced Equations and Types:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a)</strong> $2\\text{KBr}(aq) + \\text{BaI}_2(aq) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{KI}(aq) + \\text{BaBr}_2(s)$<br>\n    <em>Type:</em> <strong>Double Displacement Reaction</strong> (Precipitation).</li>\n  <li><strong>(b)</strong> $\\text{ZnCO}_3(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{ZnO}(s) + \\text{CO}_2(g)$<br>\n    <em>Type:</em> <strong>Decomposition Reaction</strong> (Thermal decomposition).</li>\n  <li><strong>(c)</strong> $\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{Cl}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{HCl}(g)$<br>\n    <em>Type:</em> <strong>Combination Reaction</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(d)</strong> $\\text{Mg}(s) + 2\\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{MgCl}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)$<br>\n    <em>Type:</em> <strong>Displacement Reaction</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (a) balanced and identified as double displacement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (b) balanced and identified as decomposition</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (c) balanced and identified as combination</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (d) balanced and identified as displacement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does one mean by exothermic and endothermic reactions? Give examples.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Exothermic Reactions:</strong> Chemical reactions in which heat or energy is evolved along with the formation of products.</p>\n<p><em>Examples:</em></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Burning of natural gas: $\\text{CH}_4(g) + 2\\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{CO}_2(g) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g) + \\text{Heat}$</li>\n  <li>Respiration: $\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6 + 6\\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow 6\\text{CO}_2 + 6\\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{Energy}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Endothermic Reactions:</strong> Chemical reactions in which energy is absorbed from the surroundings in the form of heat, light, or electricity.</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Thermal decomposition of limestone: $\\text{CaCO}_3(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{CaO}(s) + \\text{CO}_2(g)$</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition and balanced example of exothermic reaction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition and balanced example of endothermic reaction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is respiration considered an exothermic reaction? Explain.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Biological and Chemical Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>During digestion, food is broken down into simple glucose ($\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6$). In the cells, glucose combines with inhaled oxygen and undergoes controlled breakdown to produce carbon dioxide, water, and liberates energy:</p>\n$$\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6(aq) + 6\\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 6\\text{CO}_2(g) + 6\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{Energy (ATP)}$$\n<p>Since heat/chemical energy is continuously released to sustain bodily metabolic processes, cellular respiration is an <strong>exothermic process</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating breakdown of carbohydrates to glucose</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced respiration equation and energy liberation deduction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why are decomposition reactions called the opposite of combination reactions? Write equations for these reactions.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Opposite Nature Explained:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In a <strong>combination reaction</strong>, two or more substances combine to form a single substance:\n    $$2\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{Energy}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>In a <strong>decomposition reaction</strong>, a single substance breaks down upon supply of energy to form two or more simpler substances:\n    $$2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Electricity}} 2\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g)$$\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because combination builds a single compound from multiple reactants (usually exothermic) while decomposition breaks a single compound into multiple products (endothermic), they are direct chemical opposites.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Defining combination reaction with balanced equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Defining decomposition reaction with reverse equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write one equation each for decomposition reactions where energy is supplied in the form of heat, light and electricity.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Three Forms of Energy in Decomposition:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Heat (Thermal Decomposition):</strong><br>\n    $$2\\text{FeSO}_4(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + \\text{SO}_2(g) + \\text{SO}_3(g)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Light (Photolytic Decomposition):</strong><br>\n    $$2\\text{AgCl}(s) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Sunlight}} 2\\text{Ag}(s) + \\text{Cl}_2(g)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Electricity (Electrolytic Decomposition):</strong><br>\n    $$2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Electric current}} 2\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g)$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Thermal decomposition equation with states</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Photolytic decomposition equation (AgCl or AgBr)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electrolytic decomposition equation of water or NaCl</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the difference between displacement and double displacement reactions? Write equations for these reactions.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison Table:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:10px 0;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9;\"><th style=\"border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:8px;\">Displacement Reaction</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:8px;\">Double Displacement Reaction</th></tr>\n  <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:8px;\">A more reactive element displaces a less reactive element from its salt solution.</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:8px;\">Two ionic compounds exchange their ions to form two new compounds.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:8px;\">$\\text{Zn}(s) + \\text{CuSO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{ZnSO}_4(aq) + \\text{Cu}(s)$</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:8px;\">$\\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) + \\text{BaCl}_2(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4(s)\\downarrow + 2\\text{NaCl}(aq)$</td></tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Displacement definition and balanced equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Double displacement definition and precipitation equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q14</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In the refining of silver, the recovery of silver from silver nitrate solution involved displacement by copper metal. Write down the reaction involved.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Reaction Involved:</strong></p>\n<p>Copper is more electropositive and reactive than silver in the activity series, so it displaces silver from its solution:</p>\n$$2\\text{AgNO}_3(aq) + \\text{Cu}(s) \\longrightarrow \\text{Cu(NO}_3)_2(aq) + 2\\text{Ag}(s)\\downarrow$$\n<p><strong>Result:</strong> Pure metallic silver precipitates out, and the solution turns blue due to cupric nitrate $\\text{Cu(NO}_3)_2$.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced displacement equation between AgNO3 and Cu</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">State symbols and explanation of silver precipitation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q15</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What do you mean by a precipitation reaction? Explain by giving examples.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition and Example:</strong></p>\n<p>Any chemical reaction in which an insoluble solid (called a <strong>precipitate</strong>) separates out from the solution upon mixing two aqueous solutions is called a <strong>precipitation reaction</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>\n$$\\text{BaCl}_2(aq) + \\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4(s)\\downarrow + 2\\text{NaCl}(aq)$$\n<p>A white precipitate of barium sulphate ($\\text{BaSO}_4$) is instantly formed.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of precipitation reaction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equation with precipitate product</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q16</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the following in terms of gain or loss of oxygen with two examples each: (a) Oxidation, (b) Reduction.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(a) Oxidation:</strong> Chemical process that involves the gain of oxygen (or loss of hydrogen).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Example 1: $2\\text{Cu} + \\text{O}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 2\\text{CuO}$ (Copper gains oxygen to form black copper(II) oxide).</li>\n  <li>Example 2: $\\text{C} + \\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{CO}_2$ (Carbon gains oxygen).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(b) Reduction:</strong> Chemical process that involves the loss of oxygen (or gain of hydrogen).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Example 1: $\\text{CuO} + \\text{H}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Cu} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ ($\\text{CuO}$ loses oxygen to form copper).</li>\n  <li>Example 2: $\\text{ZnO} + \\text{C} \\longrightarrow \\text{Zn} + \\text{CO}$ ($\\text{ZnO}$ loses oxygen).</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Oxidation definition and two balanced equations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reduction definition and two balanced equations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q17</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A shiny brown-coloured element 'X' on heating in air becomes black in colour. Name the element 'X' and the black coloured compound formed. Write the balanced equation.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Identification and Chemical Equation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The shiny reddish-brown element 'X' is <strong>Copper metal ($\\text{Cu}$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li>When copper powder is heated in air, it reacts with oxygen to form a black surface coating of <strong>Copper(II) oxide ($\\text{CuO}$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Balanced Equation:</strong><br>\n    $$2\\text{Cu}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 2\\text{CuO}(s) \\quad \\text{(Black)}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identification of element X as Copper and black compound as Copper(II) oxide</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equation with heat symbol</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q18</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do we apply paint on iron articles?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Rust Prevention:</strong></p>\n<p>Iron rusts when exposed to both atmospheric oxygen and moisture:</p>\n$$4\\text{Fe} + 3\\text{O}_2 + 2x\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\longrightarrow 2\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 \\cdot x\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\quad \\text{(Rust)}$$\n<p>Applying a coat of paint creates an impermeable protective barrier on the iron surface that cuts off direct contact with atmospheric oxygen and water vapor, thereby preventing rusting and prolonging durability.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating iron requires oxygen and moisture to corrode</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining paint acts as physical barrier preventing contact</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q27\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q27')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q19</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Oil and fat containing food items are flushed with nitrogen. Why?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Prevention of Rancidity:</strong></p>\n<p>Oils and fats in food items undergo slow oxidation in air, producing foul-smelling and bad-tasting volatile substances (a process called <strong>rancidity</strong>). Nitrogen is an unreactive, chemically inert gas. Flushing food packets with nitrogen displaces oxygen and provides an inert atmosphere that prevents oxidation of fats, keeping snacks crisp and fresh.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining rancidity caused by oxidation of fats/oils in air</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining nitrogen is inert and displaces oxygen to prevent rancidity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q28\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q28')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Exercise Q20</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the following terms with one example each: (a) Corrosion, (b) Rancidity.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(a) Corrosion:</strong> The slow process of destruction and deterioration of a metal surface by the chemical or electrochemical action of atmospheric gases, moisture, or acids.</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> Rusting of iron (formation of reddish-brown hydrated ferric oxide $\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 \\cdot x\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$) and blackening of silver articles due to formation of silver sulphide ($\\text{Ag}_2\\text{S}$).</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Rancidity:</strong> The aerial oxidation of unsaturated fats and oils present in food items resulting in an unpleasant smell and rancid taste.</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> Butter left at room temperature for several days develops a foul taste and sour smell due to release of butyric acid.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Corrosion definition with balanced example (rusting)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rancidity definition with everyday example</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 1</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Rancidity & Food Preservation</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A food technologist observed that potato chips fried in edible vegetable oil develop an unpleasant foul smell and rancid taste when left in open containers for 3 weeks, whereas chips sealed in commercial bags remain crisp and fresh for 6 months. (i) Name the chemical phenomenon responsible for spoilage. (ii) Which gas is flushed into chips packets to prevent this change and why? (iii) Name two antioxidant additives used in fatty food preservation.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Chemical Phenomenon:</strong> <strong>Rancidity</strong> (specifically oxidative rancidity). Fats and oils in open air undergo slow oxidation by atmospheric oxygen to form volatile carboxylic acids, aldehydes, and ketones with rancid odor.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Gas Used:</strong> <strong>Nitrogen gas ($\text{N}_2$)</strong>. Nitrogen is an unreactive, inert gas at room temperature due to its strong triple covalent bond ($N equiv N$). Flushing with nitrogen expels atmospheric oxygen and provides an inert atmosphere that prevents oxidation of fats.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Antioxidant Additives:</strong> BHA (Butylated Hydroxyanisole) and BHT (Butylated Hydroxytoluene).</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <div></div>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(2)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "2": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch2\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">2</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 2: Acids, Bases and Salts</h2>\n      <p>Properties, Neutralisation, pH Scale Significance in Daily Life, Preparation & Uses of Industrial Salts — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 18, 22, 25, 28, 33) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 15)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 18 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">You have been provided with three test tubes. One of them contains distilled water and the other two contain an acidic solution and a basic solution, respectively. If you are given only red litmus paper, how will you identify the contents of each test tube?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Identification Procedure using Red Litmus Paper only:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Dip a strip of red litmus paper into each of the three test tubes one by one.</li>\n  <li>The test tube in which the red litmus turns <strong>blue</strong> contains the <strong>basic solution</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Now take this newly turned <strong>blue litmus paper</strong> and dip it into the remaining two test tubes.</li>\n  <li>The solution that turns this blue litmus paper back to <strong>red</strong> is the <strong>acidic solution</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The remaining test tube which causes <strong>no colour change</strong> with either red litmus or blue litmus contains <strong>distilled water</strong> (neutral).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Red litmus turns blue identifies the basic solution</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Using the resulting blue litmus to turn red identifies acidic solution</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">No color change on either identifies neutral distilled water</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 22 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why should curd and sour substances not be kept in brass and copper vessels?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Reason:</strong></p>\n<p>Curd and other sour food substances contain organic acids (such as lactic acid). These acids react chemically with the metals copper and zinc (present in brass) to form <strong>toxic, poisonous metal salts</strong> and liberate hydrogen gas:</p>\n$$\\text{Acid} + \\text{Metal (Cu, Zn)} \\longrightarrow \\text{Toxic Metal Salt} + \\text{H}_2(g)$$\n<p>Consuming food contaminated with these toxic copper/zinc compounds causes severe food poisoning and damage to health. Therefore, sour substances should be stored in glass, ceramic, or stainless steel containers.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mentioning curd/sour foods contain organic acids</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction with brass/copper produces toxic poisonous salts causing food poisoning</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 22 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which gas is usually liberated when an acid reacts with a metal? Illustrate with an example. How will you test for the presence of this gas?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Gas Liberated:</strong> <strong>Hydrogen gas ($\\text{H}_2$)</strong> is liberated when an active metal reacts with dilute acid.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Illustrative Example:</strong></p>\n$$\\text{Zn}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{ZnSO}_4(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n<p><strong>3. Confirmatory Test for Hydrogen Gas:</strong></p>\n<p>Pass the evolved gas through a soap solution so that soap bubbles filled with gas rise into the air. Bring a burning candle or burning splinter near the gas bubble; the gas burns with a characteristic <strong>'pop' sound</strong>, confirming the presence of hydrogen gas.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identification of hydrogen gas (H2)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equation with zinc and dilute sulphuric acid</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Burning splinter produces pop sound confirmatory test</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 22 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Metal compound A reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce effervescence. The gas evolved extinguishes a burning candle. Write a balanced chemical equation for the reaction if one of the compounds formed is calcium chloride.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Deduction of Metal Compound A:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The gas evolved extinguishes a burning candle and causes brisk effervescence; therefore, the evolved gas must be <strong>carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Since one of the products is calcium chloride ($\\text{CaCl}_2$) and the gas is $\\text{CO}_2$, the metal compound A must be a calcium carbonate or calcium hydrogen carbonate. In solid form, it is <strong>Calcium carbonate ($\\text{CaCO}_3$)</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Balanced Chemical Equation:</strong></p>\n$$\\text{CaCO}_3(s) + 2\\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{CaCl}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{CO}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying gas as CO2 because it extinguishes burning candle with effervescence</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying compound A as Calcium carbonate (CaCO3)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equation with state symbols</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 25 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do $\\text{HCl}$, $\\text{HNO}_3$, etc., show acidic characters in aqueous solutions while solutions of compounds like alcohol and glucose do not show acidic character?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Acidic behaviour is solely due to the presence of free <strong>hydrogen ions $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ or hydronium ions $\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$</strong> in aqueous solution.</li>\n  <li>Compounds like $\\text{HCl}$ and $\\text{HNO}_3$ are polar covalent molecules. In water, they ionise completely to produce hydronium ions:\n    $$\\text{HCl} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+ + \\text{Cl}^-$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Compounds like glucose ($\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6$) and ethyl alcohol ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}$) also contain hydrogen, but their hydrogen atoms are bonded by non-ionisable covalent bonds. They do not dissociate in water to produce $\\text{H}^+$ or $\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$ ions. Hence, they do not show acidic character or turn blue litmus red.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating acidic character requires H+(aq) or H3O+ ions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ionisation of HCl in water to yield hydronium ions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Absence of ionisation in covalent glucose and alcohol molecules</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 25 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does an aqueous solution of an acid conduct electricity?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Electrical Conduction:</strong></p>\n<p>Electrical conductivity in liquids requires the presence of mobile, charge-carrying particles (ions). When an acid dissolves in water, it undergoes ionisation to produce freely moving cations (hydronium ions, $\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$) and anions (such as $\\text{Cl}^-$, $\\text{SO}_4^{2-}$). Under an electric potential difference, these mobile ions migrate towards oppositely charged electrodes, thereby conducting electric current.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dissociation of acids in water into mobile ions (H3O+ and acid anions)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Migration of mobile charge-carrying ions carries electric current</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 25 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does dry $\\text{HCl}$ gas not change the colour of the dry litmus paper?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong></p>\n<p>Dry $\\text{HCl}$ gas contains covalent hydrogen chloride molecules and exists in the molecular form without any moisture. Acids exhibit acidic properties and ionise to release hydrogen ions ($\\text{H}^+$) only in the presence of water:</p>\n$$\\text{HCl} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+ + \\text{Cl}^-$$\n<p>In the total absence of moisture (dry gas and dry litmus), $\\text{HCl}$ cannot dissociate to form $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ or $\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$ ions. Since hydrogen ions are necessary to effect the colour change of litmus indicator, dry $\\text{HCl}$ gas cannot change the colour of dry blue litmus paper.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Acids dissociate to form H+ ions only in the presence of water</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dry HCl has no free ions, hence dry litmus remains unchanged</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 25 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">While diluting an acid, why is it recommended that the acid should be added to water and not water to the acid?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Thermodynamic and Safety Rationale:</strong></p>\n<p>The dilution of a concentrated mineral acid (such as concentrated $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$) is an <strong>extremely exothermic process</strong> that liberates a large quantity of heat energy.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>If water is added to concentrated acid:</strong> The small drop of water reacts with an immense excess of concentrated acid. The enormous localized heat instantaneously vaporizes the water into steam, causing the corrosive hot acid to <strong>splash violently</strong> onto the face and hands of the operator, causing severe chemical burns. The thermal shock can also shatter the glass container.</li>\n  <li><strong>If acid is added slowly to water:</strong> Water has a very high specific heat capacity. Adding the concentrated acid dropwise down the sides of the container into a large volume of water with constant stirring allows the heat generated to be absorbed and dissipated safely throughout the water without boiling or splashing.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating dilution is strongly exothermic</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Adding water to acid causes localized boiling, splashing, and glass breakage</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct method: slow addition of acid to water with constant stirring to absorb heat safely</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 25 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the concentration of hydronium ions ($\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$) affected when a solution of an acid is diluted?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Effect of Dilution on $[\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]$:</strong></p>\n<p>Dilution means adding more water to an existing acid solution without changing the amount of acid solute. As water is added, the total volume of the solution increases, but the total number of hydronium ions remains essentially the same. Therefore, the <strong>concentration of hydronium ions ($\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$) per unit volume decreases</strong>, resulting in a decrease in acidity and an increase in pH.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Concentration of hydronium ions per unit volume decreases on dilution</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Relating to increase in solution volume</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 25 • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the concentration of hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) affected when excess base is dissolved in a solution of sodium hydroxide?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Effect of Dissolving Excess Base:</strong></p>\n<p>When excess sodium hydroxide (a strong base) is dissolved in an existing $\\text{NaOH}$ solution, it dissociates completely into additional sodium ions ($\\text{Na}^+$) and hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$):</p>\n$$\\text{NaOH}(s) \\xrightarrow{\\text{water}} \\text{Na}^+(aq) + \\text{OH}^-(aq)$$\n<p>Since more $\\text{OH}^-$ ions are added to the existing volume of solution, the <strong>concentration of hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) per unit volume increases</strong>, making the solution more basic and raising its pH.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating that concentration of OH- ions per unit volume increases</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mentioning additional dissociation of added NaOH</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 28 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">You have two solutions, A and B. The pH of solution A is 6 and pH of solution B is 8. Which solution has more hydrogen ion concentration? Which of this is acidic and which one is basic?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Analysis of pH Values:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Hydrogen Ion Concentration:</strong> $\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}[\\text{H}^+]$. A lower pH value signifies a higher hydrogen ion concentration. Therefore, <strong>Solution A (pH = 6)</strong> has a higher $[\\text{H}^+]$ concentration than Solution B (pH = 8). Specifically, $[\\text{H}^+]$ in A is $10^{-6}\\text{ M}$, which is 100 times higher than that in B ($10^{-8}\\text{ M}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Acidic or Basic Nature:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Solution A ($\\text{pH} = 6 < 7$) is <strong>acidic</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Solution B ($\\text{pH} = 8 > 7$) is <strong>basic</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Solution A has higher H+ ion concentration (lower pH = higher [H+])</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Solution A is acidic (pH < 7) and Solution B is basic (pH > 7)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 28 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What effect does the concentration of $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ ions have on the nature of the solution?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Effect of $[\\text{H}^+(aq)]$ Concentration:</strong></p>\n<p>The concentration of $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ ions directly determines whether a solution is acidic, neutral, or basic, as well as the strength of its acidity:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>If $[\\text{H}^+] > 10^{-7}\\text{ M}$ (i.e., $\\text{pH} < 7$), the solution is <strong>acidic</strong>. The higher the concentration of $\\text{H}^+$ ions, the more strongly acidic the solution is.</li>\n  <li>If $[\\text{H}^+] = 10^{-7}\\text{ M}$ (i.e., $\\text{pH} = 7$), the solution is <strong>neutral</strong>.</li>\n  <li>If $[\\text{H}^+] < 10^{-7}\\text{ M}$ (i.e., $\\text{pH} > 7$), the solution is <strong>basic</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Higher [H+] makes the solution more acidic (lower pH)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Distinction between acidic ([H+] > 10^-7), neutral, and basic ([H+] < 10^-7)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 28 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Do basic solutions also have $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ ions? If yes, then why are these basic?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Presence of $\\text{H}^+$ in Basic Solutions:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Yes</strong>, basic solutions also contain $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ ions. In any aqueous solution, water self-ionises according to the ionic product of water ($K_w = [\\text{H}^+][\\text{OH}^-] = 10^{-14}$ at $25^\\circ\\text{C}$):</p>\n$$\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}^+ + \\text{OH}^-$$\n<p>A solution is basic because the concentration of <strong>hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) is much greater</strong> than the concentration of hydrogen ions ($\\text{H}^+$):</p>\n$$[\\text{OH}^-] \\gg [\\text{H}^+]$$\n<p>Because hydroxide ions vastly predominate over hydrogen ions, the solution exhibits alkaline/basic properties.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Yes, basic solutions contain H+ ions due to water auto-ionisation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">They are basic because [OH-] is far greater than [H+]</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 28 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Under what soil condition do you think a farmer would treat the soil of his fields with quick lime (calcium oxide) or slaked lime (calcium hydroxide) or chalk (calcium carbonate)?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Soil Condition and Agricultural Treatment:</strong></p>\n<p>A farmer treats the soil of his fields with quick lime ($\\text{CaO}$), slaked lime ($\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$), or chalk ($\\text{CaCO}_3$) when the soil becomes <strong>too acidic (pH significantly below 6.5)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most agricultural crops grow best in neutral or near-neutral soil (pH $6.5 - 7.5$). Acid rain, excessive application of chemical nitrogenous fertilisers, or decay of organic matter lowers the soil pH, stunting crop growth. Since quick lime, slaked lime, and chalk are basic substances, they react with and <strong>neutralise excess soil acids</strong>, restoring the soil to an optimum pH for healthy plant growth.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Condition: When soil is too acidic (pH < 6.5)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Basic lime/chalk neutralises excess soil acidity to restore optimal pH</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 33 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the common name of the compound $\\text{CaOCl}_2$?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The common name of $\\text{CaOCl}_2$ is <strong>Bleaching Powder</strong> (Chemical name: Calcium oxychloride).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bleaching powder (Calcium oxychloride)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 33 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name the substance which on treatment with chlorine yields bleaching powder.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The substance is <strong>Dry Slaked Lime</strong> (Calcium hydroxide, $\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$).</p>\n$$\\text{Ca(OH)}_2 + \\text{Cl}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{CaOCl}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dry slaked lime / Calcium hydroxide [Ca(OH)2]</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 33 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name the sodium compound which is used for softening hard water.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The sodium compound is <strong>Washing Soda</strong> (Sodium carbonate decahydrate, $\\text{Na}_2\\text{CO}_3 \\cdot 10\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$).</p>\n<p>It precipitates dissolved calcium and magnesium ions as insoluble carbonates, eliminating permanent hardness.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Washing soda / Sodium carbonate decahydrate (Na2CO3 . 10H2O)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 33 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What will happen if a solution of sodium hydrogen carbonate is heated? Give the equation of the reaction involved.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Thermal Decomposition of Sodium Hydrogen Carbonate:</strong></p>\n<p>When a solution of sodium hydrogen carbonate (baking soda, $\\text{NaHCO}_3$) is heated, it decomposes to produce <strong>sodium carbonate</strong>, <strong>water</strong>, and liberates <strong>carbon dioxide gas</strong> with effervescence:</p>\n$$2\\text{NaHCO}_3(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Na}_2\\text{CO}_3(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{CO}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating formation of sodium carbonate, water, and carbon dioxide</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced thermal decomposition equation: 2NaHCO3 -> Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 33 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write an equation to show the reaction between Plaster of Paris and water.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Setting Reaction of Plaster of Paris:</strong></p>\n<p>When Plaster of Paris (calcium sulphate hemihydrate) is mixed with water, it rehydrates and sets in about 10 to 15 minutes into a hard, crystalline solid mass called <strong>Gypsum</strong>:</p>\n$$\\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(s) + 1\\frac{1}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow \\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(s)$$\n$$\\text{(Plaster of Paris)} + \\text{(Water)} \\longrightarrow \\text{(Gypsum - Hard solid mass)}$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct chemical formulas: CaSO4.1/2H2O and CaSO4.2H2O</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced hydration equation producing gypsum</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A solution turns red litmus blue, its pH is likely to be: (a) 1 (b) 4 (c) 5 (d) 10.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) 10</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> A solution that turns red litmus blue is <strong>basic/alkaline</strong> in nature. Bases have a pH value greater than 7 on the pH scale. Among the given choices, only 10 is greater than 7; values 1, 4, and 5 indicate acidic solutions.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) 10 with reason (bases turn red litmus blue and have pH > 7)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A solution reacts with crushed egg-shells to give a gas that turns lime-water milky. The solution contains: (a) $\\text{NaCl}$ (b) $\\text{HCl}$ (c) $\\text{LiCl}$ (d) $\\text{KCl}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) $\\text{HCl}$</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Crushed egg-shells are composed predominantly of <strong>calcium carbonate ($\\text{CaCO}_3$)</strong>. When $\\text{CaCO}_3$ reacts with hydrochloric acid ($\\text{HCl}$), it liberates carbon dioxide gas ($\\text{CO}_2$):</p>\n$$\\text{CaCO}_3 + 2\\text{HCl} \\longrightarrow \\text{CaCl}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{CO}_2\\uparrow$$\n<p>The evolved $\\text{CO}_2$ reacts with lime water ($\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$) to form insoluble white calcium carbonate precipitate, turning lime water milky.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) HCl with reaction explanation and lime water test</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">10 mL of a solution of $\\text{NaOH}$ is found to be completely neutralised by 8 mL of a given solution of $\\text{HCl}$. If we take 20 mL of the same solution of $\\text{NaOH}$, the amount $\\text{HCl}$ solution (the same solution as before) required to neutralise it will be: (a) 4 mL (b) 8 mL (c) 12 mL (d) 16 mL.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) 16 mL</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Volume of $\\text{HCl}$ required for $10\\text{ mL of NaOH} = 8\\text{ mL}$.</li>\n  <li>Volume of $\\text{HCl}$ required for $1\\text{ mL of NaOH} = \\frac{8}{10}\\text{ mL} = 0.8\\text{ mL}$.</li>\n  <li>Volume of $\\text{HCl}$ required for $20\\text{ mL of NaOH} = 20 \\times 0.8 = 16\\text{ mL}$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Since the volume of base is doubled, the volume of acid required for complete neutralisation is also doubled: $2 \\times 8\\text{ mL} = 16\\text{ mL}$.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) 16 mL with direct proportionality calculation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which one of the following types of medicines is used for treating indigestion? (a) Antibiotic (b) Analgesic (c) Antacid (d) Antiseptic.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) Antacid</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Indigestion is caused by the secretion of excess hydrochloric acid in the stomach (hyperacidity). <strong>Antacids</strong> are mild, non-corrosive bases (such as magnesium hydroxide $\\text{Mg(OH)}_2$ or sodium hydrogen carbonate $\\text{NaHCO}_3$) that neutralise excess stomach acid and bring the gastric pH to a comfortable level.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) Antacid with explanation of neutralising stomach acid</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write word equations and then balanced equations for the reaction taking place when: (a) dilute sulphuric acid reacts with zinc granules. (b) dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with magnesium ribbon. (c) dilute sulphuric acid reacts with aluminium powder. (d) dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with iron filings.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Word Equations and Balanced Equations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Dilute sulphuric acid + Zinc granules:</strong><br>\n    <em>Word equation:</em> $\\text{Zinc} + \\text{Sulphuric acid} \\longrightarrow \\text{Zinc sulphate} + \\text{Hydrogen}$<br>\n    <em>Balanced equation:</em>\n    $$\\text{Zn}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{ZnSO}_4(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Dilute hydrochloric acid + Magnesium ribbon:</strong><br>\n    <em>Word equation:</em> $\\text{Magnesium} + \\text{Hydrochloric acid} \\longrightarrow \\text{Magnesium chloride} + \\text{Hydrogen}$<br>\n    <em>Balanced equation:</em>\n    $$\\text{Mg}(s) + 2\\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{MgCl}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(c) Dilute sulphuric acid + Aluminium powder:</strong><br>\n    <em>Word equation:</em> $\\text{Aluminium} + \\text{Sulphuric acid} \\longrightarrow \\text{Aluminium sulphate} + \\text{Hydrogen}$<br>\n    <em>Balanced equation:</em>\n    $$2\\text{Al}(s) + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{Al}_2(\\text{SO}_4)_3(aq) + 3\\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(d) Dilute hydrochloric acid + Iron filings:</strong><br>\n    <em>Word equation:</em> $\\text{Iron} + \\text{Hydrochloric acid} \\longrightarrow \\text{Iron(II) chloride} + \\text{Hydrogen}$<br>\n    <em>Balanced equation:</em>\n    $$\\text{Fe}(s) + 2\\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{FeCl}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (a): Word and balanced equation for Zn + H2SO4</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (b): Word and balanced equation for Mg + 2HCl</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (c): Word and balanced equation for 2Al + 3H2SO4</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction (d): Word and balanced equation for Fe + 2HCl</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Compounds such as alcohols and glucose also contain hydrogen but are not categorized as acids. Describe an activity to prove it.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Experimental Activity: Electrical Conductivity Test</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Setup:</strong> Take a beaker and fix two iron nails on a rubber cork. Place the cork inside the beaker. Connect the nails to the two terminals of a 6-volt battery through a torch bulb and an on/off switch.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test with Acid:</strong> Pour dilute $\\text{HCl}$ solution into the beaker and switch on the current. <strong>Observation:</strong> The bulb glows brightly, proving that $\\text{HCl}$ ionises in water to form mobile ions ($\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$ and $\\text{Cl}^-$) that conduct electricity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test with Glucose and Alcohol:</strong> Thoroughly rinse the beaker. Repeat the experiment separately with aqueous glucose solution and aqueous ethanol solution. <strong>Observation:</strong> In both cases, the bulb does not glow at all.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Although glucose and alcohol molecules contain hydrogen, they do not dissociate into $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ ions in water. Since acidic character is due to the formation of free $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ ions, glucose and alcohol are not categorized as acids.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Experimental setup with cork, nails, battery, bulb, and switch</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Observations: bulb glows with HCl but fails to glow with glucose and alcohol</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deduction: glucose/alcohol do not ionise to give H+ ions, hence non-acidic</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does distilled water not conduct electricity, whereas rain water does?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Distilled Water:</strong> Distilled water is pure water free from all dissolved mineral salts. Because water is a very weak electrolyte, it undergoes negligible auto-ionisation ($[\\text{H}^+] = [\\text{OH}^-] = 10^{-7}\\text{ M}$). Due to the virtually complete absence of free mobile ions, distilled water does not conduct electricity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rain Water:</strong> As rain drops fall through the atmosphere, they dissolve atmospheric gases such as carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$), sulphur dioxide ($\\text{SO}_2$), and nitrogen oxides. Dissolved $\\text{CO}_2$ forms carbonic acid:\n    $$\\text{CO}_2(g) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_2\\text{CO}_3(aq) \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}^+(aq) + \\text{HCO}_3^-(aq)$$\n    The presence of these mobile hydronium and bicarbonate ions enables rainwater to conduct electricity.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Distilled water is pure and lacks mobile ions, hence insulator</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rainwater dissolves atmospheric CO2 forming carbonic acid ions (H+ and HCO3-) that conduct</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q27\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q27')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do acids not show acidic behaviour in the absence of water?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Fundamental Chemical Principle:</strong></p>\n<p>Acidic behaviour (such as sour taste, turning blue litmus red, reacting with metals to liberate $\\text{H}_2$) is strictly caused by the presence of free <strong>hydrogen ions $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ or hydronium ions $\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$</strong>.</p>\n<p>An acid molecule consists of polar covalent bonds. It can only ionise and dissociate to release $\\text{H}^+$ ions when water molecules are present to hydrate and pull apart the ions via dipole-dipole attractions:</p>\n$$\\text{HX} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+ + \\text{X}^-$$\n<p>In the complete absence of water, acids exist entirely as neutral covalent molecules without free hydronium ions, and therefore cannot exhibit acidic behaviour.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Acidic properties depend on the presence of free H+(aq) / H3O+ ions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Covalent acid molecules dissociate into ions only when hydrated by water</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q28\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q28')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Five solutions A, B, C, D and E when tested with universal indicator showed pH as 4, 1, 11, 7 and 9, respectively. Which solution is: (a) neutral? (b) strongly alkaline? (c) strongly acidic? (d) weakly acidic? (e) weakly alkaline? Arrange the pH in increasing order of hydrogen-ion concentration.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Solution Classification based on pH:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>(a) Neutral:</strong> <strong>Solution D (pH = 7)</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Strongly alkaline:</strong> <strong>Solution C (pH = 11)</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>(c) Strongly acidic:</strong> <strong>Solution B (pH = 1)</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>(d) Weakly acidic:</strong> <strong>Solution A (pH = 4)</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>(e) Weakly alkaline:</strong> <strong>Solution E (pH = 9)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arrangement in Increasing Order of Hydrogen-ion Concentration ($[\\text{H}^+]$):</strong></p>\n<p>Since $\\text{pH} = -\\log[\\text{H}^+]$, higher pH corresponds to lower $[\\text{H}^+]$. Therefore, increasing order of $[\\text{H}^+]$ is from highest pH to lowest pH:</p>\n$$\\text{pH } 11 < 9 < 7 < 4 < 1 \\implies \\text{Solution C } < \\text{ E } < \\text{ D } < \\text{ A } < \\text{ B}$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct classification of all five solutions (A to E)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Increasing order of [H+]: C (11) < E (9) < D (7) < A (4) < B (1)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q29\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q29')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Equal lengths of magnesium ribbons are taken in test tubes A and B. Hydrochloric acid ($\\text{HCl}$) is added to test tube A, while acetic acid ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$) is added to test tube B. Amount and concentration taken for both the acids are same. In which test tube will the fizzing occur more vigorously and why?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Observation and Scientific Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Observation:</strong> Fizzing (effervescence due to the rapid liberation of hydrogen gas) will occur much <strong>more vigorously in test tube A</strong> containing hydrochloric acid.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reason:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>$\\text{HCl}$ is a <strong>strong mineral acid</strong> that undergoes almost $100\\%$ complete dissociation in aqueous solution into $\\text{H}^+$ and $\\text{Cl}^-$ ions:\n        $$\\text{HCl} \\longrightarrow \\text{H}^+ + \\text{Cl}^-$$\n        Hence, test tube A contains a very high concentration of $\\text{H}^+$ ions, causing a very rapid reaction with magnesium.\n      </li>\n      <li>$\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$ (acetic acid) is a <strong>weak organic acid</strong> that undergoes only partial dissociation in water:\n        $$\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{CH}_3\\text{COO}^- + \\text{H}^+$$\n        Because $[\\text{H}^+]$ is substantially lower in test tube B, the rate of hydrogen gas evolution is much slower.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating test tube A with HCl shows more vigorous fizzing</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">HCl is a strong acid that completely dissociates yielding high [H+]</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">CH3COOH is a weak acid that partially dissociates yielding lower [H+]</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q30\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q30')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Fresh milk has a pH of 6. How do you think the pH will change as it turns into curd? Explain your answer.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Change in pH during Curdling:</strong></p>\n<p>As fresh milk turns into curd, its <strong>pH will decrease below 6 (becoming more acidic, typically around pH 4.5 – 5.0)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> During curd formation, the bacterium <em>Lactobacillus</em> ferments the natural milk sugar (lactose) into <strong>lactic acid</strong>. The production of lactic acid increases the concentration of hydrogen ions ($\\text{H}^+$) in the milk. Because pH is inversely related to $[\\text{H}^+]$, the increase in lactic acid concentration lowers the pH of the milk.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating pH will decrease below 6 (becomes more acidic)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lactobacillus converts lactose into lactic acid, increasing [H+] and lowering pH</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q31\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q31')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A milkman adds a very small amount of baking soda to fresh milk. (a) Why does he shift the pH of the fresh milk from 6 to slightly alkaline? (b) Why does this milk take a long time to set as curd?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Detailed Answers:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Reason for shifting pH to slightly alkaline:</strong><br>\n    Baking soda ($\\text{NaHCO}_3$) is a mild base. Adding it shifts the pH of fresh milk from 6 to around 7.4–8.0 (slightly alkaline). In an alkaline medium, lactic acid bacteria cannot grow rapidly, and any small amount of lactic acid formed naturally by souring is immediately neutralised. This prevents the milk from spoiling or curdling quickly during warm weather.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Why it takes a longer time to set as curd:</strong><br>\n    For milk to coagulate into curd, its pH must drop significantly to acidic levels (around pH 4.5) to precipitate the casein protein. Because the milk has been made alkaline, the lactic acid produced initially is consumed in neutralising the added baking soda. Only after the alkaline base is completely neutralised can the pH drop below 6 to form curd. Hence, it takes a much longer time to set.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Part (a): Baking soda makes milk alkaline to prevent rapid souring/spoilage</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Part (b): Extra lactic acid is needed first to neutralise the added alkali before pH drops to coagulate curd</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q32\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q32')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Plaster of Paris should be stored in a moisture-proof container. Explain why.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason for Moisture-proof Storage:</strong></p>\n<p>Plaster of Paris ($\\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$) is a hygroscopic powder. When it comes into contact with even atmospheric moisture, it absorbs water and undergoes hydration to form <strong>Gypsum</strong> ($\\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$), setting into a hard, solid insoluble mass:</p>\n$$\\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O} + 1\\frac{1}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\longrightarrow \\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$\n<p>Once it sets into gypsum, it loses its setting ability, plasticity, and usefulness for medical casts or decorative moulding. To prevent this spoilage, it must strictly be sealed in moisture-proof containers.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction with moisture sets POP into hard gypsum (CaSO4.2H2O)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">POP loses its plasticity and moulding capability once hydrated</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q33\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q33')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q14</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is a neutralisation reaction? Give two examples.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>A chemical reaction in which an acid reacts with a base to form salt and water with the evolution of heat is called a <strong>neutralisation reaction</strong>:</p>\n$$\\text{Acid} + \\text{Base} \\longrightarrow \\text{Salt} + \\text{Water} + \\text{Heat}$$\n<p>At the ionic level, it involves combination of hydronium ions with hydroxide ions to form water: $\\text{H}^+(aq) + \\text{OH}^-(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Two Standard Examples:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>$$\\text{NaOH}(aq) + \\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{NaCl}(aq) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$</li>\n  <li>$$\\text{Mg(OH)}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{MgSO}_4(aq) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of neutralisation reaction (Acid + Base -> Salt + Water)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Two balanced chemical examples with physical state symbols</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q34\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q34')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q15</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give two important uses of washing soda and baking soda.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Two Important Uses of Washing Soda ($\\text{Na}_2\\text{CO}_3 \\cdot 10\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Used in the manufacture of glass, soap, paper, and sodium compounds such as borax.</li>\n  <li>Used as a domestic cleansing agent for laundry and for removing permanent hardness of water.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>2. Two Important Uses of Baking Soda ($\\text{NaHCO}_3$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Used as an essential ingredient of baking powder to produce $\\text{CO}_2$ gas, making cakes, bread, and pakoras light, soft, and spongy.</li>\n  <li>Used as an active antacid to neutralise excess stomach acidity, and as an ingredient in soda-acid fire extinguishers.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Two correct uses of washing soda (glass/soap manufacture, removing water hardness)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Two correct uses of baking soda (baking powder ingredient, antacid/fire extinguisher)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 2</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Chlor-Alkali Process & Industrial By-Products</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">During the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of sodium chloride (brine), three economically crucial industrial chemicals are co-produced. (i) Name the process and write the balanced overall chemical equation. (ii) Name the gas collected at the anode and the cathode respectively. (iii) Give one major commercial application for each of the three products obtained.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Process Name & Overall Equation:</strong> <strong>Chlor-Alkali Process</strong> (named because 'chlor' stands for chlorine and 'alkali' stands for sodium hydroxide).\n    $$2\\text{NaCl}(aq) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Electricity}} 2\\text{NaOH}(aq) + \\text{Cl}_2(g)\\uparrow + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Gases Liberated at Electrodes:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Anode (+):</strong> Chlorine gas ($\\text{Cl}_2$) is liberated.</li>\n      <li><strong>Cathode (-):</strong> Hydrogen gas ($\\text{H}_2$) is liberated. (Sodium hydroxide solution forms near the cathode).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Industrial Applications:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>$\\text{Cl}_2$:</strong> Water treatment (disinfectant), manufacture of PVC, CFCs, pesticides, and bleaching powder.</li>\n      <li><strong>$\\text{H}_2$:</strong> Fuel for rockets, hydrogenation of vegetable oils to form vanaspati ghee, production of ammonia for fertilisers.</li>\n      <li><strong>$\\text{NaOH}$:</strong> De-greasing metals, manufacture of soaps, detergents, paper making, and artificial textile fibres (rayon).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(1)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(3)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "3": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch3\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">3</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 3: Metals and Non-metals</h2>\n      <p>Physical & Chemical Properties, Amphoteric Oxides, Ionic Compounds & Extraction Metallurgy — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 40, 46, 49, 53, 55) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 16)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 40 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give an example of a metal which: (i) is a liquid at room temperature, (ii) can be easily cut with a knife, (iii) is the best conductor of heat, (iv) is a poor conductor of heat.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Examples of Metals with Specific Properties:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Liquid metal at room temperature:</strong> <strong>Mercury ($\\text{Hg}$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Metal that can be easily cut with a knife:</strong> <strong>Sodium ($\\text{Na}$)</strong> or <strong>Potassium ($\\text{K}$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Best conductor of heat:</strong> <strong>Silver ($\\text{Ag}$)</strong> (closely followed by Copper, $\\text{Cu}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>(iv) Poor conductor of heat:</strong> <strong>Lead ($\\text{Pb}$)</strong> and <strong>Mercury ($\\text{Hg}$)</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Mercury (Hg)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) Sodium / Potassium</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(iii) Silver / Copper</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(iv) Lead / Mercury</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 40 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the meanings of malleable and ductile.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definitions:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Malleability:</strong> The mechanical property of a metal by virtue of which it can be beaten, rolled, or hammered into thin sheets without fracturing or breaking. <em>Gold ($\\text{Au}$) and Silver ($\\text{Ag}$) are the most malleable metals.</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Ductility:</strong> The mechanical property of a metal by virtue of which it can be drawn or stretched into thin, fine wires without snapping. <em>Gold is the most ductile metal (a wire of about 2 km length can be drawn from just 1 gram of gold).</em></li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Malleability definition: capacity to be beaten into thin sheets without shattering</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ductility definition: capacity to be drawn into thin wires</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 46 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is sodium kept immersed in kerosene oil?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Reason:</strong></p>\n<p>Sodium is an extremely reactive alkali metal with a very low activation energy. When exposed to open air at room temperature, it reacts vigorously with atmospheric oxygen and moisture in a highly exothermic reaction, generating sufficient heat to ignite the liberated hydrogen gas and catch fire spontaneously:</p>\n$$4\\text{Na}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{Na}_2\\text{O}(s)$$\n$$2\\text{Na}(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{NaOH}(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow + \\text{Heat}$$\n<p>Kerosene oil is an inert hydrocarbon liquid that does not react with sodium and cuts off direct contact with atmospheric oxygen and water vapor. Therefore, sodium is stored immersed in kerosene oil to prevent accidental fires and ensure safety.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining sodium vigorously reacts with atmospheric oxygen and moisture catching fire</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Kerosene cuts off air and water contact preventing spontaneous combustion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 46 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write equations for the reactions of: (i) iron with steam (ii) calcium and potassium with water.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Balanced Chemical Equations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Reaction of Iron with Steam:</strong><br>\n    Iron does not react with cold or hot water, but red-hot iron reacts with steam to form iron(II,III) oxide (magnetic oxide) and hydrogen gas:\n    $$3\\text{Fe}(s) + 4\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4(s) + 4\\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Reaction of Calcium with Water:</strong><br>\n    Calcium reacts less violently with cold water. The heat evolved is not sufficient for hydrogen to catch fire, and calcium pieces float because bubbles of hydrogen gas stick to its surface:\n    $$\\text{Ca}(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Reaction of Potassium with Water:</strong><br>\n    Potassium reacts violently and instantaneously with cold water, evolving immense heat so that the liberated hydrogen gas catches fire with a lilac/violet flame:\n    $$2\\text{K}(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{KOH}(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow + \\text{Heat}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Iron with steam: 3Fe + 4H2O(g) -> Fe3O4 + 4H2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calcium with water: Ca + 2H2O -> Ca(OH)2 + H2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Potassium with water: 2K + 2H2O -> 2KOH + H2 + Heat</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 46 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Samples of four metals A, B, C and D were taken and added to the following solutions one by one: Metal A gives no reaction with $\\text{FeSO}_4$ and displacement with $\\text{CuSO}_4$; Metal B gives displacement with $\\text{FeSO}_4$; Metal C gives displacement only with $\\text{AgNO}_3$; Metal D gives no reaction with any solution. (i) Which is the most reactive metal? (ii) What would you observe if B is added to a solution of Copper(II) sulphate? (iii) Arrange the metals A, B, C and D in the order of decreasing reactivity.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Deduction of Reactivity:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Most Reactive Metal:</strong> <strong>Metal B</strong> is the most reactive because it is the only metal capable of displacing iron from iron(II) sulphate solution, whereas metals A, C, and D cannot displace iron.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Reaction of Metal B with $\\text{CuSO}_4$:</strong> Since B is more reactive than iron and iron is more reactive than copper, B is significantly more reactive than copper. Therefore, B will displace copper from blue copper(II) sulphate solution, causing the <strong>blue colour of the solution to fade</strong> and a <strong>reddish-brown deposit of copper</strong> to appear.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Decreasing Order of Reactivity:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>B displaces Fe (Most reactive).</li>\n      <li>A displaces Cu but not Fe.</li>\n      <li>C displaces Ag but not Cu or Fe.</li>\n      <li>D displaces none (Least reactive).</li>\n    </ul>\n    $$\\mathbf{B > A > C > D}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Metal B is the most reactive</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) B displaces copper; blue solution fades with reddish-brown copper deposit</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(iii) Decreasing reactivity order: B > A > C > D</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 46 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which gas is produced when dilute hydrochloric acid is added to a reactive metal? Write the chemical reaction when iron reacts with dilute $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Gas Produced & Chemical Reaction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Gas Produced:</strong> <strong>Hydrogen gas ($\\text{H}_2$)</strong> is produced when a reactive metal reacts with dilute acid.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reaction of Iron with Dilute $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$:</strong>\n    $$\\text{Fe}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{FeSO}_4(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n    Ferrous sulphate (light pale green solution) and hydrogen gas are formed.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identification of hydrogen gas (H2)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equation: Fe + H2SO4 -> FeSO4 + H2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 46 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What would you observe when zinc is added to a solution of iron(II) sulphate? Write the chemical reaction that takes place.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Observations and Chemical Reaction:</strong></p>\n<p>Zinc is more electropositive and reactive than iron in the reactivity series. When silvery-grey zinc granules are added to a light pale green solution of iron(II) sulphate, zinc displaces iron:</p>\n$$\\text{Zn}(s) + \\text{FeSO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{ZnSO}_4(aq) + \\text{Fe}(s)$$\n<p><strong>Observable Changes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The light pale green colour of the $\\text{FeSO}_4$ solution gradually fades and becomes completely <strong>colourless</strong> due to the formation of zinc sulphate ($\\text{ZnSO}_4$).</li>\n  <li>A grey/blackish coating of displaced <strong>metallic iron</strong> deposits on the zinc granules.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating pale green solution turns colourless and iron precipitates</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equation: Zn + FeSO4 -> ZnSO4 + Fe</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 49 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">(i) Write the electron-dot structures for sodium, oxygen and magnesium. (ii) Show the formation of $\\text{Na}_2\\text{O}$ and $\\text{MgO}$ by the transfer of electrons. (iii) What are the ions present in these compounds?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electron-dot Structures and Ionic Formation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Electron-dot structures:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Sodium ($\\text{Na}$, $Z=11$: $2, 8, 1$): $\\text{Na}\\cdot$ (one valence electron).</li>\n      <li>Oxygen ($\\text{O}$, $Z=8$: $2, 6$): $:\\ddot{\\text{O}}:$ (six valence electrons).</li>\n      <li>Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$, $Z=12$: $2, 8, 2$): $\\cdot\\text{Mg}\\cdot$ (two valence electrons).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Electron transfer formation:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Formation of $\\text{MgO}$:</strong> Magnesium transfers 2 valence electrons to an oxygen atom:\n        $$\\text{Mg} \\longrightarrow \\text{Mg}^{2+} + 2e^-$$\n        $$\\text{O} + 2e^- \\longrightarrow \\text{O}^{2-}$$\n        $$\\text{Mg}^{2+} + [:\\ddot{\\text{O}}:]^{2-} \\longrightarrow \\text{MgO}$$\n      </li>\n      <li><strong>Formation of $\\text{Na}_2\\text{O}$:</strong> Two sodium atoms each transfer 1 electron to an oxygen atom:\n        $$2\\text{Na} \\longrightarrow 2\\text{Na}^+ + 2e^-$$\n        $$\\text{O} + 2e^- \\longrightarrow [:\\ddot{\\text{O}}:]^{2-}$$\n        $$2\\text{Na}^+ + [:\\ddot{\\text{O}}:]^{2-} \\longrightarrow \\text{Na}_2\\text{O}$$\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Ions present:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>In $\\text{MgO}$: Magnesium cation ($\\text{Mg}^{2+}$) and Oxide anion ($\\text{O}^{2-}$).</li>\n      <li>In $\\text{Na}_2\\text{O}$: Sodium cation ($\\text{Na}^+$) and Oxide anion ($\\text{O}^{2-}$).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct electron-dot structures for Na, O, and Mg</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electron transfer representations for MgO and Na2O</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying cations (Mg2+, Na+) and anions (O2-)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 49 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do ionic compounds have high melting points?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scientific Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Ionic compounds consist of rigid three-dimensional crystal lattices containing closely packed oppositely charged ions (cations and anions) bound together by <strong>extremely strong inter-ionic electrostatic forces of attraction</strong>.</p>\n<p>To overcome and break these immense electrostatic forces and separate the ions into a mobile liquid state, an enormous amount of thermal energy is required. Consequently, ionic compounds (such as $\\text{NaCl}$, m.p. $1074\\text{ K}$, and $\\text{MgO}$, m.p. $3125\\text{ K}$) possess very high melting and boiling points.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Presence of strong electrostatic inter-ionic forces between cations and anions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">High amount of thermal energy required to break rigid ionic crystal lattice</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 53 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Define the following terms: (i) Mineral (ii) Ore (iii) Gangue.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definitions:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Mineral:</strong> Naturally occurring homogeneous inorganic substances containing elements or compounds with a definite chemical composition and crystalline structure found in the earth's crust.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Ore:</strong> A naturally occurring mineral deposit from which a particular metal can be extracted commercially, conveniently, and profitably. <em>(Every ore is a mineral, but every mineral is not an ore; e.g., Bauxite is an ore of Aluminium).</em></li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Gangue:</strong> The commercially worthless rocky, earthy, sandy, and siliceous impurities (such as quartz, clay, limestone) naturally associated with an ore deposit.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mineral definition: natural inorganic compounds in earth crust</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ore definition: mineral from which metal is profitably and conveniently extracted</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gangue definition: unwanted earthy/rocky impurities associated with mined ore</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 53 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name two metals which are found in nature in the free state.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Metals in Free State:</strong> <strong>Gold ($\\text{Au}$)</strong> and <strong>Platinum ($\\text{Pt}$)</strong> (also Silver, $\\text{Ag}$).</p>\n<p>They are noble metals situated at the bottom of the electrochemical reactivity series with very low chemical reactivity and high resistance to oxidation.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gold (Au) and Platinum (Pt)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 53 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What chemical process is used for obtaining a metal from its oxide?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Process: Reduction</strong></p>\n<p>The chemical process used is <strong>Reduction</strong> (removal of oxygen from the metal oxide to yield free metal). The choice of reducing agent depends on the position of the metal in the reactivity series:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Reduction by Carbon (Coke/Carbon monoxide):</strong> Used for moderately reactive metals (Zn, Fe, Pb):\n    $$\\text{ZnO}(s) + \\text{C}(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Zn}(s) + \\text{CO}(g)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Reduction by Highly Reactive Metals (Thermite process with Al):</strong> Used when oxides are strongly bonded:\n    $$\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 2\\text{Al}(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 2\\text{Fe}(l) + \\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + \\text{Heat}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Electrolytic Reduction:</strong> Used for highly reactive metals (Na, Ca, Mg, Al) where carbon cannot reduce their stable oxides.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying process as Reduction (smelting/displacement)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chemical equation: ZnO + C -> Zn + CO (or Thermite reaction)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 55 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Metallic oxides of zinc, magnesium and copper were heated with the following metals: Zinc, Magnesium, Copper. In which cases will you find displacement reactions taking place?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reactivity Order: $\\text{Mg} > \\text{Zn} > \\text{Cu}$</strong></p>\n<p>A metal can displace another metal from its oxide only if it is more reactive (higher in the reactivity series) than the metal present in the oxide.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Metal Oxide</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Zinc ($\\text{Zn}$)</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$)</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Copper ($\\text{Cu}$)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Zinc oxide ($\\text{ZnO}$)</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">No reaction</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1; color:#059669; font-weight:bold;\">Displacement</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">No reaction</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Magnesium oxide ($\\text{MgO}$)</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">No reaction</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">No reaction</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">No reaction</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Copper oxide ($\\text{CuO}$)</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1; color:#059669; font-weight:bold;\">Displacement</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1; color:#059669; font-weight:bold;\">Displacement</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">No reaction</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">ZnO with Mg gives displacement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">CuO with Zn and Mg both give displacement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">MgO gives no displacement with any of the metals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 55 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which metals do not corrode easily? And what are alloys?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Metals that do not corrode easily:</strong></p>\n<p>Noble metals situated at the bottom of the reactivity series, such as <strong>Gold ($\\text{Au}$)</strong>, <strong>Platinum ($\\text{Pt}$)</strong>, and <strong>Titanium ($\\text{Ti}$)</strong>, do not corrode easily because they are chemically inert and unreactive with atmospheric oxygen and moisture.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Definition of Alloys:</strong></p>\n<p>An alloy is a <strong>homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal</strong>, prepared by mixing the components in their molten states in definite proportions and then cooling to room temperature. <em>Examples: Brass ($\\text{Cu} + \\text{Zn}$), Bronze ($\\text{Cu} + \\text{Sn}$), Stainless steel ($\\text{Fe} + \\text{Cr} + \\text{Ni} + \\text{C}$).</em></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Metals that do not corrode: Gold and Platinum</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Alloy definition: homogeneous mixture of metals/non-metals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following pairs will give displacement reactions? (a) $\\text{NaCl}$ solution and copper metal (b) $\\text{MgCl}_2$ solution and aluminium metal (c) $\\text{FeSO}_4$ solution and silver metal (d) $\\text{AgNO}_3$ solution and copper metal.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) $\\text{AgNO}_3$ solution and copper metal</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> In the electrochemical activity series, copper ($\\text{Cu}$) lies above silver ($\\text{Ag}$) and is therefore more reactive than silver. Copper displaces silver from silver nitrate solution:</p>\n$$\\text{Cu}(s) + 2\\text{AgNO}_3(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{Cu(NO}_3)_2(aq) + 2\\text{Ag}(s)$$\n<p>In options (a), (b), and (c), the solid metal is less reactive than the metal cation in solution, so no displacement occurs.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) AgNO3 solution and copper metal with balanced displacement equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following methods is suitable for preventing an iron frying pan from rusting? (a) Applying grease (b) Applying paint (c) Applying a coating of zinc (d) All of the above.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) Applying a coating of zinc (Galvanisation)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Grease and paint cannot be applied to a frying pan because upon heating on a gas stove during cooking, grease will melt, burn, and contaminate food, while paint will char, produce toxic fumes, and peel off. A thin coating of zinc (galvanisation) or using stainless steel alloy withstands high cooking temperatures and permanently prevents rusting.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) Applying a coating of zinc with practical cooking rationale</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An element reacts with oxygen to give a compound with a high melting point. This compound is also soluble in water. The element is likely to be: (a) calcium (b) carbon (c) silicon (d) iron.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(a) calcium</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Calcium reacts with atmospheric oxygen to produce <strong>calcium oxide ($\\text{CaO}$)</strong>:</p>\n$$2\\text{Ca} + \\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow 2\\text{CaO}$$\n<p>$\\text{CaO}$ is an ionic compound with a very high melting point ($2845\\text{ K}$) and dissolves readily in water to form calcium hydroxide (slaked lime):</p>\n$$\\text{CaO} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\longrightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2$$\n<p>Carbon forms gaseous $\\text{CO}_2$; silicon oxide ($\\text{SiO}_2$) is insoluble in water; and iron oxides are also insoluble in water.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (a) calcium with ionic CaO high melting point and water solubility explanation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Food cans are coated with tin and not with zinc because: (a) zinc is costlier than tin (b) zinc has a higher melting point than tin (c) zinc is more reactive than tin (d) zinc is less reactive than tin.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) zinc is more reactive than tin</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Zinc is situated higher than tin in the chemical reactivity series. Organic acids (such as citric acid, malic acid) present in canned food would react with a zinc coating to produce toxic, poisonous zinc salts that cause severe food poisoning. In contrast, <strong>tin is far less reactive</strong>, non-toxic, and does not corrode or react with food acids.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) zinc is more reactive than tin; prevents toxic salt formation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">You are given a hammer, a battery, a bulb, wires and a switch. (a) How could you use them to distinguish between samples of metals and non-metals? (b) Assess the usefulness of these tests in distinguishing between metals and non-metals.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Experimental Differentiation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Testing Procedures:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Malleability Test (Hammer):</strong> Strike the given sample repeatedly with the hammer. If the sample flattens into a thin sheet without breaking, it is a <strong>metal</strong> (malleable). If it shatters into pieces or powder, it is a <strong>non-metal</strong> (brittle).</li>\n      <li><strong>Electrical Conductivity Test (Circuit):</strong> Connect the battery, bulb, switch, and connecting wires with test terminals. Insert the sample into the gap and close the switch. If the bulb glows, the sample is a good conductor of electricity and is a <strong>metal</strong>. If the bulb fails to glow, it is an insulator and is a <strong>non-metal</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Assessment of Usefulness:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>These physical tests are practical, rapid, and generally reliable, but have important exceptions:</li>\n      <li><strong>Exceptions:</strong> Carbon in the form of <strong>graphite</strong> is a non-metal that conducts electricity and will make the bulb glow. <strong>Sodium and potassium</strong> are metals that are soft and can be cut with a knife rather than hammered into sheets. <strong>Mercury</strong> is a liquid metal. Therefore, physical tests should be supplemented by chemical properties for absolute confirmation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hammer test distinguishes malleability (metals) vs brittleness (non-metals)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Circuit test distinguishes electrical conduction (metals) vs non-conduction (non-metals)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Assessment mentioning exceptions like graphite (conducts) and alkali metals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are amphoteric oxides? Give two examples of amphoteric oxides.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>Metal oxides that react with <strong>both acids as well as bases</strong> to produce salt and water are called <strong>amphoteric oxides</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Two Standard Examples:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Aluminium oxide ($\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Reaction with Acid:</em> $\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3 + 6\\text{HCl} \\longrightarrow 2\\text{AlCl}_3 + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$</li>\n      <li><em>Reaction with Base:</em> $\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3 + 2\\text{NaOH} \\longrightarrow 2\\text{NaAlO}_2\\text{ (Sodium aluminate)} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Zinc oxide ($\\text{ZnO}$):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Reaction with Acid:</em> $\\text{ZnO} + 2\\text{HCl} \\longrightarrow \\text{ZnCl}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$</li>\n      <li><em>Reaction with Base:</em> $\\text{ZnO} + 2\\text{NaOH} \\longrightarrow \\text{Na}_2\\text{ZnO}_2\\text{ (Sodium zincate)} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Amphoteric oxide definition: reacts with both acids and bases to yield salt and water</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Two examples (Al2O3, ZnO) with balanced acidic/basic reactions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name two metals which will displace hydrogen from dilute acids, and two metals which will not.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Metals and Reactivity towards Dilute Acids:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Two metals that displace hydrogen:</strong> <strong>Zinc ($\\text{Zn}$)</strong> and <strong>Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$)</strong> (or Iron, Aluminium).\n    <br><em>Reason:</em> They are situated above hydrogen in the reactivity series and have higher electropositivity than hydrogen.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Two metals that will NOT displace hydrogen:</strong> <strong>Copper ($\\text{Cu}$)</strong> and <strong>Silver ($\\text{Ag}$)</strong> (or Gold, Platinum).\n    <br><em>Reason:</em> They lie below hydrogen in the reactivity series and cannot reduce $\\text{H}^+$ ions into $\\text{H}_2$ gas.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Two displacing metals: Zn and Mg (above H in reactivity series)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Two non-displacing metals: Cu and Ag (below H in reactivity series)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In the electrolytic refining of a metal M, what would you take as the anode, the cathode and the electrolyte?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electrolytic Refining Setup:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Anode (Positive electrode):</strong> A thick block or slab of the <strong>impure metal M</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cathode (Negative electrode):</strong> A thin strip of <strong>pure metal M</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Electrolyte:</strong> An acidified aqueous solution of a <strong>soluble salt of metal M</strong> (for example, $\\text{CuSO}_4 + \\text{dil. } \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$ for copper refining).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Operating Mechanism:</strong> On passing electric current, equivalent metal cations dissolve from the impure anode ($M \\longrightarrow M^{n+} + ne^-$) and migrate to deposit as pure metal on the cathode ($M^{n+} + ne^- \\longrightarrow M$), leaving insoluble impurities behind as anode mud.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Anode: Block of impure metal M</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cathode: Thin strip of pure metal M</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electrolyte: Aqueous acidified solution of a soluble salt of metal M</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Pratyush took sulphur powder on a spatula and heated it. He collected the gas evolved by inverting a test tube over it. (a) What will be the action of gas on: (i) dry litmus paper? (ii) moist litmus paper? (b) Write a balanced chemical equation for the reaction taking place.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Observation and Chemical Reaction:</strong></p>\n<p>Heating sulphur in air produces <strong>sulphur dioxide gas ($\\text{SO}_2$)</strong>:</p>\n$$\\text{S}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{SO}_2(g)$$\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Action on Litmus:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>(i) Dry litmus paper:</strong> <strong>No colour change</strong> occurs because dry $\\text{SO}_2$ gas cannot ionise in the absence of moisture.</li>\n      <li><strong>(ii) Moist blue litmus paper:</strong> The moist blue litmus paper <strong>turns red</strong>. Sulphur dioxide dissolves in the moisture to form sulphurous acid ($\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_3$), which dissociates to release $\\text{H}^+(aq)$ ions:\n        $$\\text{SO}_2(g) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_3(aq) \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}^+ + \\text{HSO}_3^-$$\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Balanced Chemical Equations:</strong> Given above for combustion and hydration.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Dry litmus: No change; (ii) Moist blue litmus turns red</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equations: S + O2 -> SO2 and SO2 + H2O -> H2SO3</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State two ways to prevent the rusting of iron.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Two Standard Methods to Prevent Rusting of Iron:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Galvanisation:</strong> Coating iron and steel articles with a protective thin layer of <strong>zinc metal</strong>. Even if the zinc surface gets scratched, zinc being more electropositive oxidises preferentially (sacrificial protection), preventing the underlying iron from rusting.</li>\n  <li><strong>Painting, Greasing, or Alloying:</strong> Applying a coat of oil/grease/paint on the iron surface acts as a physical barrier preventing air and moisture from contacting the metal. Alternatively, iron is alloyed with chromium and nickel to produce <strong>Stainless Steel</strong>, which does not rust.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Method 1: Galvanisation (coating with zinc) with sacrificial protection explanation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Method 2: Barrier coating (paint/oil) or alloying (stainless steel)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What type of oxides are formed when non-metals combine with oxygen?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Nature of Non-metallic Oxides:</strong></p>\n<p>When non-metals combine with oxygen, they generally form <strong>acidic oxides</strong> or <strong>neutral oxides</strong> (covalent oxides):</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Acidic Oxides:</strong> Most non-metal oxides dissolve in water to form acids and turn blue litmus red:\n    $$\\text{CO}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{CO}_3\\text{ (Carbonic acid)}$$\n    $$\\text{SO}_3 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4\\text{ (Sulphuric acid)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Neutral Oxides:</strong> Some non-metal oxides do not react with acids or bases and have no effect on litmus indicators:\n    <br><em>Examples:</em> Carbon monoxide ($\\text{CO}$), Nitric oxide ($\\text{NO}$), Nitrous oxide ($\\text{N}_2\\text{O}$), and Water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Acidic oxides: dissolve in water to produce acids (e.g., CO2, SO2)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Neutral oxides: show neither acidic nor basic properties (e.g., CO, H2O, N2O)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give reasons: (a) Platinum, gold and silver are used to make jewellery. (b) Sodium, potassium and lithium are stored under oil. (c) Aluminium is a highly reactive metal, yet it is used to make utensils for cooking. (d) Carbonate and sulphide ores are usually converted into oxides during the process of extraction.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scientific Explanations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Platinum, gold, and silver for jewellery:</strong> They are noble metals that possess high metallic lustre, remarkable malleability and ductility, and are extremely unreactive. They do not corrode, tarnish, or react with air, water, and sweat, maintaining their brilliant shine indefinitely.</li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Storage under oil for alkali metals:</strong> Sodium, potassium, and lithium have very low ionization enthalpies. In open air, they react vigorously with atmospheric oxygen and moisture, releasing hydrogen gas that immediately catches fire. Storing them submerged under paraffin or kerosene oil shields them from oxygen and moisture.</li>\n  <li><strong>(c) Aluminium for cooking utensils:</strong> When aluminium metal is exposed to air, it naturally forms an ultra-thin, continuous, tough, and impermeable protective coating of <strong>aluminium oxide ($\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$)</strong> on its surface. This oxide film prevents any further reaction with food acids and oxygen. Moreover, aluminium is lightweight, inexpensive, and an excellent conductor of heat.</li>\n  <li><strong>(d) Conversion of carbonate and sulphide ores to oxides:</strong> It is chemically and thermodynamically far easier to reduce a metal oxide to free metal using common reducing agents like carbon (coke) or $\\text{CO}$ than to reduce metal carbonates or sulphides directly:\n    $$\\text{Calcination: } \\text{ZnCO}_3 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{ZnO} + \\text{CO}_2$$\n    $$\\text{Roasting: } 2\\text{ZnS} + 3\\text{O}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 2\\text{ZnO} + 2\\text{SO}_2$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Part (a): Lustrous, malleable, ductile, and corrosion resistant noble metals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Part (b): Highly reactive alkali metals catch fire in air/water; oil provides barrier</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Part (c): Forms self-protecting inert Al2O3 layer and has high thermal conductivity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Part (d): Easier to reduce metal oxides to metals with carbon than carbonates/sulphides</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q27\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q27')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">You must have seen tarnished copper vessels being cleaned with lemon or tamarind juice. Explain why these sour substances are effective in cleaning the vessels.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Cleaning Tarnished Copper:</strong></p>\n<p>When copper vessels are exposed to moist air containing carbon dioxide and oxygen, they slowly react to form a dull green tarnish consisting of <strong>basic copper carbonate $[\\text{CuCO}_3 \\cdot \\text{Cu(OH)}_2]$</strong>:</p>\n$$2\\text{Cu} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{CO}_2 + \\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{CuCO}_3 \\cdot \\text{Cu(OH)}_2\\text{ (Green coating)}$$\n<p>Lemon juice contains citric acid, and tamarind contains tartaric acid. These organic acids react with the basic copper carbonate and basic copper oxide to form <strong>soluble copper citrate/tartrate salts</strong> and water. Rinsing with water washes away these dissolved salts, exposing the fresh, shiny reddish-brown metallic copper surface.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tarnish is basic copper carbonate [CuCO3.Cu(OH)2]</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Citric/tartaric acid dissolves the basic coating forming soluble salts that rinse away</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q28\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q28')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q14</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Differentiate between metal and non-metal on the basis of their chemical properties.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Distinctions between Metals and Non-metals:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Property</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Metals</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Non-metals</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Nature of Ions</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Electropositive; lose valence electrons to form cations ($M \\longrightarrow M^{n+} + ne^-$).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Electronegative; gain electrons to form anions ($X + me^- \\longrightarrow X^{m-}$).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Nature of Oxides</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Form basic oxides (e.g., $\\text{Na}_2\\text{O}, \\text{CaO}$) or amphoteric oxides ($\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Form acidic oxides (e.g., $\\text{CO}_2, \\text{SO}_2$) or neutral oxides ($\\text{CO}, \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Reaction with Dilute Acids</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Active metals displace hydrogen gas from dilute acids ($M + 2\\text{H}^+ \\longrightarrow M^{2+} + \\text{H}_2$).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Do not displace hydrogen gas from dilute acids (cannot donate electrons to $\\text{H}^+$).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Action with Chlorine</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Form ionic chlorides with high melting points (e.g., $\\text{NaCl}, \\text{MgCl}_2$).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Form covalent chlorides that are volatile liquids/gases (e.g., $\\text{CCl}_4, \\text{PCl}_3$).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Distinction 1: Electropositive cations vs Electronegative anions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Distinction 2: Basic/amphoteric oxides vs Acidic/neutral oxides</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Distinction 3: Hydrogen displacement from dilute acids vs no reaction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q29\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q29')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q15</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A man went door to door posing as a goldsmith. He promised to bring back the glitter of old and dull gold ornaments. An unsuspecting lady gave a set of gold bangles to him which he dipped in a particular solution. The bangles sparkled like new but their weight was reduced drastically. The lady was upset but, after a futile argument, the man beat a hasty retreat. Can you play the detective to find out the nature of the solution he had used?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Detective Chemical Deduction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Identity of the Solution:</strong> The impostor used a freshly prepared mixture known as <strong>Aqua Regia</strong> (Latin for 'royal water').</li>\n  <li><strong>Composition:</strong> Aqua regia is a fuming mixture of <strong>concentrated hydrochloric acid ($\\text{HCl}$) and concentrated nitric acid ($\\text{HNO}_3$) in a $3 : 1$ ratio by volume</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why the Bangles Lost Weight:</strong> Gold is a noble metal that does not dissolve in either concentrated $\\text{HCl}$ or concentrated $\\text{HNO}_3$ alone. However, in aqua regia, nascent chlorine is liberated which readily oxidises and dissolves outer layers of metallic gold into soluble auric chloride ($\\text{HAuCl}_4$):\n    $$\\text{Au} + 3\\text{HNO}_3 + 4\\text{HCl} \\longrightarrow \\text{HAuCl}_4 + 3\\text{NO}_2 + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$\n    Dissolving the tarnished outer layer restored sparkle but stripped away grams of valuable gold, causing the drastic weight loss.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying solution as Aqua Regia (conc. HCl + conc. HNO3 in 3:1 ratio)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining dissolution of outer gold layer into soluble chlorauric acid reducing bangle weight</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q30\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q30')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q16</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give reasons why copper is used to make hot water tanks and not steel (an alloy of iron).</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reasons:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Reactivity with Water/Steam:</strong> <strong>Copper does not react with cold water, hot water, or steam</strong> at any temperature, because copper lies below hydrogen in the electrochemical reactivity series. In contrast, iron in steel reacts readily with steam at high temperatures to form iron oxide and hydrogen gas, causing severe internal corrosion and structural weakening:\n    $$3\\text{Fe} + 4\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4 + 4\\text{H}_2$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Thermal Conductivity:</strong> Copper is a far superior conductor of heat compared to steel, ensuring rapid and energy-efficient water heating.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Copper does not react with cold/hot water or steam</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Iron in steel reacts with steam to form rust/Fe3O4, and copper is a better thermal conductor</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 3</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Thermite Welding & Metallurgy</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">Railway tracks in remote sections are joined on-site using the Thermite process rather than conventional electric arc welding. (i) Write the balanced chemical equation for the Thermite reaction. (ii) Why is this reaction classified as a displacement reaction and an exothermic reaction? (iii) In what physical state is the reduced metal obtained and why is that beneficial for track welding?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Thermite Reaction:</strong>\n    $$\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 2\\text{Al}(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 2\\text{Fe}(l) + \\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + \\text{Heat}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Classification:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Displacement Reaction:</strong> Aluminium is more electropositive and reactive than iron; hence aluminium displaces iron from ferric oxide.</li>\n      <li><strong>Exothermic Reaction:</strong> The heat of formation of aluminium oxide ($\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$) is exceptionally high, releasing vast amounts of thermal energy.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Physical State:</strong> The reduced iron is produced in the <strong>molten (liquid) state</strong> because the reaction temperature exceeds $2500^\\circ\\text{C}$ (well above iron's melting point of $1538^\\circ\\text{C}$). This glowing molten iron flows directly into the fissure between broken railway tracks, fusing them seamlessly into a single continuous rail upon cooling.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(2)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(4)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "4": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch4\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">4</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 4: Carbon and its Compounds</h2>\n      <p>Covalent Bonding, Versatile Nature of Carbon, Homologous Series, Nomenclature, Chemical Reactions of Ethanol & Ethanoic Acid, Soaps & Detergents — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 61, 68, 71, 74) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 15)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 61 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What would be the electron dot structure of carbon dioxide which has the formula $\\text{CO}_2$?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electron-dot Structure of Carbon Dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Electronic Configuration:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Carbon ($Z=6$): $2, 4$ (needs 4 electrons to complete octet).</li>\n      <li>Oxygen ($Z=8$): $2, 6$ (needs 2 electrons to complete octet).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Bonding Mechanism:</strong> The central carbon atom shares two pairs of electrons with each of the two oxygen atoms, forming two double covalent bonds:\n    $$\\mathbf{:\\ddot{\\text{O}}::C::\\ddot{\\text{O}}:} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\text{O}=\\text{C}=\\text{O}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Each oxygen atom attains a stable neon octet ($8e^-$) and the central carbon atom attains a stable neon octet ($8e^-$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Valence electron counts: C has 4, each O has 6</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct double covalent bond dot structure O=C=O with complete octets</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 61 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What would be the electron dot structure of a molecule of sulphur which is made up of eight atoms of sulphur? (Hint: The eight atoms of sulphur are joined together in the shape of a ring.)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electron-dot Structure of Octasulphur ($S_8$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Electronic Configuration:</strong> Sulphur ($Z=16$): $2, 8, 6$ (has 6 valence electrons, requires 2 more electrons to complete its octet).</li>\n  <li><strong>Ring Structure (Crown/Puckered Conformation):</strong> In an $S_8$ molecule, each of the eight sulphur atoms forms single covalent bonds with two adjacent sulphur atoms by sharing one electron pair each:\n    $$-S-S-S-S-S-S-S-S- \\quad \\text{(closed ring)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Each sulphur atom retains two non-bonding lone pairs ($4e^-$) and shares two single bonding pairs ($4e^-$), completing its stable octet of 8 electrons.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electronic configuration of sulphur showing 6 valence electrons</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ring arrangement of 8 sulphur atoms with single covalent bonds and octets</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 68 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How many structural isomers can you draw for pentane?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Three Structural Isomers of Pentane ($\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{12}$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. n-Pentane (Pentane):</strong> Straight unbranched five-carbon chain:\n    $$\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_3$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Isopentane (2-Methylbutane):</strong> Four-carbon chain with a methyl branch at C-2:\n    $$\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}(\\text{CH}_3)-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_3$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Neopentane (2,2-Dimethylpropane):</strong> Three-carbon chain with two methyl branches at C-2:\n    $$\\text{CH}_3-\\text{C}(\\text{CH}_3)_2-\\text{CH}_3$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Therefore, exactly <strong>3 structural isomers</strong> exist for pentane.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating there are 3 structural isomers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structures and IUPAC names for n-pentane and 2-methylbutane</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structure and IUPAC name for 2,2-dimethylpropane</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 68 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the two properties of carbon which lead to the huge number of carbon compounds we see around us?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Two Unique Characteristics of Carbon:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Catenation:</strong> Carbon has the unique ability to form exceptionally strong, stable covalent bonds with other carbon atoms, giving rise to long straight chains, branched chains, and closed rings of arbitrary length and complexity. The small size of the carbon atom makes the $C-C$ bond energy very high ($348\\text{ kJ/mol}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Tetravalency:</strong> Having a valency of four, a carbon atom is capable of bonding with four other atoms of carbon or atoms of monovalent elements (H, Cl) or polyvalent elements (O, N, S). It can also form single, double, and triple bonds ($C-C, C=C, C\\equiv C$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Catenation: self-linking property forming stable long chains/rings</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tetravalency: valency of 4 allowing bonding with four atoms and multiple bonds</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 68 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What will be the formula and electron dot structure of cyclopentane?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Cyclopentane:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Molecular Formula:</strong> $\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{10}$ (General formula for cycloalkanes: $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Structure:</strong> A five-carbon ring where each carbon atom is bonded to two neighbouring carbon atoms and two hydrogen atoms via single covalent bonds:\n    $$\\text{Ring of } (-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Electron Dot Structure:</strong> Total valence electrons = $5 \\times 4\\text{ (from C)} + 10 \\times 1\\text{ (from H)} = 30$ valence electrons (15 single covalent electron pairs).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Molecular formula C5H10</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ring structure with single covalent bonds and complete octets</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 68 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Draw the structures for the following compounds: (i) Ethanoic acid (ii) Bromopentane (iii) Butanone (iv) Hexanal. Are structural isomers possible for bromopentane?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structural Formulae:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Ethanoic acid:</strong> $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{COOH}$<br>\n    $$\\text{H}-\\text{C}(\\text{H})_2-\\text{C}(=\\text{O})-\\text{OH}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Bromopentane:</strong> $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2\\text{Br}$</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Butanone:</strong> $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CO}-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_3$ (Ketone group at C-2)</li>\n  <li><strong>(iv) Hexanal:</strong> $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CHO}$ (Aldehyde group at C-1)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Are structural isomers possible for bromopentane?</strong><br>\n<strong>Yes</strong>, structural isomers are possible for bromopentane: 1-bromopentane, 2-bromopentane, and 3-bromopentane (position isomers), plus branched chain isomers like 1-bromo-2-methylbutane.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct structures for Ethanoic acid and Bromopentane</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct structures for Butanone and Hexanal</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Yes, isomers exist for bromopentane (1-bromo, 2-bromo, 3-bromopentane)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 68 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How would you name the following compounds? (i) $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{Br}$, (ii) $\\text{H}-\\text{C}(=\\text{O})-\\text{H}$, (iii) $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{C}\\equiv\\text{CH}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>IUPAC Names:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{Br}$:</strong> <strong>Bromoethane</strong> (two-carbon chain with halo-substituent).</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) $\\text{H}-\\text{CHO}$:</strong> <strong>Methanal</strong> (commonly Formaldehyde; one-carbon aldehyde).</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{C}\\equiv\\text{CH}$:</strong> <strong>Hex-1-yne</strong> (six-carbon straight chain with terminal triple bond).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Bromoethane</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) Methanal</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(iii) Hex-1-yne</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 71 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the conversion of ethanol to ethanoic acid an oxidation reaction?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>The conversion of ethanol to ethanoic acid is an oxidation reaction because it involves the <strong>addition of oxygen and removal of hydrogen</strong>:</p>\n$$\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Alkaline }\\text{KMnO}_4 + \\Delta} \\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$$\n<ul>\n  <li>Ethanol ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6\\text{O}$) contains 1 oxygen atom and 6 hydrogen atoms.</li>\n  <li>Ethanoic acid ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4\\text{O}_2$) contains 2 oxygen atoms and 4 hydrogen atoms.</li>\n  <li>There is a net gain of one oxygen atom and a loss of two hydrogen atoms per molecule, mediated by strong oxidising agents like alkaline $\\text{KMnO}_4$ or acidified $\\text{K}_2\\text{Cr}_2\\text{O}_7$.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of oxidation: addition of oxygen and removal of hydrogen</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Comparison of molecular formulae (C2H6O -> C2H4O2) and oxidising agents</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 71 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A mixture of oxygen and ethyne is burnt for welding. Can you tell why a mixture of ethyne and air is not used?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Combustion Chemistry:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Ethyne (acetylene, $\\text{HC}\\equiv\\text{CH}$) has a very high carbon-to-hydrogen ratio ($92.3\\%$ carbon by mass).</li>\n  <li><strong>When burnt in air:</strong> Atmospheric air contains only about $21\\%$ oxygen by volume. Due to the limited oxygen supply, incomplete combustion occurs, producing a luminous, sooty flame with black smoke (unburnt carbon particles) and a relatively low flame temperature (around $2200^\\circ\\text{C}$), which is inadequate for welding high-melting metals.</li>\n  <li><strong>When burnt in pure oxygen (Oxy-acetylene torch):</strong> Complete combustion occurs according to:\n    $$2\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2(g) + 5\\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 4\\text{CO}_2(g) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g) + \\text{Heat}$$\n    This produces a non-sooty, clean blue flame reaching an extremely high temperature of approximately <strong>$3000^\\circ\\text{C} - 3300^\\circ\\text{C}$</strong>, which instantly melts metals to weld them.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Burning in air results in incomplete combustion, sooty flame, and insufficient temperature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pure oxygen provides complete combustion yielding an intense 3000°C welding flame</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 74 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How would you distinguish experimentally between an alcohol and a carboxylic acid?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Experimental Differentiation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1. Sodium Hydrogen Carbonate Test (Baking Soda Test):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Add a pinch of sodium hydrogen carbonate ($\\text{NaHCO}_3$) or sodium carbonate to both test samples.</li>\n  <li><strong>Carboxylic acid (Ethanoic acid):</strong> Produces brisk effervescence due to the rapid evolution of carbon dioxide gas, which turns lime water milky:\n    $$\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH} + \\text{NaHCO}_3 \\longrightarrow \\text{CH}_3\\text{COONa} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{CO}_2\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Alcohol (Ethanol):</strong> Does not react with $\\text{NaHCO}_3$; no effervescence or gas evolution is observed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Litmus Test:</strong> Ethanoic acid turns blue litmus red; alcohol has no effect on blue litmus.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sodium hydrogen carbonate test: carboxylic acid gives brisk effervescence (CO2)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Alcohol does not react with NaHCO3 / blue litmus stays blue</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 74 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are oxidising agents?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>Substances that have the ability to add oxygen to or remove hydrogen from other substances (i.e., cause oxidation of other substances while undergoing reduction themselves) are called <strong>oxidising agents</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Examples:</em> Alkaline potassium permanganate ($\\text{KMnO}_4$) and acidified potassium dichromate ($\\text{K}_2\\text{Cr}_2\\text{O}_7$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Substances that donate oxygen or remove hydrogen with examples (KMnO4/K2Cr2O7)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Ethane, with the molecular formula $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6$ has: (a) 6 covalent bonds (b) 7 covalent bonds (c) 8 covalent bonds (d) 9 covalent bonds.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) 7 covalent bonds</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> In an ethane molecule ($\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_3$), there is:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>1 single covalent bond between the two carbon atoms ($C-C$).</li>\n  <li>6 single covalent bonds between carbon and hydrogen atoms ($6 \\times C-H$).</li>\n  <li>Total covalent bonds = $1 + 6 = \\mathbf{7\\text{ covalent bonds}}$.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) 7 covalent bonds (1 C-C bond + 6 C-H bonds)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Butanone is a four-carbon compound with the functional group: (a) carboxylic acid (b) aldehyde (c) ketone (d) alcohol.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) ketone</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> The suffix <em>'-one'</em> indicates the presence of a carbonyl group ($>C=O$) attached to two carbon atoms, which defines a <strong>ketone</strong> (Formula: $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CO}-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_3$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) ketone with formula CH3COCH2CH3</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">While cooking, if the bottom of the vessel is getting blackened on the outside, it means that: (a) the food is not cooked completely (b) the fuel is not burning completely (c) the fuel is wet (d) the fuel is burning completely.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) the fuel is not burning completely</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Blackening of cooking vessels occurs due to the deposition of unburnt carbon particles (soot). This indicates insufficient oxygen supply (air holes in the burner are clogged), resulting in <strong>incomplete combustion</strong> of the hydrocarbon fuel.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) the fuel is not burning completely due to incomplete combustion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the nature of the covalent bond using the bond formation in $\\text{CH}_3\\text{Cl}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Bond Formation in Chloromethane ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{Cl}$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Valence Electron Count:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Carbon ($Z=6$): $2, 4$ (needs 4 electrons).</li>\n      <li>Hydrogen ($Z=1$): $1$ (needs 1 electron).</li>\n      <li>Chlorine ($Z=17$): $2, 8, 7$ (needs 1 electron).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Sharing of Electron Pairs:</strong> Carbon shares one electron each with three hydrogen atoms to form three $C-H$ single covalent bonds. It also shares its fourth valence electron with one chlorine atom to form a $C-Cl$ single covalent bond.</li>\n  <li><strong>Covalent & Polar Nature:</strong> All four bonds are formed by mutual sharing of electrons (covalent). However, chlorine is substantially more electronegative than carbon, pulling the shared pair of electrons towards itself. This creates a partial negative charge on chlorine ($\\delta^-$) and a partial positive charge on carbon ($\\delta^+$), giving the $C-Cl$ bond a <strong>polar covalent nature</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mutual sharing of electron pairs completes octet of C and Cl and duplet of H</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining polar covalent nature due to higher electronegativity of chlorine</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Draw the electron dot structures for: (a) Ethanoic acid (b) $\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$ (c) Propanone (d) $\\text{F}_2$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electron Dot Structures:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Ethanoic acid ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$):</strong>\n    $$\\text{H}_3\\text{C}-\\text{C}(=\\text{O})-\\text{O}-\\text{H}$$\n    Central carbon is double-bonded to carbonyl oxygen (sharing 2 pairs) and single-bonded to hydroxyl oxygen (sharing 1 pair).\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Hydrogen sulphide ($\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$):</strong>\n    Sulphur has 6 valence electrons. It shares 1 electron each with two hydrogen atoms and retains two lone pairs:\n    $$\\text{H}:\\ddot{\\text{S}}:\\text{H}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(c) Propanone ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{COCH}_3$):</strong>\n    Central carbon shares a double bond with oxygen ($:C::\\ddot{\\text{O}}:$) and single bonds with two methyl carbons.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(d) Fluorine molecule ($\\text{F}_2$):</strong>\n    Each fluorine atom has 7 valence electrons and shares 1 electron pair:\n    $$:\\ddot{\\text{F}}:\\ddot{\\text{F}}:$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structures for ethanoic acid and H2S with lone pairs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structures for propanone and F2 with complete octets</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is an homologous series? Explain with an example.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>A series of structurally related organic compounds in which the same functional group substitutes for hydrogen in a carbon chain, such that successive members differ by a <strong>$-\\text{CH}_2-$ unit</strong> (methylene group) and by <strong>14 atomic mass units (u)</strong>, is called an <strong>homologous series</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Example: Homologous Series of Alkanes (General formula: $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+2}$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Methane ($\\text{CH}_4$, mass = $16\\text{ u}$)</li>\n  <li>Ethane ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6$, mass = $30\\text{ u}$, difference = $-\\text{CH}_2-$ and $14\\text{ u}$)</li>\n  <li>Propane ($\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_8$, mass = $44\\text{ u}$)</li>\n  <li>Butane ($\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_{10}$, mass = $58\\text{ u}$)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>All members exhibit identical chemical properties but show a gradual gradation in physical properties (boiling point, melting point, density).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition: successive members differ by -CH2- unit and 14 u with same functional group</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Alkane series example with formulas and gradation in physical properties</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How can ethanol and ethanoic acid be differentiated on the basis of their physical and chemical properties?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison Table:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Property</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Ethanol ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}$)</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Ethanoic Acid ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Smell & Melting Pt</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Pleasant spirituous smell; m.p. $156\\text{ K}$ (stays liquid in winter).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Pungent vinegar-like smell; m.p. $290\\text{ K}$ ($17^\\circ\\text{C}$, freezes into glacial ice-like solid).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Litmus Test</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Neutral; no effect on red or blue litmus.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Acidic; turns blue litmus red.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>$\\text{NaHCO}_3$ Reaction</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">No reaction; no gas evolved.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Brisk effervescence of $\\text{CO}_2$ gas that turns lime water milky.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Physical differentiation: vinegar smell/freezing point vs pleasant spirit</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chemical differentiation: NaHCO3 brisk effervescence vs no reaction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Litmus differentiation: blue litmus turns red vs neutral</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does micelle formation take place when soap is added to water? Will a micelle be formed in other solvents such as ethanol also?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Micelle Formation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Soap Molecule Structure:</strong> A soap molecule (e.g., sodium stearate $\\text{C}_{17}\\text{H}_{35}\\text{COO}^-\\text{Na}^+$) has two distinct parts:\n    <ul>\n      <li>A long hydrocarbon tail which is <strong>hydrophobic (water-fearing)</strong> and lipophilic (oil-loving).</li>\n      <li>An ionic carboxylate head ($-\\text{COO}^-\\text{Na}^+$) which is <strong>hydrophilic (water-loving)</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Micelle Assembly in Water:</strong> When dissolved in water, the hydrophobic hydrocarbon tails cluster together inside the core to escape contact with water, while the hydrophilic ionic heads point outwards into the aqueous medium. This spherical aggregate is called a <strong>micelle</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Will Micelles Form in Ethanol?</strong><br>\n    <strong>No</strong>, micelles will not form in ethanol. Ethanol is an organic solvent that is non-polar/mildly polar. The hydrocarbon tails of soap are soluble in ethanol, so they dissolve uniformly throughout ethanol rather than aggregating into micelles.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Soap molecule dual structure: hydrophobic tail and hydrophilic ionic head</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explanation of spherical micelle aggregation shielding tails from water</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Micelles do not form in ethanol because hydrophobic tails dissolve in ethanol</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why are carbon and its compounds used as fuels for most applications?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reasons:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>High Calorific Value:</strong> When carbon and hydrocarbons undergo combustion in air, strong carbon-oxygen ($C=O$) and hydrogen-oxygen ($O-H$) bonds are formed. This releases a large quantity of thermal energy per unit mass:\n    $$\\text{C} + \\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{CO}_2 + \\text{Heat and Light}$$\n    $$\\text{CH}_4 + 2\\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{CO}_2 + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O} + 890\\text{ kJ/mol}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Optimum Ignition Temperature & Controllable Burn Rate:</strong> Carbon fuels have moderate ignition temperatures and can be stored, transported, and combusted safely at controllable rates without explosive hazards.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">High calorific value / exothermic combustion releasing large heat energy</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Proper ignition temperature and controllable clean burning</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the formation of scum when hard water is treated with soap.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Formation of Scum:</strong></p>\n<p>Hard water contains dissolved salts of calcium and magnesium, such as calcium hydrogen carbonate $\\text{Ca(HCO}_3)_2$ or magnesium sulphate $\\text{MgSO}_4$.</p>\n<p>When soap (sodium stearate, $\\text{C}_{17}\\text{H}_{35}\\text{COONa}$) is added to hard water, the sodium ions in soap are replaced by calcium and magnesium ions. This produces <strong>calcium and magnesium stearates</strong>, which are insoluble in water and separate as a curdy, sticky white precipitate called <strong>scum</strong>:</p>\n$$2\\text{C}_{17}\\text{H}_{35}\\text{COONa} + \\text{Ca}^{2+} \\longrightarrow (\\text{C}_{17}\\text{H}_{35}\\text{COO})_2\\text{Ca}\\downarrow\\text{ (Scum)} + 2\\text{Na}^+$$\n<p>This scum wastes soap and sticks to fabrics, hindering lather formation until all $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\\text{Mg}^{2+}$ ions are precipitated.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hard water contains dissolved calcium and magnesium ions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction of soap with Ca2+/Mg2+ precipitates insoluble calcium/magnesium stearate (scum)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What change will you observe if you test soap with litmus paper (red and blue)?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Observation and Chemical Reason:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Observation:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Red litmus paper turns blue.</strong></li>\n      <li><strong>Blue litmus paper remains blue.</strong></li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> Soap is a sodium or potassium salt of a weak long-chain carboxylic acid (fatty acid) and a strong base (sodium hydroxide, $\\text{NaOH}$). When dissolved in water, soap undergoes anionic hydrolysis:\n    $$\\text{RCOO}^- + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{RCOOH} + \\text{OH}^-$$\n    The release of excess hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) makes the soap solution <strong>basic/alkaline</strong> in nature ($\\text{pH} \\approx 8.5 - 9.5$). Consequently, it turns red litmus blue.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Red litmus turns blue; blue litmus stays blue</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Soap is a salt of weak fatty acid and strong base, hydrolysing to alkaline solution</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is hydrogenation? What is its industrial application?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition and Reaction:</strong></p>\n<p>The addition of hydrogen to unsaturated hydrocarbons (alkenes or alkynes having double or triple bonds) in the presence of a finely divided catalyst (such as <strong>Nickel ($\\text{Ni}$)</strong> or Palladium) to convert them into saturated hydrocarbons is called <strong>hydrogenation</strong>:</p>\n$$\\text{R}_2\\text{C}=\\text{CR}_2 + \\text{H}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\text{Ni catalyst, } 200^\\circ\\text{C}} \\text{R}_2\\text{CH}-\\text{CHR}_2$$\n<p><strong>Industrial Application:</strong></p>\n<p>Hydrogenation is widely used in the food industry to convert liquid unsaturated vegetable oils (which contain healthy double bonds) into solid edible saturated fats known as <strong>Vanaspati ghee (margarine)</strong>:</p>\n$$\\text{Vegetable oil (liquid)} + \\text{H}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\text{Ni}} \\text{Vanaspati Ghee (solid)}$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Addition of hydrogen across double bonds in the presence of Ni catalyst</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Industrial conversion of liquid vegetable oils into solid saturated vanaspati ghee</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following hydrocarbons undergo addition reactions? $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6$, $\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_8$, $\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_6$, $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2$ and $\\text{CH}_4$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Analysis of Hydrocarbons:</strong></p>\n<p>Only <strong>unsaturated hydrocarbons</strong> (alkenes $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n}$ and alkynes $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n-2}$) possess carbon-carbon double or triple bonds and undergo addition reactions.</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>$\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6$ (Ethane, $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+2}$): Saturated alkane &rarr; <strong>No</strong> addition reaction.</li>\n  <li>$\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_8$ (Propane, $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+2}$): Saturated alkane &rarr; <strong>No</strong> addition reaction.</li>\n  <li><strong>$\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_6$ (Propene, $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n}$):</strong> Unsaturated alkene &rarr; <strong>YES</strong>, undergoes addition reaction.</li>\n  <li><strong>$\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2$ (Ethyne, $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n-2}$):</strong> Unsaturated alkyne &rarr; <strong>YES</strong>, undergoes addition reaction.</li>\n  <li>$\\text{CH}_4$ (Methane, $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+2}$): Saturated alkane &rarr; <strong>No</strong> addition reaction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>$\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_6$ (Propene)</strong> and <strong>$\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2$ (Ethyne)</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Criterion: only unsaturated hydrocarbons with double/triple bonds undergo addition</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct identification: C3H6 (propene) and C2H2 (ethyne)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q14</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give a test that can be used to differentiate chemically between butter and cooking oil.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Test: Bromine Water Test</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Procedure:</strong> Take about 2 mL of liquid butter and 2 mL of cooking oil in separate test tubes. Add a few drops of reddish-brown <strong>bromine water</strong> (or alkaline potassium permanganate) to each test tube and shake well.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cooking Oil (Unsaturated fat):</strong> Cooking oil contains long unsaturated carbon chains with double bonds. It undergoes an addition reaction with bromine, causing the reddish-brown colour of bromine water to <strong>decolourise and turn colourless</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Butter (Saturated fat):</strong> Butter contains saturated fatty acid chains. It does not react with bromine water under normal conditions; the reddish-brown colour of bromine water <strong>persists unchanged</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bromine water test / alkaline KMnO4 test</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cooking oil decolourises bromine water (unsaturated); butter does not decolourise (saturated)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q15</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the mechanism of the cleaning action of soaps.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Cleaning Mechanism of Soap:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Structure of Soap:</strong> A soap molecule has an elongated structure with two contrasting ends:\n    <ul>\n      <li>A non-polar <strong>hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail</strong> that repels water but dissolves in oil and grease.</li>\n      <li>A polar <strong>hydrophilic ionic head</strong> ($-\\text{COO}^-\\text{Na}^+$) that dissolves in water.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Micelle Formation Around Dirt:</strong> Dirt and grease on soiled clothes are oily and water-insoluble. When soap is added to water containing dirty clothes, the hydrophobic tails embed themselves directly into the oil droplets, while the hydrophilic ionic heads project outwards into the water. This forms spherical aggregates called <strong>micelles</strong> trapping the oil droplet in their center.</li>\n  <li><strong>Emulsification and Removal:</strong> The ionic heads at the surface of micelles carry identical negative charges, causing them to repel each other and prevent coagulation into larger droplets. When the water is agitated (by scrubbing or washing machine), the soap micelles containing trapped grease are pulled away from the fabric into the water, forming a stable emulsion that is easily rinsed away.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hydrophobic tail dissolves in oil; hydrophilic head stays in water</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Micelle formation traps oily dirt at the core</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ionic repulsion prevents coalescence, allowing dirt to rinse away with agitated water</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 4</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Esterification and Saponification</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">An organic compound 'A' of molecular formula $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6\\text{O}$ on heating with excess concentrated sulphuric acid at $443\\text{ K}$ gives compound 'B'. Compound 'A' on oxidation with alkaline $\\text{KMnO}_4$ forms compound 'C'. When 'A' and 'C' react together in the presence of an acid catalyst, a sweet-smelling compound 'D' is produced. (i) Identify compounds A, B, C and D. (ii) Write the chemical equation for the formation of compound D. (iii) What happens when compound D is heated with sodium hydroxide solution? Name this reaction.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Identification of Compounds:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Compound 'A' is <strong>Ethanol</strong> ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH}$).</li>\n      <li>Compound 'B' is <strong>Ethene</strong> ($\\text{CH}_2=\\text{CH}_2$) formed by dehydration of ethanol at $443\\text{ K}$.</li>\n      <li>Compound 'C' is <strong>Ethanoic acid</strong> ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$) formed by oxidation of ethanol.</li>\n      <li>Compound 'D' is <strong>Ethyl ethanoate</strong> (an ester, $\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOCH}_2\\text{CH}_3$) with a pleasant fruity smell.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Esterification Equation:</strong>\n    $$\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH} + \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH} \\xrightarrow{\\text{conc. }\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4} \\text{CH}_3\\text{COOC}_2\\text{H}_5 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Reaction with $\\text{NaOH}$ (Saponification):</strong> Heating ester with $\\text{NaOH}$ hydrolyses it back into alcohol and sodium ethanoate (soap base):\n    $$\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOC}_2\\text{H}_5 + \\text{NaOH} \\longrightarrow \\text{CH}_3\\text{COONa} + \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}$$\n    This reaction is known as <strong>Saponification</strong>.\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(3)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(5)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "5": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch5\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">5</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 5: Life Processes</h2>\n      <p>Nutrition, Respiration, Transportation in Animals & Plants, Excretion and Nephron Physiology — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 81, 87, 91, 96, 100) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 13)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 81 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is diffusion insufficient to meet the oxygen requirements of multicellular organisms like humans?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scientific Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>In unicellular organisms (like <em>Amoeba</em>), the entire surface area of the organism is in direct contact with the external environment; hence simple diffusion across the cell membrane is fast and sufficient to supply oxygen and remove waste.</li>\n  <li>In large, complex multicellular organisms like humans, the body volume is massive and most tissue cells are located deep inside the body, far away from the surrounding atmosphere.</li>\n  <li>Diffusion is a very slow physical transport process ($t \\propto x^2$). Calculations show that if diffusion alone were to transport oxygen in human beings, it would take an estimated <strong>3 years</strong> for a single molecule of oxygen to diffuse from our lungs to our toes.</li>\n  <li>Therefore, multicellular organisms require a specialized respiratory surface (alveoli) coupled with a rapid, pressure-driven <strong>circulatory system containing respiratory pigments (haemoglobin)</strong> to deliver oxygen efficiently to all body cells.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Multicellular body cells are not in direct contact with the environment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Diffusion is too slow for large distances; requires specialised circulatory system with haemoglobin</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 81 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What criteria do we use to decide whether something is alive?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Criteria for Living Organisms:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Invisible Molecular Movements:</strong> The most fundamental and definitive criterion is the occurrence of <strong>molecular movements</strong> within cells and subcellular structures. Living organisms are highly organized structures made of tissues, cells, and organelles that constantly repair and maintain their order against entropic breakdown.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vital Life Processes:</strong> Living beings actively carry out essential life processes including <strong>nutrition</strong> (metabolic energy uptake), <strong>respiration</strong> (cellular energy release), <strong>transportation</strong> of nutrients/gases, <strong>excretion</strong> of toxic wastes, and response to external stimuli (sensitivity).</li>\n  <li>Visible movements (such as locomotion, breathing movements, or plant phototropism) are common, but invisible maintenance molecular movements are the ultimate criterion.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fundamental criterion: invisible molecular movements repairing cellular structures</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Performing vital metabolic life processes (nutrition, respiration, transport, excretion)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 81 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are outside raw materials used for by an organism?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Outside Raw Materials:</strong></p>\n<p>Since life on Earth depends on carbon-based molecules, the primary outside raw materials consumed by organisms are:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Carbon-based Nutrients (Food):</strong> Heterotrophs consume organic matter (carbohydrates, proteins, fats) for energy and structural building blocks. Autotrophs absorb inorganic <strong>carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$):</strong> Required as a universal biochemical solvent for enzymatic cellular reactions, transport of metabolites, and photolysis in photosynthesis.</li>\n  <li><strong>Oxygen ($\\text{O}_2$):</strong> Absorbed by aerobic organisms for cellular respiration to break down glucose into ATP.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minerals (Nitrogen, Phosphorus, Iron, Magnesium):</strong> Absorbed by plants from soil to synthesize amino acids, proteins, DNA, and chlorophyll.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Carbon sources (organic food or CO2) and oxygen for respiration</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Water as biochemical medium and inorganic minerals (N, P, Fe) from soil</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 81 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What processes would you consider essential for maintaining life?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Essential Life Processes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> The process of procuring and taking in food/nutrients to yield energy and raw materials for growth.</li>\n  <li><strong>Respiration:</strong> The biochemical process of cellular gas exchange and oxidation of glucose to release biologically useful energy (ATP).</li>\n  <li><strong>Transportation:</strong> The internal circulatory mechanism that moves water, minerals, food, hormones, and respiratory gases to all tissues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Excretion:</strong> The biological removal of toxic nitrogenous waste products (urea, uric acid) generated by cellular metabolism.</li>\n  <li><strong>Control and Coordination:</strong> Neural and hormonal regulation ensuring homeostasis and response to environmental stimuli.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Listing and briefly explaining Nutrition and Respiration</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Listing and briefly explaining Transportation and Excretion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 87 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the differences between autotrophic nutrition and heterotrophic nutrition?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Distinction between Modes of Nutrition:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Parameter</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Autotrophic Nutrition</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Heterotrophic Nutrition</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Organisms synthesize their own organic food from simple inorganic raw materials ($\\text{CO}_2, \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Organisms cannot synthesize organic food; they depend directly or indirectly on autotrophs for organic nutrients.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Chlorophyll & Light</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Chlorophyll pigment and sunlight are indispensable.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Chlorophyll is absent; sunlight is not directly required for nutrition.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Storage Form</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Surplus glucose is stored as <strong>Starch</strong>.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Surplus food is stored as <strong>Glycogen</strong> or fats.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Green plants, cyanobacteria, photosynthetic bacteria.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">All animals, fungi (yeast, mushrooms), protozoans.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inorganic raw materials synthesis vs depending on external organic sources</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Presence of chlorophyll and light requirement vs absence</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Storage forms (starch vs glycogen) and valid examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 87 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Where do plants get each of the raw materials required for photosynthesis?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Sources of Photosynthetic Raw Materials:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Carbon Dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$):</strong> Absorbed directly from the ambient atmosphere through tiny microscopic pores called <strong>stomata</strong> present on the epidermal surface of leaves by gaseous diffusion.</li>\n  <li><strong>Water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$):</strong> Absorbed along with dissolved minerals from the rhizosphere soil by root hairs via osmosis, and transported upward to leaves through <strong>xylem vessels</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sunlight:</strong> Radiant solar photons are captured and absorbed by <strong>chlorophyll</strong> molecules located inside chloroplast thylakoids.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minerals (Nitrogen, Phosphorus, Iron, Magnesium):</strong> Absorbed as dissolved inorganic nitrates/nitrites and phosphates from the soil through roots.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">CO2 from atmosphere via stomata; Water from soil via roots and xylem</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sunlight captured by chlorophyll in chloroplasts; minerals from soil</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 87 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the role of the acid in our stomach?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Functions of Gastric Hydrochloric Acid ($\\text{HCl}$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Activation of Pepsinogen:</strong> The gastric glands in the stomach wall secrete inactive proenzyme pepsinogen. Gastric $\\text{HCl}$ creates a strongly acidic medium ($\\text{pH} \\approx 1.5 - 2.5$) necessary to convert inactive pepsinogen into active <strong>pepsin</strong> for breaking down dietary proteins into peptones and proteoses.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bactericidal Action:</strong> The high acidity kills bacteria and potential pathogens that enter the digestive tract along with ingested food.</li>\n  <li><strong>Food Softening:</strong> It softens tough fibrous components in food, facilitating mechanical churning and enzymatic digestion.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Creates acidic pH (1.5-2) to convert inactive pepsinogen into active pepsin</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Kills harmful ingested bacteria and pathogens</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 87 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the function of digestive enzymes?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Role of Digestive Enzymes:</strong></p>\n<p>Digestive enzymes act as biological catalysts that hydrolyse complex, large, insoluble food macromolecules into small, simple, diffusible, and water-soluble micro-molecules that can be absorbed across the intestinal epithelium into the blood stream:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Carbohydrases (e.g., Salivary amylase, Pancreatic amylase):</strong> Hydrolyse insoluble starches and complex carbohydrates into simple sugars (maltose, glucose).</li>\n  <li><strong>Proteases (e.g., Pepsin, Trypsin, Peptidases):</strong> Hydrolyse complex dietary proteins into peptides and finally into absorbable <strong>amino acids</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lipases (e.g., Pancreatic lipase):</strong> Hydrolyse emulsified fat droplets into <strong>fatty acids and glycerol</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Biological catalysts hydrolysing complex insoluble polymers into simple diffusible monomers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Specific examples: Amylase (starch to glucose), Trypsin/Pepsin (protein to amino acids), Lipase (fats to fatty acids)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 87 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the small intestine designed to absorb digested food?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structural Adaptations of Small Intestine for Absorption:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Enormous Surface Area (Villi and Microvilli):</strong> The inner mucosal lining of the ileum is folded into millions of tiny, microscopic finger-like projections called <strong>villi</strong>. Each epithelial cell of the villi bears countless sub-microscopic folds called <strong>microvilli</strong> (brush border). This increases the absorptive surface area of the gut by more than <strong>$300\\text{ m}^2$</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dense Capillary Network:</strong> Each villus is richly supplied with a dense mesh of thin-walled blood capillaries, allowing monosaccharides and amino acids to diffuse rapidly into the venous circulation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lacteal for Lipid Absorption:</strong> The center of each villus contains a specialized lymphatic capillary called a <strong>lacteal</strong>, which specifically absorbs digested fats (fatty acids and glycerol packaged as chylomicrons) into the lymphatic system.</li>\n  <li><strong>Thin Epithelial Barrier:</strong> The lining consists of a single layer of columnar epithelial cells, minimizing the diffusion distance for rapid transport.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Finger-like villi and brush-border microvilli massively expanding surface area</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dense network of blood capillaries for rapid absorption of glucose/amino acids</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Central lacteal vessel for absorption of fats and single-cell-thick epithelium</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 91 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What advantage over an aquatic organism does a terrestrial organism have with regard to obtaining oxygen for respiration?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Respiratory Advantage of Terrestrial Organisms:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Oxygen Concentration:</strong> Terrestrial organisms take in oxygen from atmospheric air, which contains an abundant and rich concentration of oxygen (approximately <strong>$21\\%$ or $210\\text{ mL/L}$</strong>). In contrast, aquatic organisms obtain oxygen dissolved in water, where oxygen concentration is extremely low (less than <strong>$8\\text{ mg/L}$ or $\\approx 0.7\\%$</strong>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Rate of Breathing:</strong> Because the amount of dissolved oxygen in aquatic habitats is so low, aquatic organisms (like fish) must pump large volumes of water over their gills, resulting in a <strong>very rapid breathing rate</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Terrestrial organisms do not have to expend high metabolic energy on high breathing rates, and their internal respiratory organs (lungs) are well protected against desiccation.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Atmospheric air has much higher oxygen content (21%) than dissolved water oxygen (<1%)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Terrestrial animals do not need high energy-consuming rapid breathing rates like aquatic animals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 91 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the different ways in which glucose is oxidised to provide energy in various organisms?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Biochemical Pathways of Glucose Breakdown:</strong></p>\n<p>In all living organisms, the initial common metabolic step is <strong>Glycolysis</strong> occurring in the cytoplasm, where one 6-carbon molecule of glucose is cleaved into two 3-carbon molecules of <strong>pyruvate</strong>, releasing a small amount of ATP.</p>\n<p>Pyruvate is subsequently broken down via three distinct metabolic pathways:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Path 1: Absence of Oxygen (Anaerobic Respiration / Fermentation in Yeast):</strong><br>\n    Occurs in cytoplasm of yeast cells; pyruvate is converted into <strong>ethanol, carbon dioxide, and 2 ATP</strong>:\n    $$\\text{Pyruvate} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Absence of } \\text{O}_2} \\text{Ethanol (2C)} + \\text{CO}_2 + \\text{Energy (2 ATP)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Path 2: Lack of Oxygen (Anaerobic pathway in Human Skeletal Muscle Cells during vigorous exercise):</strong><br>\n    When oxygen demand outstrips supply, pyruvate is converted into <strong>lactic acid and 2 ATP</strong>:\n    $$\\text{Pyruvate} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Lack of } \\text{O}_2} \\text{Lactic acid (3C)} + \\text{Energy (Cramps)}$$\n    Accumulation of lactic acid leads to muscular fatigue and painful cramps.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Path 3: Presence of Oxygen (Aerobic Respiration in Mitochondria):</strong><br>\n    Pyruvate undergoes complete oxidative decarboxylation inside the mitochondria into <strong>carbon dioxide, water, and 38 ATP</strong>:\n    $$\\text{Pyruvate} + \\text{O}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\text{Mitochondria}} 6\\text{CO}_2 + 6\\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{Energy (38 ATP)}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Initial cytoplasmic conversion of 6C glucose to 3C pyruvate (glycolysis)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Anaerobic yeast pathway (ethanol + CO2) and muscle pathway (lactic acid causing cramps)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Aerobic mitochondrial pathway (CO2 + H2O + large ATP yield)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 91 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is oxygen and carbon dioxide transported in human beings?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Gas Transport Mechanisms in Blood:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Oxygen ($\\text{O}_2$) Transport:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Oxygen has very low solubility in water/plasma. Therefore, about <strong>$97 - 98.5\\%$ of oxygen is transported bound to Haemoglobin</strong>, a specialized iron-containing respiratory pigment found inside Red Blood Cells (RBCs).</li>\n      <li>Each haemoglobin molecule reversibly binds four oxygen molecules in lung capillaries to form <strong>oxyhaemoglobin</strong>:\n        $$\\text{Hb} + 4\\text{O}_2 \\rightleftharpoons \\text{Hb(O}_2)_4$$\n      </li>\n      <li>In tissues with low partial pressure of oxygen ($p\\text{O}_2$), oxyhaemoglobin dissociates, releasing oxygen to the respiring cells. The remaining $1.5\\%$ is carried dissolved in plasma.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Carbon Dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$) Transport:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Carbon dioxide is about <strong>20 to 25 times more soluble</strong> in water than oxygen.</li>\n      <li>About <strong>$70\\%$ is transported as dissolved Bicarbonate ions ($\\text{HCO}_3^-$)</strong> in blood plasma.</li>\n      <li>About <strong>$20 - 23\\%$</strong> binds with the amino groups of haemoglobin to form <strong>carbamino-haemoglobin</strong>.</li>\n      <li>About <strong>$7\\%$</strong> travels directly dissolved as physical solute in blood plasma.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Oxygen transported mainly bound to haemoglobin in RBCs as oxyhaemoglobin</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Carbon dioxide transported primarily as dissolved bicarbonate ions (HCO3-) in plasma due to high solubility</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 91 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How are the lungs designed in human beings to maximise the area for exchange of gases?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structural Design of Human Lungs:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Extensive Branching Network:</strong> Within the lungs, the primary bronchus subdivides repeatedly into smaller and smaller airways (secondary bronchi, tertiary bronchi, and terminal bronchioles) like the branches of an inverted tree.</li>\n  <li><strong>Balloon-like Alveoli:</strong> The terminal bronchioles end in microscopic, cup-shaped balloon-like air sacs called <strong>alveoli</strong>. There are approximately <strong>300 to 500 million alveoli</strong> in both adult human lungs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Huge Surface Area:</strong> If flattened out, the collective surface area provided by these alveoli spans approximately <strong>$80\\text{ m}^2$</strong> (equivalent to the floor area of a tennis court).</li>\n  <li><strong>Ultra-thin Membrane & Dense Capillaries:</strong> The alveolar walls are just one-cell-thick and wrapped in an ultra-dense network of pulmonary capillaries, enabling instantaneous diffusion of $\\text{O}_2$ and $\\text{CO}_2$.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Branching bronchiole network terminating in millions of balloon-like alveoli</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Provides huge surface area (~80 m2) with one-cell-thick capillary-rich membranes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 96 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the components of the transport system in human beings? What are the functions of these components?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Components and Functions of Human Circulatory System:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. The Heart (Muscular Pump):</strong> A four-chambered myogenic muscular organ that pumps deoxygenated blood to the lungs for oxygenation and forcefully pumps oxygenated blood to all systemic body organs.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Blood (Fluid Connective Tissue):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Plasma:</em> Fluid matrix transporting dissolved food nutrients, carbon dioxide, nitrogenous wastes (urea), and hormones.</li>\n      <li><em>RBCs (Erythrocytes):</em> Contain haemoglobin to transport oxygen from lungs to tissues.</li>\n      <li><em>WBCs (Leukocytes):</em> Produce antibodies and engulf pathogens, providing immune defense.</li>\n      <li><em>Platelets (Thrombocytes):</em> Initiate blood clotting at injury sites to prevent haemorrhage.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Blood Vessels (Closed Conduit Network):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Arteries:</em> Thick, elastic-walled vessels carrying oxygenated blood away from the heart under high pressure.</li>\n      <li><em>Veins:</em> Thin-walled vessels with internal valves carrying deoxygenated blood back towards the heart.</li>\n      <li><em>Capillaries:</em> Microscopic one-cell-thick vessels where exchange of gases and nutrients occurs.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>4. Lymphatic System:</strong> Drains excess extracellular tissue fluid back into the venous bloodstream and transports absorbed fats.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Heart: Muscular pump maintaining blood flow to lungs and body</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Blood: Plasma, RBCs (oxygen), WBCs (immunity), Platelets (clotting)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Vessels: Arteries, veins (with valves), capillaries, and lymphatic drainage</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 96 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is it necessary to separate oxygenated and deoxygenated blood in mammals and birds?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Biological Significance of Blood Separation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>High Energy Demand (Homeothermy):</strong> Mammals and birds are <strong>warm-blooded (endothermic/homeothermic)</strong> animals. They must constantly maintain a constant, elevated internal body temperature regardless of ambient environmental fluctuations.</li>\n  <li><strong>Preventing Mixing for Maximum Efficiency:</strong> Complete anatomical separation of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood by a four-chambered heart prevents any mixing. This ensures that purely oxygen-saturated blood is delivered to metabolically active tissues.</li>\n  <li>This enables highly efficient cellular respiration and ATP generation needed to fuel thermal homeostasis and vigorous physical activity.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Warm-blooded animals (birds and mammals) need large energy to maintain body temperature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Separation prevents mixing, ensuring highly efficient oxygen delivery to tissues for high ATP output</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 96 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the components of the transport system in highly organised plants?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Vascular Transport Tissues in Higher Plants:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Xylem Tissue:</strong> Transports water and dissolved inorganic mineral salts unidirectionally upwards from the soil roots to the leaves and apical stems.\n    <br><em>Conducting elements:</em> <strong>Tracheids and Xylem Vessels</strong> (dead, hollow, lignified thick tubes).\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Phloem Tissue:</strong> Translocates soluble organic products of photosynthesis (sucrose, amino acids) bidirectionally from photosynthetic leaves (source) to storage organs and growing tissues (sink).\n    <br><em>Conducting elements:</em> <strong>Sieve Tubes and Companion Cells</strong> (living cells with perforated sieve plates).\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Xylem: conducting water and minerals upwards via vessels and tracheids</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Phloem: translocating photosynthates (sucrose) via sieve tubes and companion cells</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 96 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How are water and minerals transported in plants?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Ascent of Sap in Xylem:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Absorption at Roots (Root Pressure):</strong> Root hair cells actively absorb mineral ions from soil water using metabolic energy. This creates an osmotic concentration gradient between root cells and soil, causing water to flow continuously into the root xylem, generating a modest hydrostatic <strong>root pressure</strong> (significant during night).</li>\n  <li><strong>Transpiration Pull (Primary Driving Force):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>During daytime, water continuously evaporates as water vapour from the mesophyll cells of leaves through open stomatal pores—a process known as <strong>transpiration</strong>.</li>\n      <li>This loss creates a massive negative hydrostatic pressure (suction tension) in the leaf xylem.</li>\n      <li>Because of strong <strong>cohesion</strong> (mutual attraction between water molecules) and <strong>adhesion</strong> (attraction between water molecules and lignified xylem walls), a continuous, unbroken water column extends from roots to leaf tips.</li>\n      <li>The suction tension pulls the water column upwards through tracheids and vessels against gravity, reaching heights exceeding 100 metres in tall trees.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Active mineral uptake by roots setting up osmotic gradient and root pressure</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Transpiration at leaves creating negative suction pressure (transpiration pull)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cohesive and adhesive forces maintaining unbroken xylem water column</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 96 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is food transported in plants?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Translocation in Phloem:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Active Loading:</strong> Soluble products of photosynthesis, primarily <strong>sucrose</strong>, are actively loaded from leaf mesophyll cells into the sieve tubes of phloem using metabolic energy derived from <strong>ATP</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Osmotic Pressure Generation:</strong> The accumulation of sucrose raises the osmotic pressure inside the sieve tube, causing water to enter from adjacent xylem vessels by osmosis.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bulk Flow to Sink:</strong> The resulting high hydrostatic pressure drives the phloem sap towards regions of lower osmotic pressure (such as developing roots, fruits, seeds, and buds). There, sucrose is actively unloaded and utilized or stored as starch.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Active loading of sucrose into phloem sieve tubes using ATP</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Water entry by osmosis creates hydrostatic pressure driving bulk flow to low pressure sinks</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 100 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe the structure and functioning of nephrons.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Nephron: The Structural and Functional Excretory Unit of Kidney</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Structure of a Nephron:</strong> Each human kidney contains about 1 million nephrons. A nephron consists of:\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Malpighian Body:</strong> Composed of a double-walled cup called <strong>Bowman's capsule</strong> enclosing a tuft of capillaries called the <strong>glomerulus</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Renal Tubule:</strong> A long coiled tube differentiated into Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT), U-shaped <strong>Henle's loop</strong>, and Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT), opening into a common collecting duct.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Functioning (Three Step Process of Urine Formation):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>(a) Glomerular Ultrafiltration:</strong> Under high hydrostatic blood pressure in the glomerulus, liquid plasma containing glucose, amino acids, salts, urea, and excess water is filtered across the glomerular membrane into Bowman's capsule as primary filtrate ($\\approx 180\\text{ litres/day}$). Blood cells and proteins are retained.</li>\n      <li><strong>(b) Selective Tubular Reabsorption:</strong> As the filtrate flows along the PCT and Henle's loop, all essential nutrients (100% glucose, amino acids, and major ions) and most water ($\\approx 99\\%$) are selectively reabsorbed back into the peritubular capillaries.</li>\n      <li><strong>(c) Tubular Secretion:</strong> Excess potassium, hydrogen ions, and metabolic wastes are actively secreted from capillaries into the distal tubule to maintain electrolyte balance. The remaining fluid ($1 - 1.5\\text{ L/day}$) is concentrated <strong>urine</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Nephron structure: Bowman capsule, glomerulus, renal tubules (PCT, Loop of Henle, DCT)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ultrafiltration under pressure forming primary filtrate (180 L/day)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Selective tubular reabsorption of glucose, amino acids, water and tubular secretion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 100 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the methods used by plants to get rid of excretory products?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Excretory Mechanisms in Plants:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Gaseous Wastes:</strong> Surplus oxygen produced during photosynthesis and excess carbon dioxide produced during respiration are expelled by simple diffusion through <strong>stomata</strong> in leaves and <strong>lenticels</strong> in stems.</li>\n  <li><strong>Excess Water:</strong> Removed continuously into the atmosphere as water vapour via <strong>transpiration</strong> through stomata.</li>\n  <li><strong>Storage in Shedding Organs:</strong> Many metabolic wastes are stored in cellular vacuoles of older leaves, bark, and dead xylem tissues that periodically <strong>wither, turn yellow, and fall off</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resins and Gums:</strong> Waste products are stored as insoluble resins and gums, especially in old, non-functional xylem.</li>\n  <li><strong>Excretion into Soil:</strong> Some excretory substances are secreted directly into the soil surrounding their roots.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gaseous exchange of O2/CO2 through stomata and water removal via transpiration</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Storage in falling leaves/bark, resins/gums in old xylem, and root secretions into soil</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 100 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the amount of urine produced regulated?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Regulation of Urine Volume:</strong></p>\n<p>The total volume of urine produced daily depends on two primary physiological factors:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Volume of Excess Water in the Body:</strong> If a person drinks plenty of fluids, the body retains less water and the kidney reabsorbs less water, producing a large volume of dilute urine. If dehydrated (or on a hot summer day with heavy sweating), kidneys reabsorb maximum water, producing a small volume of concentrated urine.</li>\n  <li><strong>Amount of Dissolved Nitrogenous Wastes:</strong> The more dissolved waste products (urea, salts) to be excreted, the more water is needed to dissolve and eliminate them safely without osmotic injury.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hormonal Control (ADH):</strong> Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus stimulate the posterior pituitary to secrete <strong>Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH / Vasopressin)</strong>, which increases water permeability of DCT and collecting ducts for reabsorption when dehydrated.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dependence on amount of excess water present in body and amount of dissolved waste</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) controlling reabsorption in collecting ducts</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The kidneys in human beings are a part of the system for: (a) nutrition (b) respiration (c) excretion (d) transportation.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) excretion</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Kidneys filter out metabolic nitrogenous wastes such as urea and uric acid from circulating blood, forming urine to maintain osmoregulatory balance.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) excretion with filtration of nitrogenous waste explanation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The xylem in plants are responsible for: (a) transport of water (b) transport of food (c) transport of amino acids (d) transport of oxygen.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(a) transport of water</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Xylem vessels and tracheids conduct water and dissolved minerals from roots upwards to leaves. Food and amino acids are transported by phloem.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (a) transport of water via xylem vessels and tracheids</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The autotrophic mode of nutrition requires: (a) carbon dioxide and water (b) chlorophyll (c) sunlight (d) all of the above.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) all of the above</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Photosynthesis requires carbon dioxide and water as raw materials, chlorophyll pigment to absorb light photons, and radiant solar energy to drive the chemical reduction of $\\text{CO}_2$ into carbohydrates.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) all of the above with role of each factor in photosynthesis</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The breakdown of pyruvate to give carbon dioxide, water and energy takes place in: (a) cytoplasm (b) mitochondria (c) chloroplast (d) nucleus.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) mitochondria</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Aerobic respiration, which oxidises 3-carbon pyruvate into carbon dioxide, water, and 36-38 ATP molecules via the Krebs cycle and electron transport chain, occurs inside the <strong>mitochondria</strong>. Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) mitochondria with aerobic respiration explanation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How are fats digested in our bodies? Where does this process take place?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Digestion of Fats:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Site of Digestion:</strong> Digestion of fats takes place predominantly in the <strong>small intestine (duodenum and ileum)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 1: Emulsification by Bile Salts:</strong> Fats enter the duodenum as large, insoluble lipid globules that cannot be easily accessed by water-soluble enzymes. The liver secretes <strong>bile juice</strong> containing bile salts (sodium glycocholate and sodium taurocholate). Bile salts break down large fat globules into minute droplets, increasing the surface area for enzyme action. This is called <strong>emulsification</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 2: Hydrolysis by Pancreatic and Intestinal Lipase:</strong> The pancreas secretes <strong>lipase</strong> in pancreatic juice, and intestinal glands secrete intestinal lipase. Lipase hydrolyses emulsified fat molecules into absorbable <strong>fatty acids and glycerol</strong>:\n    $$\\text{Emulsified Fats} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Lipase}} \\text{Fatty acids} + \\text{Glycerol}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>These products are absorbed into the central lacteals of the intestinal villi.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Site: Small intestine (duodenum)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Emulsification: Bile salts break large fat globules into small droplets to increase surface area</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Enzymatic hydrolysis: Pancreatic/intestinal lipase converts fats into fatty acids and glycerol</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q27\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q27')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the role of saliva in the digestion of food?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Functions of Saliva in Digestion:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Chemical Digestion of Starch:</strong> Saliva contains the digestive enzyme <strong>salivary amylase (ptyalin)</strong>. It breaks down complex insoluble starch molecules into simpler disaccharide sugars (maltose) at an optimum pH of $\\approx 6.8$:\n    $$\\text{Starch} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Salivary amylase}} \\text{Maltose (Sugar)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Lubrication and Bolus Formation:</strong> Saliva contains water and <strong>mucus</strong> that moistens and lubricates dry food particles. Mastication by teeth blends food with saliva into a smooth, cohesive ball called a <strong>bolus</strong>, making swallowing (deglutition) down the oesophagus smooth and effortless.</li>\n  <li><strong>Oral Hygiene:</strong> Saliva contains <strong>lysozyme</strong>, an antibacterial enzyme that destroys oral bacteria.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Salivary amylase hydrolyses complex starch into maltose sugars</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mucus lubricates and binds food into a swallowable bolus and lysozyme kills bacteria</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q28\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q28')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the necessary conditions for autotrophic nutrition and what are its by-products?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Conditions and By-products of Autotrophic Nutrition:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Necessary Conditions:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Presence of the green pigment <strong>chlorophyll</strong> inside chloroplasts to absorb light energy.</li>\n      <li>Availability of <strong>sunlight</strong> photons.</li>\n      <li>Supply of inorganic <strong>carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$)</strong> from atmosphere.</li>\n      <li>Supply of <strong>water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$)</strong> absorbed from soil.</li>\n    </ul>\n    $$6\\text{CO}_2 + 12\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\xrightarrow[\\text{Chlorophyll}]{\\text{Sunlight}} \\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6 + 6\\text{O}_2\\uparrow + 6\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>By-products of Autotrophic Nutrition:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Oxygen gas ($\\text{O}_2$):</strong> Released as a vital by-product formed by the photolysis of water.</li>\n      <li><strong>Water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$):</strong> Released as transpired vapour.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Conditions: Chlorophyll, sunlight, CO2, and water with balanced photosynthesis equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">By-products: Oxygen (O2) from photolysis of water and transpired water vapour</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q29\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q29')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the differences between aerobic and anaerobic respiration? Name some organisms that use the anaerobic mode of respiration.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Aerobic and Anaerobic Respiration:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Aerobic Respiration</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Anaerobic Respiration</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Oxygen Requirement</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Takes place strictly in the presence of oxygen.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Takes place in the total absence or deficit of oxygen.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Site of Occurrence</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Begins in cytoplasm, completed in mitochondria.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Occurs entirely in the cytoplasm.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>End Products</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$) and Water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Ethanol and $\\text{CO}_2$ (in yeast) OR Lactic acid (in muscles).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Energy Yield</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Complete oxidation yielding <strong>38 ATP</strong> per glucose.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Incomplete oxidation yielding only <strong>2 ATP</strong> per glucose.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Organisms using Anaerobic Respiration:</strong> <strong>Yeast</strong> (<em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</em>), anaerobic bacteria (e.g., <em>Clostridium tetani</em>), parasitic intestinal worms (e.g., <em>Ascaris</em>, tapeworm), and human skeletal muscles during extreme exercise.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Oxygen presence vs absence, and mitochondrial site vs cytoplasmic site</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">End products (CO2 + H2O vs ethanol/lactic acid) and energy yield (38 ATP vs 2 ATP)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Organisms: Yeast, anaerobic bacteria, parasitic roundworms</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q30\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q30')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How are the alveoli designed to maximise the exchange of gases?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structural Adaptations of Alveoli:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Gigantic Surface Area:</strong> Hundreds of millions of tiny balloon-like alveoli in both lungs provide a combined surface area of roughly <strong>$80\\text{ m}^2$</strong>, maximizing the interface for gaseous diffusion.</li>\n  <li><strong>Extremely Thin Diffusion Barrier:</strong> The alveolar wall is composed of a single layer of squamous epithelial cells, and the pulmonary capillary is made of a single endothelial layer. The total diffusion thickness is less than <strong>$0.5\\ \\mu\\text{m}$</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Extensive Capillary Network:</strong> The outer surface of each alveolus is wrapped in an intricate web of fine blood capillaries, ensuring high blood flow and steep partial pressure gradients for rapid $\\text{O}_2$ absorption and $\\text{CO}_2$ release.</li>\n  <li><strong>Moist Inner Lining:</strong> Alveolar surfaces are coated with a thin moisture film, allowing oxygen gas to dissolve before diffusing across cell membranes.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Immense surface area (~80 m2) provided by millions of microscopic balloon sacs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Extremely thin membrane (one-cell-thick), moist surface, and rich blood capillary network</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q31\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q31')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What would be the consequences of a deficiency of haemoglobin in our bodies?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Consequences of Haemoglobin Deficiency (Anaemia):</strong></p>\n<p>Haemoglobin is the iron-containing pigment in red blood cells that transports oxygen to body tissues. A deficiency of haemoglobin leads to a condition called <strong>Anaemia</strong>, with the following physiological effects:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Reduced Oxygen Carrying Capacity:</strong> The oxygen-carrying capacity of blood drops drastically. Tissue cells receive inadequate oxygen supplies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Impaired Cellular Respiration:</strong> Due to oxygen deprivation, the rate of aerobic cellular oxidation slows down, resulting in diminished ATP production.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fatigue and Breathlessness:</strong> The person experiences chronic exhaustion, physical weakness, pale skin, dizziness, and shortness of breath (hyperventilation as the body tries to compensate for oxygen deficits).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Condition known as anaemia resulting in reduced oxygen delivery to body cells</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Leads to decreased ATP production causing extreme fatigue, breathlessness, and paleness</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q32\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q32')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe double circulation of blood in human beings. Why is it necessary?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Double Circulation in Humans:</strong></p>\n<p>In human beings, blood travels through the heart <strong>twice</strong> during each complete circuit of the body. This consists of two separate circulatory circuits:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Pulmonary Circulation:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Deoxygenated blood from body tissues enters the Right Atrium &rarr; passes into the Right Ventricle.</li>\n      <li>The Right Ventricle pumps it through the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong> to the lungs.</li>\n      <li>In the lungs, blood discharges $\\text{CO}_2$ and picks up $\\text{O}_2$.</li>\n      <li>Oxygenated blood returns to the Left Atrium via the <strong>pulmonary veins</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Systemic Circulation:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Oxygenated blood flows from Left Atrium &rarr; into the thick-walled Left Ventricle.</li>\n      <li>The Left Ventricle pumps it under high pressure into the <strong>aorta</strong>, distributing it to all systemic organs and body tissues.</li>\n      <li>In tissues, oxygen is delivered and metabolic $\\text{CO}_2$ is picked up.</li>\n      <li>Deoxygenated blood returns via the vena cava to the Right Atrium.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why Double Circulation is Necessary:</strong></p>\n<p>It completely separates oxygen-rich blood from deoxygenated blood, ensuring that systemic organs receive pure oxygenated blood under high pressure. This sustains the high metabolic rate and continuous heat production required by warm-blooded mammals.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pulmonary circulation: Right ventricle -> Lungs -> Left atrium</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Systemic circulation: Left ventricle -> Body tissues -> Right atrium</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Necessity: Prevents mixing of oxygenated/deoxygenated blood for high energy/temperature maintenance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q33\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q33')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the differences between the transport of materials in xylem and phloem?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Xylem and Phloem Transport:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Parameter</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Transport in Xylem</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Transport in Phloem</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Substances Transported</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Water and dissolved inorganic mineral salts.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Organic nutrients (sucrose, amino acids, hormones).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Direction of Flow</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Strictly <strong>unidirectional</strong> (upwards from roots to leaves).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Bidirectional</strong> (from source leaves to sinks in roots/buds).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Driving Mechanism</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Physical forces (transpiration pull, root pressure, cohesion); non-ATP driven.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Active transport; requires energy in the form of <strong>ATP</strong> to build osmotic pressure.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Cell Nature</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Vessels and tracheids are <strong>dead lignified cells</strong>.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Sieve tubes and companion cells are <strong>living cells</strong>.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Xylem conducts water/minerals unidirectionally using physical transpiration pull</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Phloem translocates organic nutrients bidirectionally using metabolic ATP energy</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q34\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q34')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Compare the functioning of alveoli in the lungs and nephrons in the kidneys with respect to their structure and functioning.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Alveoli (Lungs) and Nephrons (Kidneys):</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Alveoli (Respiratory System)</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Nephrons (Excretory System)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Structural Design</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Microscopic balloon-like air sacs surrounded by an extensive mesh of pulmonary capillaries.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Elongated tubular structures consisting of Bowman's capsule, glomerulus, Henle's loop, and collecting tubules.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Nature of Filtration</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Passive physical diffusion of gases across a thin semipermeable alveolar membrane.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">High-pressure glomerular ultrafiltration of liquid blood plasma followed by selective tubular reabsorption.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Substances Exchanged</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Oxygen gas taken in from air; Carbon dioxide gas expelled from blood.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Toxic nitrogenous wastes (urea, uric acid) and excess ions expelled as urine; glucose and water reabsorbed.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Surface Area Expansion</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Expands surface area to $\\approx 80\\text{ m}^2$ for rapid gas exchange.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Coiled tubules provide immense surface area for tubular reabsorption.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structural comparison: Balloon air sacs vs Bowman capsule and renal tubules</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Functional comparison: Gaseous diffusion (O2/CO2) vs liquid ultrafiltration and reabsorption</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Waste products comparison: Expulsion of CO2 vs expulsion of urea/nitrogenous waste in urine</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 5</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Haemodialysis (Artificial Kidney)</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">In cases of chronic renal failure, patients are kept alive using an artificial kidney (haemodialysis machine). (i) What is the function of the cellophane tubes in the dialyser? (ii) What is the composition of the dialysing solution compared to normal blood plasma? (iii) How does haemodialysis differ fundamentally from a healthy living kidney in terms of tubular reabsorption?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Function of Cellophane Tubes:</strong> Cellophane tubes are made of a selectively semipermeable membrane that allows small solute molecules like urea, uric acid, and excess mineral salts to diffuse through while retaining large blood cells and plasma proteins inside the blood circuit.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Composition of Dialysing Fluid:</strong> The dialysing fluid has the exact same osmotic pressure and concentration of essential electrolytes and glucose as normal blood plasma, but it contains <strong>zero nitrogenous wastes (zero urea)</strong>. This steep concentration gradient ensures that urea and uric acid diffuse passively out of the patient's blood into the dialysing fluid.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Difference from Natural Kidney:</strong> In a natural kidney, approximately $180\\text{ litres}$ of primary filtrate is filtered daily and $99\\%$ of water and glucose is selectively reabsorbed along the renal tubules. In haemodialysis, <strong>there is no tubular reabsorption</strong>; only waste diffusion takes place across cellophane tubes into the bath.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(4)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(6)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "6": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch6\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">6</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 6: Control and Coordination</h2>\n      <p>Nervous System, Reflex Arcs, Brain Anatomy, Phytohormones & Plant Tropisms, Endocrine Glands — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 105, 108, 112) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 12)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 105 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the difference between a reflex action and walking?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Reflex Action:</strong> An instantaneous, involuntary, and automatic motor response to a sensory stimulus executed without conscious thinking or cerebral processing. It is mediated primarily by the <strong>spinal cord</strong> (e.g., withdrawing hand on touching a hot plate).</li>\n  <li><strong>Walking:</strong> A <strong>voluntary action</strong> initiated, coordinated, and controlled consciously by the cerebral cortex and cerebellum of the <strong>brain</strong> according to the organism's conscious will and intention.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reflex: involuntary, unconscious, mediated by spinal cord</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Walking: voluntary, learned, conscious, controlled by brain (cerebrum/cerebellum)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 105 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What happens at the synapse between two neurons?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Transmission across Synapse:</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>synapse</strong> is a microscopic junction gap separating the axon terminal of one neuron from the dendrite of the next neuron.</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>When an electrical nerve impulse reaches the axon terminal, it triggers synaptic vesicles to release chemical messenger molecules called <strong>neurotransmitters</strong> (e.g., acetylcholine) into the synaptic cleft.</li>\n  <li>These chemical molecules diffuse across the microscopic synaptic gap and bind to specific receptor sites on the dendrite of the adjacent neuron.</li>\n  <li>This chemical binding sets off a new electrical impulse in the receiving neuron. Because neurotransmitters are released only from axon terminals, impulses travel strictly in a <strong>unidirectional</strong> manner across synapses.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electrical impulse releases neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Neurotransmitters diffuse across synapse, bind to dendrite receptors, and generate new impulse</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 105 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which part of the brain maintains posture and equilibrium of the body?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Posture and equilibrium of the body are maintained by the <strong>Cerebellum</strong>, which is a component of the <strong>hindbrain</strong>. It coordinates voluntary motor activities and muscular precision.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cerebellum (Hindbrain)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 105 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How do we detect the smell of an agarbatti (incense stick)?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Olfactory Perception:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Volatile chemical fragrance molecules from the burning incense stick diffuse into the air and enter the nasal cavity during breathing.</li>\n  <li>These molecules dissolve in the nasal mucus and bind to specialized sensory receptors called <strong>olfactory receptors</strong> located in the olfactory epithelium.</li>\n  <li>Olfactory receptors generate electrical nerve impulses that travel via the olfactory nerve to the <strong>olfactory lobes of the forebrain (cerebrum)</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The forebrain interprets the sensory signals by comparing them with previously stored memories of smells, enabling conscious recognition of the incense fragrance.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Olfactory receptors in nasal epithelium detect volatile fragrance molecules and generate impulses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Impulses sent via olfactory nerve to forebrain (cerebrum) for interpretation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 105 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the role of the brain in reflex action?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Role of the Brain in Reflex Actions:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The brain plays <strong>no direct role in initiating or executing spinal reflex actions</strong>. To ensure survival and avoid tissue damage, reflex arcs are completed inside the spinal cord so the motor response occurs within milliseconds without waiting for slow cerebral processing.</li>\n  <li>However, as the sensory neuron enters the spinal cord, a parallel relay neuron sends an informational signal upward to the brain.</li>\n  <li>The brain receives this sensory information, creates a conscious awareness of the event (e.g., perceiving pain), and stores the memory to modify future voluntary behaviour.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Spinal reflex is executed autonomously by spinal cord without prior brain intervention</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Brain receives secondary signal for conscious awareness of pain and memory recording</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 108 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are plant hormones (phytohormones)?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>Plant hormones (phytohormones) are naturally occurring organic chemical compounds synthesized in minute quantities in one part of a plant (such as shoot tips, root tips, young leaves) and translocated to other parts where they regulate physiological processes including growth, cell division, cell elongation, flowering, and responses to environmental stimuli.</p>\n<p><em>Examples:</em> Auxins, Gibberellins, Cytokinins (growth promoters), and Abscisic acid (growth inhibitor).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chemical substances synthesized in minute amounts regulating growth and physiological responses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Examples: Auxins, Gibberellins, Cytokinins, Abscisic acid</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 108 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the movement of leaves of the sensitive plant different from the movement of a shoot towards light?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Plant Movements:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Movement of Sensitive Plant (Mimosa)</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Movement of Shoot Towards Light</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Type of Movement</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Nastic movement</strong> (non-directional; thigmonasty).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Tropic movement</strong> (directional; positive phototropism).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Growth Dependence</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Growth-independent</strong>; caused by rapid turgor pressure changes in pulvinus cells.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Growth-dependent</strong>; caused by differential cell elongation mediated by auxin.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Speed & Reversibility</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Extremely rapid and fully reversible.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Slow, gradual, and irreversible.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sensitive plant: non-directional nastic movement independent of growth caused by turgor changes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Shoot phototropism: directional tropic movement dependent on growth caused by auxin elongation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 108 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give an example of a plant hormone that promotes growth.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Auxin</strong> (or <strong>Gibberellin</strong> / <strong>Cytokinin</strong>) is a plant hormone that promotes growth by stimulating cell elongation, apical dominance, and cell division.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Auxin / Gibberellin / Cytokinin</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 108 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How do auxins promote the growth of a tendril around a support?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Thigmotropic Mechanism of Tendrils:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Tendrils of climbing plants (like pea, bitter gourd) are sensitive to contact.</li>\n  <li>When a tendril touches an external physical support, <strong>auxin diffuses away from the side in contact towards the side away from the support</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The higher concentration of auxin on the non-contact side stimulates rapid cell elongation and faster growth on that side compared to the contact side.</li>\n  <li>This unequal, differential growth rate causes the tendril to curve inwards and coil tightly around the support like a spring, securing the climbing plant.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Auxin diffuses away from contact side to the side away from support</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Higher auxin causes greater cell elongation on outer side, coiling tendril around support</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 108 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Design an experiment to demonstrate hydrotropism.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Hydrotropism Demonstration:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Setup:</strong> Take a flat tray filled with dry soil. Sow germinating pea seeds in the soil. At one end of the tray, place a porous clay pot filled with water.</li>\n  <li><strong>Observation:</strong> Keep the setup undisturbed for 4 to 6 days. As the seeds germinate, observe the direction of root growth.</li>\n  <li><strong>Result:</strong> The radicles (young roots) initially grow downwards due to positive geotropism, but soon curve and grow horizontally <strong>towards the moist porous water pot</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> This directional curvature of plant roots towards moisture demonstrates <strong>positive hydrotropism</strong> (overcoming positive geotropism).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Experimental setup with germinating seeds and porous water vessel in dry soil</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Observation showing roots curving towards water source proves positive hydrotropism</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 112 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does chemical coordination take place in animals?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Endocrine Chemical Coordination:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Chemical coordination in animals is mediated by specialized ductless glands called <strong>endocrine glands</strong>.</li>\n  <li>These glands synthesize and secrete chemical messengers called <strong>hormones</strong> directly into the bloodstream without any ducts.</li>\n  <li>The circulating blood transports hormones throughout the entire body. Hormones act specifically on distant <strong>target tissues and organs</strong> that bear specialized complementary receptor proteins on their cell surfaces.</li>\n  <li>Upon binding to target receptors, hormones initiate specific biochemical responses (e.g., insulin regulating blood glucose uptake, adrenaline accelerating heart rate).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ductless endocrine glands secrete hormones directly into bloodstream</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hormones travel via blood to specific target organs bearing complementary receptors</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 112 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the use of iodised salt advisable?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason for Using Iodised Salt:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Iodine is an essential dietary mineral required by the <strong>thyroid gland</strong> to synthesize the hormone <strong>thyroxin</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Thyroxin regulates the basal metabolic rate (BMR) and orchestrates carbohydrate, protein, and fat metabolism for balanced physical growth and mental development.</li>\n  <li>If iodine is deficient in our diet, the thyroid gland cannot synthesize sufficient thyroxin and enlarges in an attempt to capture more iodine, causing a disease called <strong>Goitre</strong> (characterized by a swollen neck).</li>\n  <li>Therefore, consuming iodised common salt provides the daily micro-requirement of iodine and prevents goitre and thyroid dysfunction.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Iodine is necessary for thyroid gland to synthesize thyroxin hormone</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deficiency causes thyroid enlargement resulting in goitre and metabolic slowdown</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 112 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does our body respond when adrenaline is secreted into the blood?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Physiological \"Fight or Flight\" Response to Adrenaline:</strong></p>\n<p>When an individual faces stress, fear, or physical danger, the adrenal medulla secretes <strong>adrenaline</strong> directly into the blood, triggering rapid systemic adjustments:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Accelerated Heart Rate:</strong> The heart beats faster and pumps with greater stroke volume, supplying more oxygen to skeletal muscles.</li>\n  <li><strong>Diversion of Blood Flow:</strong> Smooth muscles around arterioles in the digestive system and skin contract, diverting blood supply primarily to active skeletal muscles.</li>\n  <li><strong>Increased Breathing Rate:</strong> Diaphragm and rib muscles contract faster, increasing pulmonary oxygen uptake.</li>\n  <li><strong>Glycogen Breakdown:</strong> Liver converts stored glycogen into glucose, flooding the bloodstream with metabolic energy to fight or flee.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Heart beats faster delivering more oxygen to skeletal muscles</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Blood diverted from skin/gut to muscles and breathing rate accelerates for emergency action</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 112 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why are some patients of diabetes treated by giving injections of insulin?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason for Insulin Injections:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Insulin</strong> is a peptide hormone produced by the $\\beta$-cells of the Islets of Langerhans in the <strong>pancreas</strong>. Its primary role is to promote the cellular uptake of glucose and stimulate the liver and muscles to convert excess glucose into stored glycogen, keeping blood sugar within normal limits ($70 - 140\\text{ mg/dL}$).</li>\n  <li>In patients suffering from <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>, the pancreas fails to produce adequate insulin. As a result, glucose cannot enter cells, leading to dangerously elevated blood sugar levels (hyperglycaemia) and loss of glucose in urine.</li>\n  <li>Administering insulin injections artificially supplements the hormone, lowering blood sugar levels to normal physiological ranges and preventing organ damage.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Insulin produced by pancreas regulates and lowers blood glucose levels</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Diabetic deficiency causes hyperglycaemia; insulin injections restore normal blood sugar</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following is a plant hormone? (a) Insulin (b) Thyroxin (c) Oestrogen (d) Cytokinin.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) Cytokinin</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Cytokinins are plant hormones that promote cell division (cytokinesis) and are present in high concentrations in rapidly dividing tissues such as fruits and seeds. Insulin, thyroxin, and oestrogen are animal hormones.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) Cytokinin with identification of plant cell division role</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The gap between two neurons is called a: (a) dendrite (b) synapse (c) axon (d) impulse.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) synapse</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> The microscopic structural and functional junction gap across which nerve impulses are transmitted via chemical neurotransmitters from an axon terminal to the dendrite of the next neuron is called a <strong>synapse</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) synapse with definition</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The brain is responsible for: (a) thinking (b) regulating the heart beat (c) balancing the body (d) all of the above.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) all of the above</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> The cerebrum controls thinking and reasoning, the medulla oblongata regulates vital involuntary activities like heartbeat and respiration, and the cerebellum maintains posture and body balance.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) all of the above with roles of cerebrum, medulla, and cerebellum</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the function of receptors in our body? Think of situations where receptors do not work properly. What problems are likely to arise?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Role of Receptors and Malfunction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Function of Receptors:</strong> Receptors are specialized sensory structures located in our sense organs that detect environmental stimuli (light, sound, heat, pressure, taste, smell) and convert them into electrical nerve impulses to inform the central nervous system.</li>\n  <li><strong>Problems When Receptors Malfunction:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Photoreceptors (Eyes):</em> Inability to perceive light signals, leading to partial or complete blindness and inability to navigate hazards.</li>\n      <li><em>Thermoreceptors / Nociceptors (Skin):</em> If heat and pain receptors fail, a person touching a scalding hot surface will not feel pain or withdraw their hand, resulting in severe tissue burns and ulceration.</li>\n      <li><em>Gustatory & Olfactory Receptors (Tongue & Nose):</em> Loss of taste and smell (as in severe viral respiratory infections), impairing appetite and preventing detection of spoiled food or leaking toxic gas.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Function: Detecting external stimuli and converting into nerve impulses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Consequences of pain receptor failure: Severe burns/injuries due to lack of withdrawal reflexes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Consequences of sensory receptor failure: Loss of environmental awareness/hazards</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Draw the structure of a neuron and explain its function.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Neuron Structure and Function:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Structural Components:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Cyton (Cell Body / Soma):</strong> Contains a prominent nucleus, neuroplasm, and Nissl's granules.</li>\n      <li><strong>Dendrites:</strong> Fine, branched protoplasmic projections radiating from the cell body that receive sensory inputs and chemical signals from adjacent neurons.</li>\n      <li><strong>Axon:</strong> A long cylindrical cytoplasmic process wrapped in an insulating <strong>myelin sheath</strong> (interrupted at Nodes of Ranvier) that conducts impulses rapidly away from the cyton.</li>\n      <li><strong>Axon Terminal / Synaptic Knobs:</strong> Bulbous nerve endings that store and release neurotransmitters.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Function:</strong> Neurons are specialized excitable cells that perceive stimuli, convert them into electrical nerve impulses, transmit impulses across synapses, and coordinate rapid communication between sensory receptors, the central nervous system, and effector muscles/glands.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Neuron structure description: Cyton (cell body), dendrites, axon with myelin sheath, terminals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Function: Receiving, conducting, and transmitting electrical nerve impulses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does phototropism occur in plants?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Phototropism:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>When growing plant shoots detect unilateral light coming from one side, the plant growth hormone <strong>auxin</strong> synthesized at the shoot tip diffuses towards the <strong>shaded side</strong> of the shoot.</li>\n  <li>The elevated concentration of auxin on the darker/shaded side stimulates cells to elongate more rapidly than cells on the illuminated side.</li>\n  <li>Because the shaded side grows faster than the sunlit side, the shoot bends smoothly towards the light source, exhibiting <strong>positive phototropism</strong>. Roots exhibit negative phototropism by growing away from light.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Auxin synthesised at shoot tip diffuses to the shaded side</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Higher auxin concentration accelerates cell elongation on shaded side causing bending towards light</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which signals will get disrupted in case of a spinal cord injury?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Disrupted Signals:</strong></p>\n<p>The spinal cord acts as the central communication highway between peripheral nerves and the brain. In the event of a spinal cord injury, the following signals will be severely disrupted:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Spinal Reflex Arcs:</strong> Automatic involuntary reflex actions below the level of injury (such as knee-jerk, withdrawal reflex) will be blocked.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ascending Sensory Signals:</strong> Sensory impulses carrying sensations of touch, temperature, pain, and pressure from lower body regions cannot reach the brain, causing loss of sensation (anaesthesia).</li>\n  <li><strong>Descending Motor Signals:</strong> Voluntary motor commands from the cerebral cortex cannot reach peripheral muscles, resulting in paralysis of limbs and loss of bladder/bowel control.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Disruption of spinal reflex actions below injury level</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Disruption of ascending sensory signals (pain/touch) and descending motor signals (voluntary paralysis)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does chemical coordination occur in plants?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Coordination in Plants:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Plants do not have a nervous system or muscular tissue. Coordination is carried out entirely through chemical substances called <strong>phytohormones (plant hormones)</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Different hormones are produced in response to environmental stimuli:\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Auxins:</strong> Direct tropic curvatures (phototropism, geotropism) by differential cell elongation.</li>\n      <li><strong>Gibberellins:</strong> Promote stem elongation and break seed dormancy.</li>\n      <li><strong>Cytokinins:</strong> Promote rapid cell division in fruits and seeds and delay leaf senescence.</li>\n      <li><strong>Abscisic Acid (ABA):</strong> Stress hormone that inhibits growth, causes stomatal closure during drought, and induces leaf fall (abscission).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>These chemical signals diffuse through plant tissues, activating biochemical responses in target cells.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Phytohormones synthesised in cells diffuse to regulate physiological growth responses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Specific hormones and roles: Auxins (tropism), Cytokinins (cell division), ABA (growth inhibition)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the need for a system of control and coordination in an organism?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Need for Control and Coordination:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Integration of Diverse Organs:</strong> A multicellular organism contains trillions of cells organized into specialized organs (heart, lungs, digestive tract, muscles). For the organism to survive, these organs must work in harmony and precise synchrony rather than independently.</li>\n  <li><strong>Survival and Response to Stimuli:</strong> Animals must perceive environmental changes (predators, food, danger) and execute immediate coordinated movements to survive.</li>\n  <li><strong>Homeostasis:</strong> It maintains internal physiological stability (body temperature, blood glucose levels, blood pressure, osmoregulation) despite fluctuations in the external environment.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Harmonising and integrating the independent functions of different organ systems</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ensuring timely survival responses to external stimuli and maintaining internal homeostasis</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How are involuntary actions and reflex actions different from each other?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Involuntary Action</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Reflex Action</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Controlling Center</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Controlled by the <strong>hindbrain (medulla oblongata)</strong> and midbrain.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Controlled primarily by the <strong>spinal cord</strong> (spinal reflex arc).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Nature of Trigger</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Occur continuously or rhythmically without needing a sudden external emergency stimulus.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Triggered instantaneously in response to a sudden external sensory stimulus.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Heartbeat, peristalsis in gut, breathing movements, blood pressure.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Knee jerk, pupil constriction in bright light, pulling hand away from heat.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Involuntary actions: Controlled by hindbrain/medulla; regular continuous physiological actions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reflex actions: Controlled by spinal cord; sudden emergency responses to sensory stimuli</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Compare and contrast nervous and hormonal mechanisms for control and coordination in animals.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Nervous vs Hormonal Mechanisms:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Property</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Nervous Coordination</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Hormonal Coordination</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Signal Medium</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Electrochemical nerve impulses transmitted along specialized axons.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Chemical molecules (hormones) circulating in the bloodstream.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Speed of Action</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Extremely rapid (milliseconds).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Slow, gradual response (minutes to days).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Duration of Effect</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Short-lived and transient; ceases once impulse stops.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Long-lasting and persistent physiological changes.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Target Specificity</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Point-to-point wiring; acts only on cells directly innervated by nerve endings.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Widespread; acts on all cells bearing matching receptor proteins.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electrochemical impulses via neurons vs chemical messengers via bloodstream</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rapid short-lived point-to-point response vs slow long-lasting widespread response</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Innervated cells only vs all cells bearing receptor proteins</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the difference between the manner in which movement takes place in a sensitive plant and the movement in our legs?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Movement Mechanisms:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Movement in Sensitive Plant (<em>Mimosa pudica</em>):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Plants lack nervous tissue and specialized contractile muscle proteins.</li>\n      <li>Movement is mediated by chemical transmission of signals that alter the <strong>turgor pressure</strong> in the pulvinus cells at leaf bases. By losing water, cells become flaccid, causing the leaves to droop.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Movement in Our Legs:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Initiated by conscious motor nerve impulses from the brain acting across neuromuscular junctions.</li>\n      <li>Leg movements rely on specialized skeletal muscle cells containing specialized <strong>contractile proteins (actin and myosin)</strong> that actively slide across one another, changing cell shape and contracting the muscle.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Plant movement: changes in cell turgor pressure (water loss/gain) without muscles</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Leg movement: conscious nervous control causing sliding of contractile proteins (actin/myosin) in muscle cells</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 6</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Feedback Mechanism in Endocrine Regulation</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">The timing and amount of hormone released are strictly regulated by feedback mechanisms. (i) Explain the negative feedback mechanism regulating blood sugar levels using insulin. (ii) Which organ detects the rise in blood glucose and which cells secrete the corrective hormone? (iii) What happens to hormone secretion once blood glucose returns to normal?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Negative Feedback Mechanism:</strong> When blood sugar levels rise after a meal, this physiological deviation acts as a signal to the pancreas. The pancreas secretes insulin, which stimulates body cells to take up glucose and liver cells to convert glucose into glycogen. As blood sugar drops back to the normal baseline, the stimulus for insulin secretion disappears, turning off hormone release.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Detecting Organ and Secretory Cells:</strong> The <strong>Pancreas</strong> detects the elevated blood glucose, and its <strong>$\\beta$-cells of the Islets of Langerhans</strong> secrete insulin.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Response upon Normalisation:</strong> Once blood glucose levels fall back to the normal physiological range ($70 - 100\\text{ mg/dL}$ fasting), negative feedback inhibits the $\\beta$-cells, causing insulin secretion to decline sharply, preventing hypoglycaemia.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(5)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(7)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "7": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch7\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">7</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 7: How do Organisms Reproduce?</h2>\n      <p>Asexual Modes (Fission, Budding, Spores, Vegetative Propagation), Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants, Human Reproductive System & Contraception — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 118, 123, 128) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 11)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 118 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the importance of DNA copying in reproduction?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Significance of DNA Copying:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Transmission of Genetic Blueprint:</strong> DNA contains all biochemical instructions for cellular design, organ structure, and physiological functioning. Accurate DNA replication before cell division ensures that identical genetic blueprints are transmitted from parental cells to offspring.</li>\n  <li><strong>Facilitating Natural Variations:</strong> No biochemical replication mechanism is 100% error-free. Small subtle inaccuracies during replication introduce minor genetic <strong>variations</strong> in the progeny. These variations provide raw material for natural selection, enabling species to survive in changing ecological environments.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ensures accurate inheritance of genetic traits and body design from parent to offspring</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inaccuracies during replication create variations essential for evolution and survival</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 118 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is variation beneficial to the species but not necessarily for the individual?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Variation: Species vs Individual:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>For the Individual:</strong> A drastic mutation or sudden variation may be disadvantageous or lethal for an individual organism if it disrupts normal cellular metabolism or physiology in its current habitat.</li>\n  <li><strong>For the Species:</strong> If a catastrophic environmental change occurs (such as global warming, temperature rise, or drought), a uniform population without variation could be completely wiped out. However, if some variant individuals in the population possess heat-resistant or drought-resistant traits, they will survive the catastrophe and reproduce, preventing the species from becoming extinct.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Individual may suffer disadvantage or death from drastic unsuited mutations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Species benefits because variant members survive sudden ecological crises, preventing extinction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 123 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does binary fission differ from multiple fission?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Binary Fission</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Multiple Fission</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Number of Daughters</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Parent cell divides into exactly <strong>two</strong> equal daughter cells.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Parent cell divides simultaneously into <strong>many</strong> daughter individuals.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Conditions & Cyst</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Occurs during favorable conditions; no cyst is formed.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Often occurs during unfavorable conditions; parent encloses in a protective cyst wall.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><em>Amoeba</em>, <em>Paramecium</em>, <em>Leishmania</em>.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><em>Plasmodium</em> (malarial parasite), encysted <em>Amoeba</em>.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Binary fission: Parent divides into 2 equal daughters in favorable conditions (Amoeba)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Multiple fission: Parent divides into many daughters simultaneously inside cyst (Plasmodium)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 123 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How will an organism be benefited if it reproduces through spores?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Benefits of Spore Formation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Protective Thick Wall:</strong> Spores (as in <em>Rhizopus</em> bread mould) are microscopic reproductive units enclosed by thick, durable protective coats that resist extreme desiccation, high temperatures, and lack of nutrients. They can remain dormant for months.</li>\n  <li><strong>Easy Aerial Dispersal:</strong> Spores are minute and lightweight, allowing them to be dispersed over vast geographic distances by wind and air currents.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rapid Proliferation:</strong> A single sporangium produces thousands of spores. Upon landing on a suitable moist substrate with organic food, each spore germinates into a new mycelium colony.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Thick protective coat withstands unfavorable harsh environmental conditions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lightweight allows wide aerial dispersal and rapid multiplication upon favorable contact</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 123 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Can you think of reasons why more complex organisms cannot give rise to new individuals through regeneration?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Simple organisms like <em>Planaria</em> and <em>Hydra</em> have relatively simple, non-differentiated body organization containing abundant uncommitted stem-like reserve cells capable of rapid mitosis and differentiation into any tissue.</li>\n  <li>In complex multicellular organisms (like humans, birds, insects), cells are organized into specialized tissues, tissues into organs, and organs into integrated organ systems. Once cells differentiate into specialized nerve, muscle, or bone cells, they lose the totipotent ability to regenerate an entire organism from a severed body part.</li>\n  <li>Therefore, complex organisms require specialized reproductive tissues and germ cells (gametes) rather than simple physical regeneration.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Complex organisms have high structural differentiation: cells -> tissues -> organs -> systems</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Specialised differentiated cells lose totipotency and cannot regenerate an entire body from a fragment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 123 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is vegetative propagation practised for growing some types of plants?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Advantages of Vegetative Propagation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Seedless Plants:</strong> Plants that have lost the capacity to produce viable seeds (such as banana, seedless grapes, orange, rose, and jasmine) can be propagated only through vegetative parts (cuttings, runners, grafting).</li>\n  <li><strong>Genetic Uniformity:</strong> All offspring plants produced vegetatively are genetically identical clones of the parent, preserving desirable commercial characteristics (fruit flavor, disease resistance, flower size).</li>\n  <li><strong>Faster Maturity:</strong> Plants grown vegetatively bear flowers and fruits much earlier than those raised from seeds.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Propagates seedless plants (banana, rose, jasmine) that cannot reproduce by seeds</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Preserves exact desirable parental traits (genetic clones) and ensures faster flowering/fruiting</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 123 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is DNA copying an essential part of the process of reproduction?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Essential Nature of DNA Copying:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Reproduction involves the creation of a new independent living individual resembling its parents in basic body structure and biochemical capabilities.</li>\n  <li>DNA in chromosomes stores the master biochemical blueprint for building proteins and cell structures.</li>\n  <li>Therefore, DNA copying is essential to create two identical copies of genetic material so that each daughter cell receives a complete set of genetic instructions along with sufficient cellular apparatus to sustain life.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">DNA contains all genetic information for structural and metabolic functioning</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Copying ensures both daughter cells receive complete parental genetic material</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 128 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the process of pollination different from fertilisation?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Pollination</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Fertilisation</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Physical transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of a flower.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Biochemical fusion of the male gamete with the female gamete (egg) to form a diploid zygote.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Site of Occurrence</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Occurs on the outer surface of the stigma.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Occurs inside the ovule situated within the ovary.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>External Agents</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Requires pollinating agents (wind, insects, water, birds).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Internal process; mediated by pollen tube growth.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pollination: physical transfer of pollen from anther to stigma via wind/insects</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fertilisation: internal biochemical fusion of male gamete and egg forming a diploid zygote</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 128 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the role of the seminal vesicles and the prostate gland?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Functions of Accessory Glands in Male Reproductive System:</strong></p>\n<p>The secretions of the <strong>seminal vesicles</strong> and <strong>prostate gland</strong> mix with sperm cells from the vas deferens to form <strong>semen</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Fluid Medium for Transport:</strong> Their alkaline secretions provide a liquid medium that allows sperm cells to swim actively and smoothly through the male urethra and female reproductive tract.</li>\n  <li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> Seminal vesicle fluid contains <strong>fructose</strong>, citric acid, and enzymes that provide metabolic energy to power sperm motility.</li>\n  <li><strong>Neutralising Acidity:</strong> The alkaline secretions neutralize the acidic environment of both the male urethra and the female vagina, protecting sperm from death.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Provide fluid medium and nutrients (fructose) for sperm motility and viability</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Alkaline secretions neutralise acidity in urethra and vagina to protect sperms</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 128 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the changes seen in girls at the time of puberty?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Secondary Sexual Characteristics in Girls at Puberty (Ages 10–12):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Development of Breasts:</strong> Enlargement of mammary glands with darkening of skin of the nipples.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pubic and Axillary Hair:</strong> Growth of fine hair in the pubic region and underarm armpits.</li>\n  <li><strong>Broadening of Hips:</strong> Widening of the pelvic girdle and fat deposition on hips and thighs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Initiation of Menstruation (Menarche):</strong> Commencing monthly menstrual bleeding cycle signaling ovarian ovulation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Skin Changes:</strong> Increased sebaceous gland secretion, sometimes leading to acne.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Breast development, pelvic widening, and pubic/axillary hair growth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Commencement of menstruation (menarche) and ovulation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 128 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does the embryo get nourishment inside the mother's body?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Nourishment via Placenta:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The developing embryo is connected to the maternal uterine wall by a specialized disc-like vascular tissue called the <strong>Placenta</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Structure:</strong> The placenta contains millions of microscopic finger-like <strong>villi</strong> on the embryonic side, embedded into large maternal blood spaces on the mother's side.</li>\n  <li><strong>Function:</strong> This vascular interface provides a huge surface area through which glucose, amino acids, and oxygen diffuse directly from maternal blood into fetal capillaries, while metabolic wastes ($\\text{CO}_2$, urea) produced by the embryo diffuse into maternal blood for excretion.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Specialised disc-shaped tissue called placenta connecting embryo to uterine wall</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Placental villi facilitate diffusion of glucose and oxygen into embryo and remove waste</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 128 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">If a woman is using a copper-T, will it help in protecting her from sexually transmitted diseases?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>No</strong>, a copper-T does not protect against Sexually Transmitted Diseases (STDs).</p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> A copper-T is an Intrauterine Device (IUD) placed inside the uterus to prevent implantation of the embryo. It does not provide a physical barrier to prevent fluid contact between reproductive organs; hence viral and bacterial STD pathogens (like HIV, syphilis, gonorrhoea) can still be transmitted. Only <strong>barrier methods (condoms)</strong> protect against STDs.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">No; copper-T prevents pregnancy/implantation but does not block fluid contact transmitting STDs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Asexual reproduction takes place through budding in: (a) amoeba (b) yeast (c) plasmodium (d) leishmania.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) yeast</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Yeast reproduces asexually by forming small bulb-like cellular outgrowths called <strong>buds</strong> that detach from the parent cell. <em>Amoeba</em> and <em>Leishmania</em> reproduce by binary fission, and <em>Plasmodium</em> reproduces by multiple fission.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) yeast with budding description</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following is not a part of the female reproductive system in human beings? (a) Ovary (b) Uterus (c) Vas deferens (d) Fallopian tube.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) Vas deferens</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> The <strong>vas deferens</strong> is a sperm duct belonging to the <strong>male</strong> reproductive system that conducts sperm from the epididymis to the urethra. Ovary, uterus, and fallopian tubes are female reproductive organs.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) Vas deferens with identification as male sperm duct</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The anther contains: (a) sepals (b) ovules (c) carpel (d) pollen grains.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) pollen grains</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> The anther is the terminal bilobed fertile part of the stamen (male organ) that produces microspores called <strong>pollen grains</strong> containing male gametes.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) pollen grains</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the advantages of sexual reproduction over asexual reproduction?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Advantages of Sexual Reproduction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Genetic Diversity & Variation:</strong> Sexual reproduction involves the fusion of gametes from two genetically distinct individuals, combined with crossing-over during meiosis. This produces offspring with novel genetic combinations and high phenotypic <strong>diversity</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Adaptation and Survival:</strong> Increased variation enhances a population's capacity to adapt to changing environments, resist emerging pathogens, and avoid extinction during natural catastrophes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Driver of Evolution:</strong> Variations generated by sexual reproduction serve as the fundamental raw material for natural selection and speciation.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Generates genetic diversity through combination of genes from two parents and meiosis</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Promotes better adaptability and survival against changing environmental conditions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Provides essential variations fueling natural selection and biological evolution</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the functions performed by the testis in human beings?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Dual Functions of Testes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Spermatogenesis:</strong> Production of male gametes (<strong>sperms</strong>) within the seminiferous tubules.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hormone Secretion:</strong> Endocrine Leydig cells in the testes secrete the primary male sex hormone <strong>testosterone</strong>, which controls:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Development of secondary sexual characteristics in males (facial hair, deep voice, muscle development).</li>\n      <li>Maturation of sperm and male accessory reproductive glands.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Production of male gametes (sperms)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Secretion of testosterone hormone regulating secondary sexual characteristics</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does menstruation occur?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason for Menstruation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Every month, one ovary releases a mature egg (ovulation). In anticipation of receiving a fertilized zygote, the inner lining of the uterus (<strong>endometrium</strong>) thickens, becomes soft, and develops an extensive blood capillary network to nourish a potential embryo.</li>\n  <li>If the egg is <strong>not fertilised</strong> by a sperm, the unfertilised ovum disintegrates.</li>\n  <li>Deprived of hormonal support (progesterone levels plummet), the thick, vascular uterine endometrial lining breaks down and sheds.</li>\n  <li>The detached mucosal lining along with blood and unfertilised egg is discharged through the vagina as <strong>menstruation</strong>, lasting approximately 3 to 5 days.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Uterine lining thickens each month to prepare for receiving fertilized egg</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">If fertilisation fails, vascular endometrial lining breaks down and discharges as blood/tissue</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Draw a labelled diagram of the longitudinal section of a flower.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Four Whorls of a Typical Flower (Longitudinal Section):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Calyx (Sepals):</strong> Outermost green leaf-like whorl protecting the flower in bud stage.</li>\n  <li><strong>Corolla (Petals):</strong> Brightly coloured whorl that attracts insect pollinators.</li>\n  <li><strong>Androecium (Male Reproductive Whorl - Stamens):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Filament:</em> Slender supportive stalk.</li>\n      <li><em>Anther:</em> Bilobed terminal head producing pollen grains.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Gynoecium / Pistil (Female Reproductive Whorl - Carpel):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Stigma:</em> Sticky terminal surface that traps pollen grains.</li>\n      <li><em>Style:</em> Elongated tubular neck connecting stigma to ovary.</li>\n      <li><em>Ovary:</em> Swollen basal chamber enclosing one or more <strong>ovules</strong> with female egg cells.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Labelled female carpel whorl: Stigma, style, ovary with ovules</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Labelled male stamen whorl: Anther and filament, plus petals and sepals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the different methods of contraception?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Contraceptive Methods:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Barrier Methods:</strong> Physical barriers like condoms, diaphragms, and cervical caps prevent physical contact and entry of sperms into the female genital tract. <em>(Condoms also provide protection against STDs).</em></li>\n  <li><strong>2. Chemical / Hormonal Methods:</strong> Oral contraceptive pills contain synthetic estrogen and progesterone combinations that inhibit ovulation so that no egg is released from the ovary.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Intrauterine Contraceptive Devices (IUCDs):</strong> Devices such as <strong>Copper-T</strong> inserted into the uterus by healthcare providers prevent implantation of the blastocyst.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Surgical Methods (Sterilization):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Vasectomy:</em> In males, a small section of each vas deferens is cut and tied to prevent sperm transfer into semen.</li>\n      <li><em>Tubectomy:</em> In females, a portion of each fallopian tube is cut and tied to prevent eggs from reaching the site of fertilisation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Barrier methods (condoms) and chemical/hormonal pills preventing ovulation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">IUCDs (Copper-T) preventing uterine implantation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Surgical methods: Vasectomy (males) and Tubectomy (females) as permanent contraception</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How are the modes for reproduction different in unicellular and multicellular organisms?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Unicellular Organisms:</strong> The entire body consists of a single microscopic cell. Reproduction is predominantly asexual and occurs by simple direct cell division (binary fission in <em>Amoeba</em>, budding in yeast, multiple fission in <em>Plasmodium</em>) where the parent cell divides into two or more daughters without specialized germ cells.</li>\n  <li><strong>Multicellular Organisms:</strong> Possess complex bodies with structural differentiation into tissues and organs. They cannot reproduce by simple fission because cells are specialized. Reproduction predominantly occurs sexually through specialized <strong>reproductive organs producing haploid gametes</strong> (sperms and ova), ensuring genetic recombination.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Unicellular: Simple direct asexual cell division (binary fission/budding) without gametes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Multicellular: Complex reproductive organs producing specialized haploid gametes via meiosis</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does reproduction help in providing stability to populations of species?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Role of Reproduction in Population Stability:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Living organisms in a population inevitably die due to aging, predation, disease, and environmental hazards.</li>\n  <li>Reproduction counterbalances mortality by continually adding new individuals of the same species to replace deceased members.</li>\n  <li>DNA copying during reproduction transmits species-specific morphological and physiological traits that keep the population suited to its specific ecological niche, maintaining demographic and genetic stability.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Replaces individuals lost due to death, disease, and aging to maintain population size</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Transmits adaptive body design ensuring stable occupation of ecological niche</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What could be the reasons for adopting contraceptive methods?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reasons for Adopting Contraceptive Methods:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Family Planning and Population Control:</strong> Prevents unwanted pregnancies, allowing parents to space births and support children with adequate nutrition, education, and resources.</li>\n  <li><strong>Protection of Maternal Health:</strong> Frequent, closely spaced pregnancies cause severe maternal anaemia, malnutrition, and reproductive health hazards.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention of STDs:</strong> Barrier methods (condoms) provide effective protection against sexually transmitted infections such as HIV/AIDS, syphilis, and gonorrhoea.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Prevention of unwanted pregnancies and family planning</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Protection of maternal health and prevention of STDs like HIV</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 7</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Double Fertilisation in Angiosperms</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">In flowering plants, the pollen tube delivers two male gametes into the embryo sac inside the ovule. (i) What is syngamy and what does it produce? (ii) What is triple fusion and what does it produce? (iii) Why is this process called double fertilisation?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Syngamy:</strong> The fusion of one haploid male gamete ($n$) with the haploid female egg cell ($n$) to form a diploid <strong>zygote ($2n$)</strong>, which later develops into the embryo.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Triple Fusion:</strong> The fusion of the second haploid male gamete ($n$) with the diploid secondary nucleus / two polar nuclei ($2n$) to form the triploid <strong>Primary Endosperm Nucleus (PEN, $3n$)</strong>, which develops into nutritive endosperm tissue.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Double Fertilisation:</strong> Because two distinct fertilisation events (syngamy and triple fusion) take place simultaneously in the same female embryo sac, the phenomenon is called <strong>Double Fertilisation</strong> (unique to angiosperms).</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(6)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(8)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "8": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch8\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">8</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 8: Heredity</h2>\n      <p>Mendelian Genetics (Monohybrid & Dihybrid Crosses), Law of Segregation, Independent Assortment, Chromosomes & Sex Determination — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 133, 138) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 10)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 133 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">If a trait A exists in 10% of a population of an asexually reproducing species and a trait B exists in 60% of the same population, which trait is likely to have arisen earlier?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Deduction:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Trait B</strong> is likely to have arisen earlier.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> In an asexually reproducing population, individuals reproduce by cell division where offspring are near-exact genetic clones of the parent with only minimal copying variations. A trait that originates earlier has had many more generations to replicate, accumulate, and spread across a larger fraction of the population ($60\\%$). Conversely, Trait A, being present in only $10\\%$ of the population, is a more recent mutation.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Trait B arose earlier</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Asexually reproducing traits accumulate over generations; 60% prevalence indicates more generations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 133 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does the creation of variations in a species promote survival?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Survival Value of Variations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Environmental niches are subject to drastic, unforeseen changes (such as sudden global warming, drought, freezing temperatures, or water acidity).</li>\n  <li>If an entire population is genetically identical, an environmental catastrophe can wipe out all individuals simultaneously.</li>\n  <li>However, if variations exist among individuals, some variants will possess pre-adapted traits (such as heat tolerance in bacteria) that allow them to survive the change. These surviving variants reproduce, ensuring that the species does not face extinction.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Environmental conditions change unpredictably</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pre-existing variations enable some individuals to tolerate extremes, preventing species extinction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 138 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How do Mendel's experiments show that traits may be dominant or recessive?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mendel's Monohybrid Cross:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Parental Cross:</strong> Mendel crossed pure-breeding tall pea plants ($TT$) with pure-breeding dwarf pea plants ($tt$).</li>\n  <li><strong>$F_1$ Generation Observation:</strong> In the first filial ($F_1$) generation, <strong>all plants were tall</strong>. There were no medium-height or dwarf plants.\n    $$TT \\times tt \\longrightarrow \\text{All } Tt \\text{ (Tall)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>$F_2$ Generation (Self-pollination of $F_1$):</strong> When $F_1$ tall plants were self-pollinated ($Tt \\times Tt$), both tall and dwarf plants reappeared in the $F_2$ generation in a strict <strong>$3 : 1$ ratio</strong> (75% tall, 25% dwarf).</li>\n  <li><strong>Deduction:</strong> The dwarf trait ($t$) was present in the $F_1$ generation but remained unexpressed in the presence of the tall allele ($T$).\n    <ul>\n      <li>The trait that expresses itself in $F_1$ is <strong>Dominant</strong> (Tall).</li>\n      <li>The trait that remains hidden in $F_1$ and reappears in $F_2$ is <strong>Recessive</strong> (Dwarf).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Monohybrid cross TT x tt yields 100% tall plants in F1 generation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Selfing F1 yields 3 tall : 1 dwarf phenotypic ratio in F2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Concludes: Expressed trait in F1 is dominant; hidden trait reappearing in F2 is recessive</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 138 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How do Mendel's experiments show that traits are inherited independently?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mendel's Dihybrid Cross:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Parental Cross:</strong> Mendel crossed a pea plant having <strong>Round Yellow seeds ($RRYY$)</strong> with a plant having <strong>Wrinkled Green seeds ($rryy$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>$F_1$ Progeny:</strong> All $F_1$ plants produced <strong>Round Yellow seeds ($RrYy$)</strong> because round and yellow are dominant traits.</li>\n  <li><strong>$F_2$ Generation (Selfing $F_1$):</strong> When $F_1$ plants were selfed ($RrYy \\times RrYy$), four distinct seed phenotypes were produced in the $F_2$ generation:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Round Yellow: <strong>9</strong> (Parental)</li>\n      <li>Round Green: <strong>3</strong> (Recombinant)</li>\n      <li>Wrinkled Yellow: <strong>3</strong> (Recombinant)</li>\n      <li>Wrinkled Green: <strong>1</strong> (Parental)</li>\n    </ul>\n    Phenotypic dihybrid ratio = <strong>$9 : 3 : 3 : 1$</strong>.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion (Law of Independent Assortment):</strong> The appearance of completely new non-parental combinations (Round Green and Wrinkled Yellow) proves that the inheritance of seed shape allele ($R/r$) is completely independent of the inheritance of seed colour allele ($Y/y$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dihybrid cross RRYY x rryy yields 100% Round Yellow (RrYy) in F1</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">F2 selfing yields 9:3:3:1 ratio with new recombinant phenotypes (Round Green & Wrinkled Yellow)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deduces Law of Independent Assortment: Alleles assort independently into gametes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 138 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A man with blood group A marries a woman with blood group O and their daughter has blood group O. Is this information enough to tell you which of the traits – blood group A or O – is dominant? Why or why not?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Genotypic Analysis:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>No</strong>, this information alone is not sufficient to establish which blood group allele is dominant.</li>\n  <li><strong>Case 1 (If A is dominant over O):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Mother with blood group O must be homozygous: $I^O I^O$.</li>\n      <li>Father with blood group A could be heterozygous: $I^A I^O$.</li>\n      <li>Their daughter inherits $I^O$ from father and $I^O$ from mother &rarr; daughter has blood group O ($I^O I^O$). This is consistent.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Case 2 (If O were dominant over A):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Father with blood group A would be homozygous $I^A I^A$.</li>\n      <li>Mother with blood group O could be heterozygous $I^O I^A$.</li>\n      <li>Daughter could inherit $I^O$ from mother and $I^A$ from father &rarr; daughter has blood group O. This could also be theoretically possible.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Without knowing the genotypic pedigree of other siblings or extended family, the cross alone does not deduce dominance. (From biological knowledge, $I^A$ is dominant and $I^O$ is recessive).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Not enough information from a single cross</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Father could be heterozygous IAIO or mother could be heterozygous; both models explain daughter having group O</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 138 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the sex of the child determined in human beings?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chromosomal Mechanism of Sex Determination:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Human beings possess <strong>23 pairs of chromosomes</strong> in somatic cell nuclei: 22 pairs of autosomes and <strong>1 pair of sex chromosomes</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Female (Homomorphic):</strong> Females have two identical X chromosomes (<strong>$XX$</strong>). All maternal ova produced during meiosis carry one X chromosome ($22 + X$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Male (Heteromorphic):</strong> Males have one X chromosome and one shorter Y chromosome (<strong>$XY$</strong>). Spermatogenesis produces two equal types of sperms in a $50 : 50$ ratio:\n    <ul>\n      <li>$50\\%$ of sperms carry an <strong>$X$ chromosome</strong> ($22 + X$).</li>\n      <li>$50\\%$ of sperms carry a <strong>$Y$ chromosome</strong> ($22 + Y$).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Fertilisation:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>If an ovum is fertilised by an <strong>X-bearing sperm</strong>, the zygote is <strong>$XX$</strong> &rarr; develops into a <strong>Female child (Girl)</strong>.</li>\n      <li>If an ovum is fertilised by a <strong>Y-bearing sperm</strong>, the zygote is <strong>$XY$</strong> &rarr; develops into a <strong>Male child (Boy)</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Therefore, sex is genetically determined at the moment of fertilisation by the father's sperm with an exact $50\\%$ theoretical probability for either sex.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Females are XX producing only X-type ova; Males are XY producing 50% X and 50% Y sperms</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fertilisation: X sperm + X ovum -> XX female; Y sperm + X ovum -> XY male</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Father determines the sex of child with 1:1 statistical probability</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A Mendelian experiment consisted of breeding tall pea plants bearing violet flowers with short pea plants bearing white flowers. The progeny all had violet flowers, but almost half of them were short. This suggests that the genetic make-up of the tall parent can be depicted as: (a) TTWW (b) TTww (c) TtWW (d) TtWw.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) TtWW</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Genotypic Deduction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Flower Colour:</strong> All progeny in the cross have violet flowers. Since white is recessive ($ww$), the tall parent must be homozygous dominant for flower colour (<strong>$WW$</strong>) so that all progeny receive at least one dominant $W$ allele.</li>\n  <li><strong>Plant Height:</strong> Almost half of the progeny are short/dwarf ($tt$). For a cross with a short plant ($tt$) to yield $50\\%$ short progeny ($Tt : tt = 1 : 1$), the tall parent must be heterozygous for height (<strong>$Tt$</strong>).</li>\n  <li>Combining both traits, the genetic makeup of the tall parent is <strong>$TtWW$</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) TtWW</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">All violet progeny implies WW; 50% short progeny in test-cross implies Tt</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A study found that children with light-coloured eyes are likely to have parents with light-coloured eyes. On this basis, can we say whether the light eye colour trait is dominant or recessive? Why or why not?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scientific Analysis:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>No</strong>, we cannot definitively conclude whether the light eye colour trait is dominant or recessive based solely on this observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>If light eye colour is a <strong>recessive trait ($bb$)</strong>, two light-eyed parents ($bb \\times bb$) will produce $100\\%$ light-eyed children ($bb$).</li>\n  <li>If light eye colour were a <strong>dominant trait ($BB$ or $Bb$)</strong>, two light-eyed parents could also produce light-eyed children ($BB \\times BB \\longrightarrow BB$).</li>\n  <li>To determine dominance conclusively, we require crosses between contrasting phenotypes (e.g., light-eyed parent $\\times$ dark-eyed parent) across at least two generations to observe which trait masks the other in $F_1$ and reappears in $F_2$.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cannot determine dominance without reciprocal contrasting crosses across two generations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Homozygous recessive and homozygous dominant both produce identical offspring phenotypes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Outline a project which aims to find the dominant coat colour in dogs.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Project Outline:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Selection of Pure-breeding Parents:</strong> Select pure-breeding homozygous dogs of two distinct contrasting coat colours, for example, pure black coat ($BB$) and pure white coat ($bb$).</li>\n  <li><strong>First Generation ($F_1$ Cross):</strong> Cross the pure black dog with the pure white dog ($BB \\times bb$). Observe the coat colour of all puppies in the $F_1$ litter.</li>\n  <li><strong>Deduction of Dominance:</strong> The coat colour that expresses itself in $100\\%$ of the $F_1$ puppies represents the <strong>dominant coat colour</strong>. (If all puppies are black, black is dominant).</li>\n  <li><strong>$F_2$ Verification:</strong> Interbreed $F_1$ black dogs ($Bb \\times Bb$). The appearance of black and white puppies in approximately a $3 : 1$ ratio in the $F_2$ generation confirms that black is dominant and white is recessive.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cross pure homozygous black dog with pure white dog to obtain F1 generation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Trait expressed in F1 is dominant; confirmed by 3:1 reappearance in F2 litter</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain how sexual reproduction gives rise to more viable variations than asexual reproduction. How does this affect the evolution of those organisms that reproduce sexually?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Origin of Variations in Sexual Reproduction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Biparental Inheritance:</strong> Sexual reproduction combines genetic material from two distinct parents, each carrying unique mutations and allele combinations.</li>\n  <li><strong>Meiotic Recombination & Independent Assortment:</strong> During gamete formation, homologous chromosomes undergo <strong>crossing over</strong> (reciprocal exchange of genetic segments) and maternal and paternal chromosomes assort independently, creating millions of genetically unique gametes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Random Fertilisation:</strong> Any male gamete can randomly fertilise any female gamete ($2^{23} \\times 2^{23}$ combinations in humans), creating virtually infinite variation in the zygotes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Impact on Evolution:</strong> In contrast to asexual reproduction where variations arise solely from rare replication errors, sexual reproduction continuously generates vast pools of genetic diversity. Natural selection acts on these diverse variations, selecting advantageous adaptations and accelerating evolutionary divergence and speciation.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Biparental genetic contribution combines diverse alleles from two individuals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Crossing over during meiosis and independent assortment generates unique gametes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rich variation pool accelerates natural selection and evolutionary adaptation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the equal genetic contribution of male and female parents ensured in the progeny?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism Ensuring Equal Genetic Contribution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Somatic body cells in human parents are <strong>diploid ($2n$)</strong>, containing two complete sets of chromosomes (46 chromosomes).</li>\n  <li>During gametogenesis, specialized germ cells undergo <strong>reductional cell division (Meiosis)</strong>, halving the chromosome number. Consequently, both sperm (male gamete) and ovum (female gamete) are <strong>haploid ($n$)</strong>, carrying exactly 23 chromosomes.</li>\n  <li>During fertilisation, the haploid nucleus of the sperm fuses with the haploid nucleus of the ovum:\n    $$n\\text{ (from Father)} + n\\text{ (from Mother)} \\longrightarrow 2n\\text{ (Zygote)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>The resulting zygote receives exactly 23 maternal chromosomes and 23 paternal chromosomes, ensuring identical $50\\%$ genetic contribution from both parents.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Meiosis halves chromosome number producing haploid gametes (n = 23 chromosomes)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fertilisation restores diploid status (2n = 46), ensuring exact 50% contribution from each parent</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Only variations that confer an advantage to an individual organism will survive in a population. Do you agree with this statement? Why or why not?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Critical Evaluation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>No, the statement is not completely accurate.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>While it is true that variations offering an immediate adaptive advantage are positively favored by <strong>natural selection</strong> and spread rapidly in a population, not all surviving variations are advantageous.</li>\n  <li><strong>Neutral Variations:</strong> Many genetic variations are functionally neutral (they neither help nor hinder survival, such as free vs attached earlobes or different fingerprint patterns). These neutral traits persist in populations over generations without being eliminated.</li>\n  <li><strong>Genetic Drift:</strong> In small populations, chance random events (accidents, floods, fires) can cause non-advantageous or even disadvantageous alleles to become fixed purely by random sampling errors rather than adaptive survival.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Disagreement: Not all surviving variations confer direct selective advantage</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Neutral variations persist without selection, and genetic drift can fix neutral/neutral-deleterious traits</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Mendel explain that traits may be dominant or recessive? Explain with monohybrid cross.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Law of Dominance:</strong></p>\n<p>Mendel crossed a homozygous tall pea plant ($TT$) with a homozygous dwarf plant ($tt$).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In the $F_1$ generation, all offspring plants possessed the hybrid genotype $Tt$, but exhibited the <strong>tall phenotype</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The dwarf trait seemed to disappear. When $F_1$ plants were self-pollinated ($Tt \\times Tt$), the dwarf trait reappeared in $25\\%$ of the $F_2$ plants ($3\\text{ Tall} : 1\\text{ Dwarf}$).</li>\n  <li>Mendel concluded that each trait is controlled by a pair of distinct unit factors (alleles). In a dissimilar pair ($Tt$), one factor masks the phenotypic expression of the other. The allele that expresses itself is called <strong>Dominant ($T$)</strong>, and the masked allele is called <strong>Recessive ($t$)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cross TT x tt yields all Tt (tall) in F1; selfing produces 3:1 in F2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">In heterozygous pair Tt, the expressed allele is dominant and suppressed allele is recessive</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain with the help of a cross how the sex of a child is genetically determined in humans.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Sex Determination Cross:</strong></p>\n<p>Father: $44\\text{ Autosomes} + XY$ &nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp; Mother: $44\\text{ Autosomes} + XX$</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Father \\ Mother</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Ovum (X)</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Ovum (X)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Sperm (X)</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>XX (Female Child - Girl)</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>XX (Female Child - Girl)</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Sperm (Y)</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>XY (Male Child - Boy)</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>XY (Male Child - Boy)</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Outcome:</strong> 2 out of 4 are $XX$ (Girl) and 2 out of 4 are $XY$ (Boy). Probability of having a boy or a girl is exactly $50\\%$ ($1 : 1$). The sex is determined by whether the fertilising sperm carries an X or a Y chromosome.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Punnett square showing gametic combinations: XX (50%) and XY (50%)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deducing 1:1 ratio and explaining sperm determines the gender</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are chromosomes? How many pairs of chromosomes are present in human cells?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition and Number of Chromosomes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Chromosomes:</strong> Deeply staining, thread-like rod-shaped nucleoprotein structures located inside the cell nucleus, consisting of tightly coiled double-stranded DNA wrapped around basic histone proteins. They carry genes—the units of heredity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Number in Human Cells:</strong> Human diploid somatic cells contain <strong>23 pairs of chromosomes</strong> (46 chromosomes in total):\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>22 pairs of Autosomes:</strong> Regulate non-sexual somatic traits identically in males and females.</li>\n      <li><strong>1 pair of Sex Chromosomes (Allosomes):</strong> Determines biological sex ($XX$ in females, $XY$ in males).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Thread-like structures composed of DNA and histones carrying hereditary genes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">23 pairs in human somatic cells: 22 pairs autosomes + 1 pair sex chromosomes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is a gene? Where are genes located?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition and Location of Genes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Gene:</strong> A gene is the basic physical and functional unit of heredity. Chemically, it is a specific segment or sequence of nucleotides in a <strong>DNA molecule</strong> that encodes the amino acid sequence for synthesizing a specific functional protein or enzyme, thereby determining a particular physical or biochemical trait of an organism.</li>\n  <li><strong>Location:</strong> Genes are located at specific, fixed linear positions called <strong>loci</strong> (singular: locus) along the length of <strong>chromosomes</strong> inside the cell nucleus.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Specific segment of DNA carrying instructions for synthesizing a protein determining a trait</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Located at specific fixed positions (loci) along chromosomes inside the nucleus</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Board Benchmark • Q17</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are alleles? Differentiate between homozygous and heterozygous genotypes with an example.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definitions:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Alleles:</strong> Alternative forms of a given gene located at the same locus on homologous chromosomes that govern contrasting expressions of a trait (e.g., $T$ for tallness and $t$ for dwarfness).</li>\n  <li><strong>Homozygous Genotype:</strong> An organism carrying two identical alleles for a particular trait (e.g., $TT$ for homozygous tall or $tt$ for homozygous dwarf).</li>\n  <li><strong>Heterozygous Genotype:</strong> An organism carrying two dissimilar alleles for a particular trait (e.g., $Tt$ for heterozygous tall).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Alleles defined as alternative forms of a gene at the same chromosomal locus</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Homozygous (identical alleles: TT/tt) vs Heterozygous (dissimilar alleles: Tt)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Board Benchmark • Q18</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the difference between phenotype and genotype?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Phenotype</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Genotype</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">The observable physical, physiological, or biochemical manifestation of a trait in an individual.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">The specific genetic/allelic constitution of an individual responsible for a trait.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Observation</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Directly observable by physical inspection (e.g., Tall or Dwarf).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Determined through breeding crosses or DNA sequencing (e.g., $TT, Tt, tt$).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Phenotype: observable physical manifestation of a trait</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Genotype: genetic/allelic makeup of an organism for that trait</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Board Benchmark • Q19</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did Gregor Johann Mendel select garden pea plant (Pisum sativum) for his hybridization experiments?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Advantages of Selecting Garden Pea (<em>Pisum sativum</em>):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Distinct Contrasting Traits:</strong> Pea plants have easily distinguishable, clear contrasting traits (e.g., Tall/Dwarf, Round/Wrinkled seeds, Yellow/Green seeds).</li>\n  <li><strong>Short Life Span & High Seed Yield:</strong> It is an annual plant with a brief life cycle (2–3 months), allowing multiple generations to be studied quickly, and produces abundant seeds per pod.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bisexual Flowers with Easy Self/Cross Pollination:</strong> Naturally self-pollinating due to enclosed petals, but easy to artificially cross-pollinate by simple emasculation (removing anthers) and dusting desired pollen.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Clear distinct contrasting traits (tall/dwarf, round/wrinkled)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Short life cycle, high seed yield, and naturally self-pollinating with easy emasculation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Board Benchmark • Q20</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State Mendel's Law of Segregation and Law of Independent Assortment.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mendel's Laws of Inheritance:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Law of Segregation (Purity of Gametes):</strong> During gamete formation (meiosis), the paired alleles of a gene segregate (separate) from each other randomly such that each gamete receives only one allele of the pair with equal probability.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Law of Independent Assortment:</strong> When two pairs of contrasting traits are combined in a hybrid (dihybrid cross), the segregation of alleles of one gene pair into gametes is completely independent of the segregation of alleles of the other gene pair.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Law of Segregation: paired alleles separate during gamete formation into haploid gametes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Law of Independent Assortment: alleles of different genes assort independently during meiosis</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 8</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Pedigree & Blood Group Inheritance</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">In a human maternity hospital, two babies got mixed up. Baby 1 has blood group O, and Baby 2 has blood group AB. Couple X has Father with blood group A and Mother with blood group B. Couple Y has Father with blood group AB and Mother with blood group O. (i) Determine the child of Couple Y with genetic justification. (ii) Can Couple X be the biological parents of Baby 1? Explain the required genotypes. (iii) Which blood group alleles exhibit codominance in humans?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Couple Y:</strong> Father has group AB ($I^A I^B$) and Mother has group O ($I^O I^O$).\n    <br>Possible children: $I^A I^O$ (Group A) or $I^B I^O$ (Group B).\n    <br>Couple Y can <strong>never have a child with blood group O ($I^O I^O$) or AB ($I^A I^B$)</strong>.\n    <br>Therefore, an error in hospital labeling occurred: Baby 1 cannot belong to Y. However, between Couple X ($I^A I^O \\times I^B I^O$), all four blood groups (A, B, AB, O) can be born!\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Couple X:</strong> Yes, if Father is heterozygous A ($I^A I^O$) and Mother is heterozygous B ($I^B I^O$), they have a $25\\%$ probability of having a child with blood group O ($I^O I^O$) and $25\\%$ probability of blood group AB ($I^A I^B$).</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Codominance:</strong> Alleles <strong>$I^A$ and $I^B$</strong> exhibit codominance: when both are present together ($I^A I^B$), both A and B antigens are expressed on RBCs, producing blood group AB.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(7)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(9)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "9": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch9\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">9</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 9: Light – Reflection and Refraction</h2>\n      <p>Spherical Mirrors, Mirror Formula & Magnification, Laws of Refraction, Snell's Law, Refractive Index, Lenses & Lens Power — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 149, 153, 161, 166) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 17)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 149 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Define the principal focus of a concave mirror.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>principal focus</strong> of a concave mirror is a fixed point on its principal axis at which all incident light rays traveling parallel and close to the principal axis actually converge and intersect after undergoing reflection from the mirror surface.</p>\n<p>It is denoted by the capital letter <strong>$F$</strong>, and lies in front of the reflecting surface at a distance equal to half the radius of curvature ($f = R/2$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Point on principal axis where parallel incident rays converge after reflection</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Denoted by F and lies in front of concave mirror at distance f = R/2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 149 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The radius of curvature of a spherical mirror is 20 cm. What is its focal length?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>The relationship between the radius of curvature ($R$) and focal length ($f$) of a spherical mirror is given by:</p>\n$$f = \\frac{R}{2}$$\n<p>Given: $R = 20\\text{ cm}$</p>\n$$f = \\frac{20\\text{ cm}}{2} = \\mathbf{10\\text{ cm}}$$\n<p>Therefore, the focal length of the spherical mirror is <strong>$10\\text{ cm}$</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula: f = R / 2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Substitution and correct answer: f = 10 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 149 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name a mirror that can give an erect and enlarged image of an object.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>A <strong>Concave mirror</strong> can produce an erect and enlarged (magnified) virtual image when the object is placed between its pole ($P$) and principal focus ($F$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Concave mirror (when object is placed between pole and focus)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 149 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do we prefer a convex mirror as a rear-view mirror in vehicles?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reasons:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Always Produces an Erect Image:</strong> A convex mirror always forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image of objects situated behind the vehicle, regardless of their distance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Much Wider Field of View:</strong> Because a convex mirror is curved outwards towards the driver, it captures light from a much wider angle than a plane mirror of the same size, enabling the driver to view a much larger traffic area behind the vehicle.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Always forms an erect and diminished image</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Curved outwards giving a much wider field of view to the driver</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 153 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Find the focal length of a convex mirror whose radius of curvature is 32 cm.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>According to the Cartesian sign convention, the radius of curvature of a convex mirror is positive: $R = +32\\text{ cm}$.</p>\n$$f = \\frac{R}{2} = \\frac{+32\\text{ cm}}{2} = \\mathbf{+16\\text{ cm}}$$\n<p>The focal length of the convex mirror is <strong>$+16\\text{ cm}$</strong> (located behind the mirror).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula: f = R / 2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct answer with positive sign convention: +16 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 153 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A concave mirror produces three times magnified (enlarged) real image of an object placed at 10 cm in front of it. Where is the image located?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Given Data:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Object distance, $u = -10\\text{ cm}$ (in front of mirror).</li>\n      <li>Image is <em>real and inverted</em>, so magnification $m = -3$.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Mirror Magnification Formula:</strong>\n    $$m = -\\frac{v}{u}$$\n    $$-3 = -\\frac{v}{-10} = \\frac{v}{10}$$\n    $$v = -3 \\times 10 = \\mathbf{-30\\text{ cm}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The negative sign indicates that the image is formed on the same side as the object. The image is located at a distance of <strong>$30\\text{ cm}$ in front of the concave mirror</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying real inverted image implies m = -3 and u = -10 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Using m = -v/u to find v = -30 cm (30 cm in front of mirror)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 161 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A ray of light travelling in air enters obliquely into water. Does the light ray bend towards the normal or away from the normal? Why?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Refraction Analysis:</strong></p>\n<p>The ray of light will <strong>bend towards the normal</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Water is an <strong>optically denser medium</strong> compared to air (refractive index of water $n_w \\approx 1.33$ vs air $n_a \\approx 1.00$). When light passes obliquely from an optically rarer medium (air) into an optically denser medium (water), its speed slows down ($v = c/n$). According to Snell's law of refraction ($\\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} = \\frac{n_2}{n_1} > 1 \\implies \\angle r < \\angle i$), the light ray bends towards the normal line at the interface.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Light bends towards the normal</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reason: Water is optically denser than air, causing speed of light to decrease</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 161 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Light enters from air to glass having refractive index 1.50. What is the speed of light in the glass? The speed of light in vacuum is $3 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>The absolute refractive index of a medium is defined as:</p>\n$$n = \\frac{c}{v}$$\n<p>Where $c = 3 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$ and $n = 1.50$.</p>\n$$v = \\frac{c}{n} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}}{1.50} = \\mathbf{2 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}}$$\n<p>The speed of light in glass is <strong>$2 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula: v = c / n</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculation: v = (3 x 10^8) / 1.50 = 2 x 10^8 m/s</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 161 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Find out, from the NCERT table of refractive indices, the medium having highest optical density. Also find the medium with lowest optical density.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Medium with Highest Optical Density:</strong> <strong>Diamond</strong>, with a refractive index of <strong>$n = 2.42$</strong>. Optical density is highest because light travels slowest in diamond ($v \\approx 1.24 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Medium with Lowest Optical Density:</strong> <strong>Air</strong>, with a refractive index of <strong>$n = 1.0003$</strong> (approaching vacuum $n=1$), where light travels fastest ($v \\approx 3 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Highest optical density: Diamond (n = 2.42)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lowest optical density: Air (n = 1.0003)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 161 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">You are given kerosene, turpentine and water. In which of these does the light travel fastest?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<p>The refractive indices of the given liquid media are:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Water: $n_w = 1.33$</li>\n  <li>Kerosene: $n_k = 1.44$</li>\n  <li>Turpentine oil: $n_t = 1.47$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Since speed of light in a medium is inversely proportional to its refractive index ($v \\propto 1/n$), light travels fastest in the medium having the <strong>lowest refractive index</strong>. Therefore, light travels fastest in <strong>Water</strong> ($v_w = c / 1.33 \\approx 2.26 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inverse relation v = c/n (lowest n gives fastest speed)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct medium: Water (n = 1.33)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 161 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The refractive index of diamond is 2.42. What is the meaning of this statement?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Physical Meaning:</strong></p>\n<p>The absolute refractive index of diamond $n = \\frac{c}{v} = 2.42$ means that:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The ratio of the speed of light in vacuum (or air) to the speed of light in diamond is $2.42$.</li>\n  <li>Light travels <strong>$2.42$ times slower in diamond</strong> than in vacuum:\n    $$v_{\\text{diamond}} = \\frac{c}{2.42} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}}{2.42} \\approx 1.24 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Diamond has an extraordinarily high optical density and a very small critical angle ($\\approx 24.4^\\circ$), causing total internal reflection and brilliant sparkle.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ratio of speed of light in vacuum to speed in diamond is 2.42</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Speed of light in diamond is reduced to 1/2.42 of its speed in vacuum</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 166 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Define 1 dioptre of power of a lens.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1 Dioptre (1 D)</strong> is defined as the optical power of a lens having a focal length of exactly <strong>one metre ($1\\text{ m}$)</strong>.</p>\n$$P = \\frac{1}{f\\text{ (in metres)}} \\implies 1\\text{ D} = 1\\text{ m}^{-1}$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">1 Dioptre is the power of a lens of focal length 1 metre (1 D = 1 m^-1)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 166 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A convex lens forms a real and inverted image of a needle at a distance of 50 cm from it. Where is the needle placed in front of the convex lens if the image is equal to the size of the object? Also, find the power of the lens.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Object Position:</strong> For a convex lens, an image of the <em>same size</em> as the object ($m = -1$) is formed when the object is placed at a distance equal to twice the focal length ($2f$) in front of the lens. The image is formed at $2f$ on the other side:\n    $$v = +50\\text{ cm} \\implies 2f = 50\\text{ cm} \\implies f = +25\\text{ cm} = +0.25\\text{ m}$$\n    $$u = -2f = \\mathbf{-50\\text{ cm}}$$\n    Therefore, the needle is placed <strong>$50\\text{ cm}$ in front of the convex lens</strong>.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Power of the Lens:</strong>\n    $$P = \\frac{1}{f\\text{ (in metres)}} = \\frac{1}{+0.25\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{+4\\text{ D}}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Object placed at 2F: u = -50 cm in front of lens</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Focal length f = +0.25 m and Power P = +4 D</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 166 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Find the power of a concave lens of focal length 2 m.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>For a concave lens, the focal length is negative: $f = -2\\text{ m}$.</p>\n$$P = \\frac{1}{f\\text{ (in metres)}} = \\frac{1}{-2\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{-0.5\\text{ D}}$$\n<p>The power of the concave lens is <strong>$-0.5\\text{ Dioptre}$</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Concave lens f = -2 m; P = 1 / (-2) = -0.5 D</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which one of the following materials cannot be used to make a lens? (a) Water (b) Glass (c) Plastic (d) Clay.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) Clay</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> A lens requires an optically transparent medium through which light can pass and undergo refraction. Clay is completely opaque and absorbs/blocks all incident light, so it cannot be used to construct a lens.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) Clay with transparency explanation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The image formed by a concave mirror is observed to be virtual, erect and larger than the object. Where should be the position of the object? (a) Between the principal focus and the centre of curvature (b) At the centre of curvature (c) Beyond the centre of curvature (d) Between the pole of the mirror and its principal focus.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) Between the pole of the mirror and its principal focus</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> When an object is placed between the pole ($P$) and principal focus ($F$) of a concave mirror, the reflected rays diverge. When produced backwards behind the mirror, they intersect to form a virtual, erect, and magnified image.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) Between the pole of the mirror and its principal focus</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Where should an object be placed in front of a convex lens to get a real image of the size of the object? (a) At the principal focus of the lens (b) At twice the focal length (c) At infinity (d) Between the optical centre of the lens and its principal focus.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) At twice the focal length ($2F_1$)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> When an object is placed at $2F_1$ in front of a convex lens, refracted rays converge at $2F_2$ on the opposite side, producing a real, inverted image having magnification $m = -1$ (equal to object size).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) At twice the focal length (2F1)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A spherical mirror and a thin spherical lens have each a focal length of -15 cm. The mirror and the lens are likely to be: (a) both concave (b) both convex (c) the mirror is concave and the lens is convex (d) the mirror is convex, but the lens is concave.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(a) both concave</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> According to the New Cartesian Sign Convention, distances measured to the left of the optical origin (against incident light) are negative. Both a concave mirror and a concave lens have their principal focal points located to the left, so their focal lengths are always <strong>negative</strong> ($f < 0$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (a) both concave with sign convention justification</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">No matter how far you stand from a mirror, your image appears erect. The mirror is likely to be: (a) only plane (b) only concave (c) only convex (d) either plane or convex.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) either plane or convex</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> A plane mirror always forms a virtual and erect image of the same size at all distances. A convex mirror also always forms a virtual and erect diminished image at all object distances.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) either plane or convex</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following lenses would you prefer to use while reading small letters found in a dictionary? (a) A convex lens of focal length 50 cm (b) A concave lens of focal length 50 cm (c) A convex lens of focal length 5 cm (d) A concave lens of focal length 5 cm.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) A convex lens of focal length 5 cm</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> To read small print, a convex lens is used as a simple magnifying glass. Magnifying power is $M = 1 + \\frac{D}{f}$. A smaller focal length ($f = 5\\text{ cm}$) yields a much higher optical power ($P = 1/0.05 = +20\\text{ D}$) and greater magnification than a $50\\text{ cm}$ lens ($P = +2\\text{ D}$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) A convex lens of focal length 5 cm with power/magnification rationale</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">We wish to obtain an erect image of an object, using a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm. What should be the range of distance of the object from the mirror? What is the nature of the image? Is the image larger or smaller than the object? Draw a ray diagram to show the image formation in this case.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Analysis:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Range of Object Distance:</strong> A concave mirror forms an erect image only when the object is placed between its pole ($P$) and principal focus ($F$). Given focal length $f = 15\\text{ cm}$, the object distance $u$ must be:\n    $$\\mathbf{0 < u < 15\\text{ cm}}$$\n    (i.e., less than $15\\text{ cm}$ in front of the mirror).\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Nature of the Image:</strong> The image is <strong>Virtual and Erect</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Size of Image:</strong> The image is <strong>Magnified (larger than the object)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ray Diagram:</strong> A ray from the top of the object parallel to the principal axis reflects through the focus $F$. Another ray striking the pole reflects symmetrically at an equal angle. The diverging reflected rays are extended backwards behind the mirror where they intersect to produce the erect virtual image $A'B'$.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Range: Between 0 and 15 cm from the mirror (between pole and focus)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Nature: Virtual and Erect; Size: Magnified (larger than object)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ray diagram showing rays diverging in front and converging behind mirror</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name the type of mirror used in the following situations: (a) Headlights of a car (b) Side/rear-view mirror of a vehicle (c) Solar furnace. Support your answer with reason.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Types of Mirrors and Scientific Rationale:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Headlights of a Car:</strong> <strong>Concave mirror</strong>.\n    <br><em>Reason:</em> When a high-intensity light bulb is placed at the principal focus of a concave reflector, all reflected rays emerge as a powerful, parallel beam of light that illuminates the road far ahead.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Side/rear-view Mirror of a Vehicle:</strong> <strong>Convex mirror</strong>.\n    <br><em>Reason:</em> Always forms an upright, diminished virtual image and has a wide outward curvature providing a broader field of view of trailing traffic.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(c) Solar Furnace:</strong> <strong>Large Concave mirror</strong> (or parabolic mirror).\n    <br><em>Reason:</em> It collects incoming parallel solar rays from the sun over a huge aperture and concentrates them at its principal focus, generating extreme temperatures ($>1800^\\circ\\text{C}$).\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(a) Concave mirror: bulb at focus produces parallel beam</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(b) Convex mirror: wide field of view with erect diminished image</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(c) Concave mirror: concentrates parallel solar rays at focus to generate intense heat</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">One-half of a convex lens is covered with a black paper. Will this lens produce a complete image of the object? Verify your answer experimentally. Explain your observations.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Experimental Verification and Scientific Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Will it produce a complete image?</strong> <strong>Yes</strong>, the lens will still produce a complete, full-sized image of the object.</li>\n  <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> Every point on the illuminated object emits countless light rays in all directions that strike all parts of the convex lens. Covering the lower (or upper) half with black paper only blocks half of the total light rays. The remaining uncovered half still refracts sufficient light rays to converge at the focal plane, forming the entire image.</li>\n  <li><strong>Observable Change:</strong> Because the total amount of light entering the lens is halved, the <strong>brightness and intensity of the image is reduced</strong> (the image appears fainter).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Yes, the lens produces a complete image of the object</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Uncovered half refracts light to form complete image, but image intensity/brightness is halved</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An object 5 cm in length is held 25 cm away from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. Draw the ray diagram and find the position, size and the nature of the image formed.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Given Data:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Object height, $h_1 = +5\\text{ cm}$</li>\n      <li>Object distance, $u = -25\\text{ cm}$</li>\n      <li>Focal length of converging lens, $f = +10\\text{ cm}$</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Lens Formula:</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\implies \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} + \\frac{1}{u}$$\n    $$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{-25} = \\frac{5 - 2}{50} = \\frac{3}{50}$$\n    $$v = \\frac{50}{3} = \\mathbf{+16.67\\text{ cm}}$$\n    The image is formed at a distance of <strong>$16.67\\text{ cm}$ on the other side of the lens</strong>.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Size and Nature of Image:</strong>\n    $$m = \\frac{h_2}{h_1} = \\frac{v}{u} \\implies h_2 = h_1 \\left(\\frac{v}{u}\\right)$$\n    $$h_2 = 5 \\times \\left(\\frac{50/3}{-25}\\right) = 5 \\times \\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = -\\frac{10}{3} = \\mathbf{-3.33\\text{ cm}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The negative sign indicates that the image is <strong>Real and Inverted</strong>, diminished in size ($3.33\\text{ cm}$ tall).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Using lens formula to find v = +16.67 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Magnification formula giving h2 = -3.33 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Nature: Real, inverted, diminished, formed on opposite side</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A concave lens of focal length 15 cm forms an image 10 cm from the lens. How far is the object placed from the lens? Draw the ray diagram.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Given Data:</strong> A concave lens always forms a virtual, erect image on the same side as the object:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Focal length, $f = -15\\text{ cm}$</li>\n      <li>Image distance, $v = -10\\text{ cm}$</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Lens Formula:</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\implies \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{f}$$\n    $$\\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{-10} - \\frac{1}{-15} = -\\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{15} = \\frac{-3 + 2}{30} = -\\frac{1}{30}$$\n    $$u = \\mathbf{-30\\text{ cm}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The object is placed at a distance of <strong>$30\\text{ cm}$ in front of the concave lens</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Assigning proper signs: f = -15 cm, v = -10 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lens formula substitution and solving u = -30 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ray diagram showing virtual erect diminished image at 10 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An object is placed at a distance of 10 cm from a convex mirror of focal length 15 cm. Find the position and nature of the image.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Given Data:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Object distance, $u = -10\\text{ cm}$</li>\n      <li>Focal length of convex mirror, $f = +15\\text{ cm}$</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Mirror Formula:</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} \\implies \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u}$$\n    $$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{15} - \\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\right) = \\frac{1}{15} + \\frac{1}{10} = \\frac{2 + 3}{30} = \\frac{5}{30} = \\frac{1}{6}$$\n    $$v = \\mathbf{+6\\text{ cm}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Magnification:</strong>\n    $$m = -\\frac{v}{u} = -\\frac{+6}{-10} = \\mathbf{+0.6}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The image is formed at a distance of <strong>$6\\text{ cm}$ behind the mirror</strong>. It is <strong>Virtual, Erect, and Diminished</strong> ($0.6$ times object size).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mirror formula calculation: v = +6 cm behind the mirror</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Magnification m = +0.6; Nature is virtual, erect, and diminished</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q27\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q27')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The magnification produced by a plane mirror is +1. What does this mean?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Physical Interpretation:</strong></p>\n<p>Magnification formula is $m = \\frac{h_2}{h_1} = -\\frac{v}{u}$. Here, $m = +1$ conveys two important physical facts:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Magnitude ($|m| = 1$):</strong> The height of the image ($h_2$) is exactly equal to the height of the object ($h_1$). There is no size distortion.</li>\n  <li><strong>Positive Sign ($+$):</strong> The positive sign indicates that the image is <strong>Virtual and Erect</strong>, formed behind the mirror at the exact same distance as the object is in front of the mirror ($v = -u$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Magnitude 1 indicates image size is equal to object size (h2 = h1)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Positive sign indicates image is virtual and erect</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q28\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q28')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q14</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An object 5.0 cm in length is placed at a distance of 20 cm in front of a convex mirror of radius of curvature 30 cm. Find the position of the image, its nature and size.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Given Data:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>$h_1 = +5.0\\text{ cm}$</li>\n      <li>$u = -20\\text{ cm}$</li>\n      <li>Radius of curvature $R = +30\\text{ cm} \\implies f = R/2 = +15\\text{ cm}$</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Position of Image ($v$):</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{15} - \\left(-\\frac{1}{20}\\right) = \\frac{1}{15} + \\frac{1}{20} = \\frac{4 + 3}{60} = \\frac{7}{60}$$\n    $$v = \\frac{60}{7} = \\mathbf{+8.57\\text{ cm}}$$\n    The image is formed <strong>$8.57\\text{ cm}$ behind the convex mirror</strong>.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Size of Image ($h_2$):</strong>\n    $$m = -\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h_2}{h_1} \\implies h_2 = -\\frac{v \\times h_1}{u}$$\n    $$h_2 = -\\frac{(60/7) \\times 5}{-20} = +\\frac{300}{140} = \\frac{15}{7} = \\mathbf{+2.14\\text{ cm}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Nature:</strong> The image is <strong>Virtual, Erect, and Diminished</strong> ($2.14\\text{ cm}$ height).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculating focal length f = +15 cm and mirror formula to get v = +8.57 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculating image height h2 = +2.14 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Nature: Virtual, erect, and diminished formed behind the mirror</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q29\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q29')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q15</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An object of size 7.0 cm is placed at 27 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 18 cm. At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed, so that a sharp focussed image can be obtained? Find the size and the nature of the image.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Given Data:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>$h_1 = +7.0\\text{ cm}$</li>\n      <li>$u = -27\\text{ cm}$</li>\n      <li>$f = -18\\text{ cm}$ (concave mirror)</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Screen Distance ($v$):</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{-18} - \\left(\\frac{1}{-27}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{18} + \\frac{1}{27} = \\frac{-3 + 2}{54} = -\\frac{1}{54}$$\n    $$v = \\mathbf{-54\\text{ cm}}$$\n    Therefore, the screen should be placed at a distance of <strong>$54\\text{ cm}$ in front of the mirror</strong>.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Image Size ($h_2$):</strong>\n    $$h_2 = -\\frac{v \\times h_1}{u} = -\\frac{(-54) \\times 7.0}{-27} = \\mathbf{-14.0\\text{ cm}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Nature:</strong> The image is <strong>Real, Inverted, and Enlarged</strong> (twice the object height).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mirror formula yielding screen distance v = -54 cm in front of mirror</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Image height calculation h2 = -14.0 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Nature: Real, inverted, and magnified</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q30\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q30')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q16</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Find the focal length of a lens of power -2.0 D. What type of lens is this?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n$$P = \\frac{1}{f\\text{ (in metres)}} \\implies f = \\frac{1}{P}$$\n$$f = \\frac{1}{-2.0\\text{ D}} = -0.5\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{-50\\text{ cm}}$$\n<p><strong>Lens Type:</strong> Because the focal length and power are negative, this is a <strong>Concave lens (Diverging lens)</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculation: f = 1/P = -0.5 m = -50 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying lens as Concave lens (diverging lens)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q31\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q31')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q17</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A doctor has prescribed a corrective lens of power +1.5 D. Find the focal length of the lens. Is the prescribed lens diverging or converging?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n$$f = \\frac{1}{P} = \\frac{1}{+1.5\\text{ D}} = \\frac{10}{15}\\text{ m} = +\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{+0.67\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{+66.7\\text{ cm}}$$\n<p><strong>Nature of Lens:</strong> Since the focal length and power are positive, the prescribed corrective lens is a <strong>Convex lens (Converging lens)</strong> used to correct hypermetropia (long-sightedness).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Focal length calculation: f = +0.67 m (+66.7 cm)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying lens as Converging lens (Convex lens)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 9</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Lens Combination & Optical Instruments</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">An optician places two thin lenses in contact: Lens 1 of focal length $+20\\text{ cm}$ and Lens 2 of focal length $-50\\text{ cm}$. (i) Calculate the net power of the combination. (ii) What is the effective focal length of the combination? (iii) Does the combination behave as a converging or diverging system?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Net Power:</strong>\n    $$P_1 = \\frac{1}{+0.20\\text{ m}} = +5\\text{ D}, \\quad P_2 = \\frac{1}{-0.50\\text{ m}} = -2\\text{ D}$$\n    $$P_{\\text{net}} = P_1 + P_2 = +5\\text{ D} - 2\\text{ D} = \\mathbf{+3\\text{ D}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Effective Focal Length:</strong>\n    $$F = \\frac{1}{P_{\\text{net}}} = \\frac{1}{+3}\\text{ m} = +0.333\\text{ m} = \\mathbf{+33.3\\text{ cm}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) System Behavior:</strong> Because the net power ($+3\\text{ D}$) and effective focal length ($+33.3\\text{ cm}$) are positive, the combination behaves as a <strong>Converging lens system</strong>.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(8)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(10)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "10": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch10\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">10</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 10: The Human Eye and the Colourful World</h2>\n      <p>Structure of Human Eye, Power of Accommodation, Myopia & Hypermetropia Correction, Refraction through Prism, Atmospheric Refraction & Scattering — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 176) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 13)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 176 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is meant by power of accommodation of the eye?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>The ability of the human eye lens to adjust its focal length with the aid of the <strong>ciliary muscles</strong> so as to focus clear, sharp images of objects situated at varying distances (from 25 cm to infinity) onto the retina is called the <strong>power of accommodation</strong> of the eye.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>When viewing distant objects, ciliary muscles relax, the eye lens becomes thin, and focal length increases to maximum.</li>\n  <li>When viewing nearby objects, ciliary muscles contract, the crystalline lens becomes thicker and more convex, decreasing its focal length.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ability of eye lens to adjust focal length via ciliary muscles to focus objects at various distances</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mechanism: Relaxation for distant objects vs contraction/thickening for near objects</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 176 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A person with a myopic eye cannot see objects beyond 1.2 m distinctly. What should be the type of the corrective lens used to restore proper vision?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation and Prescription:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Defect:</strong> The person is suffering from <strong>Myopia (Near-sightedness)</strong>. The far point of the eye has shifted from infinity to $d = 1.2\\text{ m}$.</li>\n  <li>To see distant objects at infinity ($u = -\\infty$), the corrective lens must form a virtual image of the distant object at the person's defective far point ($v = -1.2\\text{ m}$):\n    $$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{-1.2\\text{ m}} - \\frac{1}{-\\infty} = -\\frac{1}{1.2\\text{ m}}$$\n    $$f = \\mathbf{-1.2\\text{ m}}$$\n    $$P = \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{-1.2\\text{ m}} = -0.83\\text{ D}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Lens Type:</strong> A <strong>Concave lens (Diverging lens)</strong> of focal length $1.2\\text{ m}$ (Power $-0.83\\text{ D}$) is required.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lens type: Concave lens (diverging lens)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculation: f = -1.2 m, Power P = -0.83 D</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 176 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the near point and far point of the human eye with normal vision?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reference Points for Normal Vision:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Near Point (Least Distance of Distinct Vision, $D$):</strong> The minimum distance at which an object can be seen most distinctly and comfortably without eye strain is <strong>$25\\text{ cm}$</strong> for a normal young adult eye.</li>\n  <li><strong>Far Point:</strong> The farthest point up to which the eye can see objects clearly is <strong>Infinity ($\\infty$)</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Near point = 25 cm (least distance of distinct vision)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Far point = Infinity (infinite distance)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 176 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A student has difficulty reading the blackboard while sitting in the last row. What could be the defect the child is suffering from? How can it be corrected?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Defect Diagnosis & Remedy:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Defect:</strong> The student is suffering from <strong>Myopia (Short-sightedness / Near-sightedness)</strong> because they can see near objects clearly but have difficulty seeing distant objects distinctly (the blackboard image is formed in front of the retina).</li>\n  <li><strong>Correction:</strong> It can be corrected by wearing spectacles fitted with a <strong>Concave lens (Diverging lens)</strong> of suitable focal length and power. The concave lens diverges incoming parallel light rays so that they appear to originate from the myopic far point, bringing the focused image exactly onto the retina.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Diagnosis: Myopia (short-sightedness)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Remedy: Spectacles with concave lenses of appropriate focal length/power</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The human eye can focus objects at different distances by adjusting the focal length of the eye lens. This is due to: (a) presbyopia (b) accommodation (c) near-sightedness (d) far-sightedness.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) accommodation</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> The physiological process of altering the curvature and focal length of the crystalline lens using ciliary muscles is termed accommodation.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) accommodation with definition</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The human eye forms the image of an object at its: (a) cornea (b) iris (c) pupil (d) retina.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) retina</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> The retina is the light-sensitive screen lining the back of the eye containing millions of photoreceptor cells (rods and cones) that generate electrical signals upon light reception.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) retina</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The least distance of distinct vision for a young adult with normal vision is about: (a) 25 m (b) 2.5 cm (c) 25 cm (d) 2.5 m.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) 25 cm</strong></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) 25 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The change in focal length of an eye lens is caused by the action of the: (a) pupil (b) retina (c) ciliary muscles (d) iris.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) ciliary muscles</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Ciliary muscles contract or relax to alter the radius of curvature and thickness of the flexible crystalline lens, modifying its focal length.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) ciliary muscles</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A person needs a lens of power -5.5 dioptres for correcting his distant vision. For correcting his near vision he needs a lens of power +1.5 dioptre. What is the focal length of the lens required for correcting (i) distant vision, and (ii) near vision?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) For Distant Vision:</strong>\n    $$P_1 = -5.5\\text{ D}$$\n    $$f_1 = \\frac{1}{P_1} = \\frac{1}{-5.5}\\text{ m} = -\\frac{10}{55}\\text{ m} = -\\frac{2}{11}\\text{ m} \\approx \\mathbf{-0.182\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{-18.2\\text{ cm}}$$\n    (Concave lens of focal length $-18.2\\text{ cm}$).\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) For Near Vision:</strong>\n    $$P_2 = +1.5\\text{ D}$$\n    $$f_2 = \\frac{1}{P_2} = \\frac{1}{+1.5}\\text{ m} = +\\frac{10}{15}\\text{ m} = +\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ m} \\approx \\mathbf{+0.667\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{+66.7\\text{ cm}}$$\n    (Convex lens of focal length $+66.7\\text{ cm}$).\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Distant vision: f1 = -0.182 m = -18.2 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) Near vision: f2 = +0.667 m = +66.7 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The far point of a myopic person is 80 cm in front of the eye. What is the nature and power of the lens required to correct the problem?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>For viewing distant objects at infinity ($u = -\\infty$), the corrective lens must form a virtual image at the person's defective far point ($v = -80\\text{ cm} = -0.8\\text{ m}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Lens Formula:</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{-0.8\\text{ m}} - \\frac{1}{-\\infty} = -\\frac{1}{0.8\\text{ m}}$$\n    $$f = \\mathbf{-0.8\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{-80\\text{ cm}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Power of Lens:</strong>\n    $$P = \\frac{1}{f\\text{ (m)}} = \\frac{1}{-0.8\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{-1.25\\text{ D}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Nature:</strong> A <strong>Concave lens</strong> of power <strong>$-1.25\\text{ Dioptre}$</strong> is required.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Focal length calculation f = -80 cm (-0.8 m)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Power calculation P = -1.25 D and Concave lens nature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Make a diagram to show how hypermetropia is corrected. The near point of a hypermetropic eye is 1 m. What is the power of the lens required to correct this defect? Assume that the near point of the normal eye is 25 cm.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Optical Situation:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Standard near point (object position), $u = -25\\text{ cm} = -0.25\\text{ m}$.</li>\n      <li>Defective near point (image position), $v = -1\\text{ m} = -100\\text{ cm}$.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Lens Formula:</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{-100} - \\left(-\\frac{1}{25}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{100} + \\frac{1}{25} = \\frac{-1 + 4}{100} = \\frac{3}{100}\\text{ cm}^{-1}$$\n    $$f = +\\frac{100}{3}\\text{ cm} = +\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ m}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Power of Corrective Lens:</strong>\n    $$P = \\frac{1}{f\\text{ (m)}} = \\frac{1}{+1/3\\text{ m}} = \\mathbf{+3.0\\text{ D}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Remedy:</strong> A <strong>Convex lens (converging lens)</strong> of power <strong>$+3.0\\text{ D}$</strong> is required. It converges incoming rays from $25\\text{ cm}$ so they appear to diverge from the near point at $1\\text{ m}$.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct identification: u = -25 cm, v = -100 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculating f = +1/3 m and Power P = +3.0 D</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Convex lens specification and correction diagram description</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is a normal eye not able to see clearly the objects placed closer than 25 cm?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>To see nearby objects distinctly, the ciliary muscles must contract to increase the curvature and thickness of the crystalline lens, shortening its focal length.</li>\n  <li>However, the ciliary muscles have a physiological limit to which they can contract. The lens cannot be compressed or curved beyond a maximum limit.</li>\n  <li>If an object is held closer than $25\\text{ cm}$, the required sharp convergence cannot be achieved by the eye lens, causing the light rays to focus <strong>behind the retina</strong>, resulting in blurred vision and severe ocular muscle strain.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ciliary muscles cannot contract beyond a physiological limit</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Focal length cannot decrease further; image falls behind retina causing blurred vision</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What happens to the image distance in the eye when we increase the distance of an object from the eye?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>image distance in the eye remains constant</strong> (it always equals the fixed distance between the eye lens and the retina, approximately $2.5\\text{ cm}$).</p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> When the object distance ($u$) increases, the ciliary muscles relax, making the crystalline lens thinner. This increases the focal length of the eye lens by accommodation such that the real and inverted image is always formed precisely on the fixed retina.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Image distance remains unchanged/constant (distance from lens to retina)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Accommodation adjusts lens focal length so image always focuses on the retina</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do stars twinkle?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Atmospheric Refraction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Stars are located at vast astronomical distances from Earth and act as point-sized sources of light.</li>\n  <li>The Earth's atmosphere consists of continuously churning layers of air with differing physical temperatures, densities, and refractive indices ($n$).</li>\n  <li>As starlight traverses these dynamically turbulent atmospheric layers, it undergoes continuous and fluctuating <strong>atmospheric refraction</strong>.</li>\n  <li>This causes the apparent position of the star and the quantity of starlight reaching the observer's pupil to fluctuate rapidly from moment to moment. Sometimes more light enters our eye (star appears bright), and sometimes less light enters (star appears faint), producing the optical illusion called <strong>twinkling</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stars are point-sized distant sources subject to atmospheric refraction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Turbulent air layers cause continuous fluctuations in apparent position and light intensity entering eye</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain why the planets do not twinkle.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason Why Planets Do Not Twinkle:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Planets are situated much closer to Earth than distant stars. Hence, they do not appear as point-sized sources, but rather as <strong>extended optical sources</strong> (a dense collection of millions of individual point sources of light).</li>\n  <li>Although light rays originating from individual points on a planet undergo atmospheric refraction and intensity fluctuations, the bright and faint variations from different points average out across the entire disc:\n    $$\\sum \\Delta I_{\\text{fluctuations}} \\approx 0$$\n  </li>\n  <li>The total amount of light entering our eye from all points combined remains constant over time. Therefore, planets shine with steady light without twinkling.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Planets are close extended sources comprising a large collection of point sources</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fluctuations from different point sources cancel out on average, nullifying twinkling</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the Sun appear reddish early in the morning and at sunset?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Phenomenon of Rayleigh Scattering:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Early in the morning (at sunrise) and late in the evening (at sunset), the Sun is situated near the horizon. Sunlight must travel through a <strong>much thicker layer of the atmosphere and a greater distance</strong> to reach the observer compared to noon.</li>\n  <li>According to Rayleigh's scattering law, the intensity of scattered light is inversely proportional to the fourth power of wavelength:\n    $$I_{\\text{scattered}} \\propto \\frac{1}{\\lambda^4}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Shorter wavelengths (blue, violet) are scattered away strongly in all directions by atmospheric molecules.</li>\n  <li>The least-scattered <strong>longer wavelengths (red and orange)</strong> pass straight through the atmosphere and reach our eyes, causing the Sun and surrounding horizon to appear reddish.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sun near horizon means light travels longer atmospheric distance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rayleigh scattering scatters short blue wavelengths; long red wavelengths reach observer</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the sky appear dark instead of blue to an astronaut?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The blue appearance of the sky on Earth is caused by the <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong> of shorter blue wavelengths of sunlight by tiny gas molecules ($N_2, O_2$) present in the atmosphere.</li>\n  <li>In outer space, there is a total <strong>absence of an atmosphere</strong> (vacuum).</li>\n  <li>In the absence of air molecules and particulate matter, no scattering of sunlight takes place. Sunlight travels in straight lines without illumination of surrounding space. Hence, to an astronaut or in space, the sky appears completely pitch <strong>dark/black</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">No atmosphere or air particles exist at high altitudes / outer space</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Absence of scattering of light leaves surrounding space dark/black</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 10</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Atmospheric Phenomena & Rainbow Formation</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A rainbow is a natural spectrum appearing in the sky after a rain shower. (i) In which direction with respect to the Sun does a rainbow always form? (ii) What acts as tiny prisms in the atmosphere? (iii) Name the three sequential optical phenomena that occur inside a water droplet during rainbow formation.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Direction:</strong> A rainbow is always formed in a direction <strong>opposite to that of the Sun</strong> (the observer's back must face the Sun).</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Tiny Prisms:</strong> Suspended <strong>spherical raindrops</strong> in the atmosphere act as miniature prisms.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Three Optical Phenomena:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>1. <strong>Refraction and Dispersion:</strong> Incident sunlight enters the front surface of the raindrop, refracting and splitting into constituent spectral colours (VIBGYOR).</li>\n      <li>2. <strong>Internal Reflection:</strong> The dispersed rays strike the back inner wall of the droplet and undergo internal reflection.</li>\n      <li>3. <strong>Refraction:</strong> The reflected coloured rays refract again as they emerge out of the front surface of the droplet towards the observer's eye.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(9)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(11)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "11": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch11\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">11</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 11: Electricity</h2>\n      <p>Electric Current & Potential Difference, Ohm's Law & Factors Affecting Resistance, Series & Parallel Resistors, Joule's Heating & Electrical Power — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 187, 189, 198, 203, 206, 209) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 18)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 187 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does an electric circuit mean?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>An electric circuit is a <strong>continuous and closed conducting path</strong> through which an electric current can flow. It typically comprises electrical components such as a voltage source (cell/battery), conducting connecting wires, loads (bulb/resistor), and a switch or key.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Continuous and closed conducting path for electric current flow</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 187 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Define the unit of current.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>The SI unit of electric current is the <strong>Ampere (A)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>1 Ampere</strong> is defined as the flow of electric current through a cross-section of a conductor when <strong>one coulomb of electric charge</strong> passes through it in <strong>one second</strong>:</p>\n$$1\\text{ A} = \\frac{1\\text{ Coulomb}}{1\\text{ Second}} = 1\\text{ C/s}$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">1 Ampere = 1 Coulomb per second (1 A = 1 C/s)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 187 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Calculate the number of electrons constituting one coulomb of charge.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>According to the quantization of electric charge:</p>\n$$Q = n e \\implies n = \\frac{Q}{e}$$\n<p>Given: Total charge $Q = 1\\text{ C}$, elementary charge of an electron $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\text{ C}$.</p>\n$$n = \\frac{1}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}} = \\frac{10^{19}}{1.6} = \\mathbf{6.25 \\times 10^{18}\\text{ electrons}}$$\n<p>Therefore, <strong>$6.25 \\times 10^{18}$ electrons</strong> constitute one coulomb of negative charge.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula: n = Q / e</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct value: n = 6.25 x 10^18 electrons</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 189 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name a device that helps to maintain a potential difference across a conductor.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>A <strong>Battery</strong> (or an electric cell) helps to maintain a steady potential difference across the terminals of a conductor by converting stored chemical energy into electrical energy.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electric cell or battery</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 189 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is meant by saying that the potential difference between two points is 1 V?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>The potential difference between two points is <strong>1 Volt (1 V)</strong> if <strong>one Joule of work</strong> is done in moving a positive electric charge of <strong>one Coulomb</strong> from one point to the other across an electric field:</p>\n$$1\\text{ Volt} = \\frac{1\\text{ Joule}}{1\\text{ Coulomb}} = 1\\text{ J/C}$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">1 Volt = 1 Joule of work done per 1 Coulomb of charge moved</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 189 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How much energy is given to each coulomb of charge passing through a 6 V battery?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>The relationship between work done (energy transferred $W$), charge ($Q$), and potential difference ($V$) is:</p>\n$$W = Q \\times V$$\n<p>Given: Charge $Q = 1\\text{ C}$, Potential difference $V = 6\\text{ V}$.</p>\n$$W = 1\\text{ C} \\times 6\\text{ V} = \\mathbf{6\\text{ Joules}}$$\n<p>Therefore, <strong>6 Joules</strong> of energy is given to each coulomb of charge.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula: W = Q x V</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculation: W = 1 C x 6 V = 6 J</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 198 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">On what factors does the resistance of a conductor depend?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Factors Determining Electrical Resistance ($R$):</strong></p>\n<p>The resistance of a uniform metallic conductor depends on:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Length ($l$):</strong> Resistance is directly proportional to conductor length ($R \\propto l$). Doubling the length doubles resistance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Area of Cross-section ($A$):</strong> Resistance is inversely proportional to cross-sectional area ($R \\propto 1/A$). Thicker wires have less resistance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Nature of Material (Resistivity, $\\rho$):</strong> Characterized by the specific electrical resistivity $\\rho$ of the material.</li>\n  <li><strong>Temperature ($T$):</strong> For pure metallic conductors, resistance increases linearly with rising temperature.</li>\n</ol>\n$$R = \\rho \\frac{l}{A}$$\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Directly proportional to length (l) and inversely proportional to area of cross-section (A)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Nature of material (resistivity rho) and temperature with formula R = rho*l/A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 198 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Will current flow more easily through a thick wire or a thin wire of the same material, when connected to the same source? Why?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Answer & Scientific Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Current will flow <strong>more easily through the thick wire</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Resistance of a conductor is inversely proportional to its cross-sectional area ($R \\propto 1/A$). A thick wire possesses a larger cross-sectional area ($A_{\\text{thick}} > A_{\\text{thin}}$), and therefore has significantly less electrical resistance ($R_{\\text{thick}} < R_{\\text{thin}}$). By Ohm's law ($I = V/R$), for the same applied potential difference, smaller resistance permits a larger current to flow easily with less internal collision.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Flows more easily through thick wire</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Larger cross-sectional area implies lower resistance (R proportional to 1/A), allowing more current</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 198 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Let the resistance of an electrical component remains constant while the potential difference across the two ends of the component decreases to half of its former value. What change will occur in the current through it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>According to Ohm's law, electric current is directly proportional to potential difference across a constant resistance:</p>\n$$I = \\frac{V}{R}$$\n<p>If the new potential difference is $V' = \\frac{V}{2}$ and resistance $R$ remains constant:</p>\n$$I' = \\frac{V'}{R} = \\frac{V/2}{R} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{V}{R}\\right) = \\frac{I}{2}$$\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The current flowing through the component will also <strong>decrease to half of its initial value</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ohm's law I = V/R with constant resistance shows I proportional to V</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Halving potential difference halves the electric current (I' = I / 2)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 198 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why are coils of electric toasters and electric irons made of an alloy rather than a pure metal?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reasons:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>High Electrical Resistivity:</strong> Alloys (such as <strong>Nichrome</strong>, an alloy of Ni, Cr, Mn, and Fe) have significantly higher resistivity ($\\approx 100 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega\\cdot\\text{m}$) than pure metals like copper or aluminium. According to Joule's heating law ($H = I^2 R t$), higher resistance generates intense heat.</li>\n  <li><strong>High Melting Point and Resistance to Oxidation:</strong> Alloys do not easily oxidise (burn or melt) even when heated red-hot to temperatures exceeding $900^\\circ\\text{C}$ in air, ensuring long operating life.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">High electrical resistivity generates large Joule heating</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">High melting point and does not oxidise (burn) at red-hot temperatures</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 198 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Use data of electrical resistivity of substances to answer: (a) Which among iron and mercury is a better conductor? (b) Which material is the best conductor?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Resistivity Data:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Resistivity of Iron ($\\text{Fe}$): $\\rho_{\\text{iron}} = 10.0 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega\\cdot\\text{m}$</li>\n  <li>Resistivity of Mercury ($\\text{Hg}$): $\\rho_{\\text{mercury}} = 94.0 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega\\cdot\\text{m}$</li>\n  <li>Resistivity of Silver ($\\text{Ag}$): $\\rho_{\\text{silver}} = 1.60 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega\\cdot\\text{m}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Answers:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Iron vs Mercury:</strong> <strong>Iron</strong> is a better conductor because its electrical resistivity is about 9.4 times lower than that of mercury (lower $\\rho$ allows higher electrical conductivity).</li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Best Conductor:</strong> <strong>Silver ($\\text{Ag}$)</strong> is the best electrical conductor because it possesses the lowest resistivity among all known materials ($1.60 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega\\cdot\\text{m}$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(a) Iron is a better conductor (lower resistivity than mercury)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(b) Silver is the best electrical conductor (lowest resistivity)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 203 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Draw a schematic diagram of a circuit consisting of a battery of three cells of 2 V each, a 5 ohm resistor, an 8 ohm resistor, and a 12 ohm resistor, and a plug key, all connected in series.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Circuit Specification:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Battery:</strong> 3 cells of $2\\text{ V}$ each connected in series provide a total electromotive force of $V = 3 \\times 2 = \\mathbf{6\\text{ V}}$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resistors in Series:</strong> Resistors $R_1 = 5\\ \\Omega$, $R_2 = 8\\ \\Omega$, and $R_3 = 12\\ \\Omega$ are connected end-to-end in a single continuous loop.</li>\n  <li><strong>Plug Key:</strong> Connected in series to open or close the circuit.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct series combination of 3 cells giving total 6 V</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">End-to-end series connection of 5 ohm, 8 ohm, 12 ohm resistors and plug key</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 203 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Redraw the circuit of Question 1, putting in an ammeter to measure the current through the resistors and a voltmeter to measure the potential difference across the 12 ohm resistor. What would be the readings in the ammeter and the voltmeter?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Circuit Calculation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Total Series Resistance:</strong>\n    $$R_{\\text{eq}} = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 = 5 + 8 + 12 = \\mathbf{25\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Ammeter Reading (Circuit Current, $I$):</strong>\n    The ammeter is connected in series. By Ohm's law:\n    $$I = \\frac{V}{R_{\\text{eq}}} = \\frac{6\\text{ V}}{25\\ \\Omega} = \\mathbf{0.24\\text{ A}}$$\n    Therefore, the ammeter reading is <strong>$0.24\\text{ A}$</strong>.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Voltmeter Reading ($V_{12}$ across 12 ohm resistor):</strong>\n    The voltmeter is connected in parallel across the $12\\ \\Omega$ resistor.\n    $$V_{12} = I \\times R_3 = 0.24\\text{ A} \\times 12\\ \\Omega = \\mathbf{2.88\\text{ V}}$$\n    Therefore, the voltmeter reading is <strong>$2.88\\text{ V}$</strong>.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculation of equivalent resistance: Req = 5 + 8 + 12 = 25 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ammeter reading: I = V / Req = 6 / 25 = 0.24 A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Voltmeter reading: V = I * R3 = 0.24 * 12 = 2.88 V</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 206 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Judge the equivalent resistance when the following are connected in parallel – (a) 1 ohm and $10^6$ ohm, (b) 1 ohm and $10^3$ ohm and $10^6$ ohm.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Analysis of Parallel Combinations:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fundamental Rule:</strong> The equivalent resistance of any parallel combination is always strictly <strong>less than the lowest individual resistance</strong> in the network ($R_p < R_{\\text{min}}$).</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) $1\\ \\Omega$ and $10^6\\ \\Omega$ in parallel:</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{10^6} = \\frac{10^6 + 1}{10^6} \\approx 1 \\implies R_p = \\frac{10^6}{1000001} \\approx \\mathbf{0.999999\\ \\Omega} \\quad (\\mathbf{< 1\\ \\Omega})$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) $1\\ \\Omega$, $10^3\\ \\Omega$, and $10^6\\ \\Omega$ in parallel:</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{1000} + \\frac{1}{10^6} = \\frac{1000000 + 1000 + 1}{10^6} = \\frac{1001001}{10^6}$$\n    $$R_p = \\frac{10^6}{1001001} \\approx \\mathbf{0.999\\ \\Omega} \\quad (\\mathbf{< 1\\ \\Omega})$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(a) Equivalent resistance is slightly less than 1 ohm (~0.999999 ohm)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(b) Equivalent resistance is slightly less than 1 ohm (~0.999 ohm)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 206 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An electric lamp of 100 ohm, a toaster of resistance 50 ohm, and a water filter of resistance 500 ohm are connected in parallel to a 220 V source. What is the resistance of an electric iron connected to the same source that takes as much current as all three appliances, and what is the current through it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Equivalent Resistance of Three Parallel Appliances ($R_p$):</strong>\n    $$\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{R_1} + \\frac{1}{R_2} + \\frac{1}{R_3} = \\frac{1}{100} + \\frac{1}{50} + \\frac{1}{500}$$\n    $$\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{5 + 10 + 1}{500} = \\frac{16}{500}$$\n    $$R_p = \\frac{500}{16} = \\mathbf{31.25\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Total Current Drawn ($I$):</strong>\n    $$I = \\frac{V}{R_p} = \\frac{220\\text{ V}}{31.25\\ \\Omega} = \\mathbf{7.04\\text{ A}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Electric Iron:</strong> Since the electric iron takes as much current as all three appliances combined when connected to $220\\text{ V}$, its resistance must equal the equivalent resistance:\n    $$\\mathbf{R_{\\text{iron}} = 31.25\\ \\Omega}, \\quad \\mathbf{I_{\\text{iron}} = 7.04\\text{ A}}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Parallel formula 1/Rp = 1/100 + 1/50 + 1/500 = 16/500 yielding Rp = 31.25 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Current drawn I = 220 / 31.25 = 7.04 A; R_iron = 31.25 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 206 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the advantages of connecting electrical devices in parallel with the battery instead of connecting them in series?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Advantages of Parallel Circuit in Domestic Wiring:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Independent Operation:</strong> Each appliance gets its own independent on/off switch. If one appliance faults or is turned off, the other appliances continue to operate normally without interruption. In a series circuit, any break stops current in all devices.</li>\n  <li><strong>Full Mains Voltage across Every Device:</strong> In parallel, each appliance receives the full rated line voltage ($220\\text{ V}$), allowing devices to operate at full intended power. In series, voltage divides among devices, causing bulbs to glow dimly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Different Current Requirements Satisfied:</strong> Devices with widely different power ratings (e.g., $1000\\text{ W}$ heater requiring $4.5\\text{ A}$ vs $40\\text{ W}$ lamp requiring $0.18\\text{ A}$) draw their required current according to their individual resistance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Low Total Circuit Resistance:</strong> The overall equivalent resistance of the domestic wiring is low, preventing excessive overall power dissipation.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Appliances operate independently with separate switches (break in one does not affect others)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Each appliance receives full line voltage (220 V)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Each device draws its independently required current according to power rating</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 206 • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How can three resistors of resistances 2 ohm, 3 ohm, and 6 ohm be connected to give a total resistance of (a) 4 ohm, (b) 1 ohm?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Circuit Configuration:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) To obtain $4\\ \\Omega$:</strong>\n    Connect the $3\\ \\Omega$ and $6\\ \\Omega$ resistors in <strong>parallel</strong>, and connect this parallel group in <strong>series with the $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor</strong>:\n    $$R_p = \\frac{3 \\times 6}{3 + 6} = \\frac{18}{9} = 2\\ \\Omega$$\n    $$R_{\\text{total}} = 2\\ \\Omega + R_p = 2 + 2 = \\mathbf{4\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) To obtain $1\\ \\Omega$:</strong>\n    Connect all three resistors ($2\\ \\Omega, 3\\ \\Omega, 6\\ \\Omega$) in <strong>parallel</strong>:\n    $$\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3 + 2 + 1}{6} = \\frac{6}{6} = 1\\ \\Omega^{-1}$$\n    $$R_p = \\mathbf{1\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(a) 3 ohm and 6 ohm in parallel (giving 2 ohm) in series with 2 ohm gives 4 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(b) All three resistors in parallel: 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 6/6 gives 1 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 206 • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is (a) the highest, (b) the lowest total resistance that can be secured by combinations of four coils of resistance 4 ohm, 8 ohm, 12 ohm, 24 ohm?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Highest Resistance (Series Combination):</strong>\n    Connecting all four coils in series yields maximum equivalent resistance:\n    $$R_{\\text{max}} = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + R_4 = 4 + 8 + 12 + 24 = \\mathbf{48\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Lowest Resistance (Parallel Combination):</strong>\n    Connecting all four coils in parallel yields minimum equivalent resistance:\n    $$\\frac{1}{R_{\\text{min}}} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} + \\frac{1}{12} + \\frac{1}{24} = \\frac{6 + 3 + 2 + 1}{24} = \\frac{12}{24} = \\frac{1}{2}$$\n    $$R_{\\text{min}} = \\mathbf{2\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(a) Highest: Series connection R = 4 + 8 + 12 + 24 = 48 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(b) Lowest: Parallel connection 1/R = 1/4 + 1/8 + 1/12 + 1/24 = 1/2 -> R = 2 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 209 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the cord of an electric heater not glow while the heating element does?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Application of Joule's Heating Law ($H = I^2 R t$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The connecting cord and the heating element are connected in series; therefore, the exact same electric current ($I$) flows through both.</li>\n  <li><strong>Connecting Cord:</strong> Made of thick copper or aluminium with very low electrical resistivity, hence its resistance ($R_{\\text{cord}}$) is negligible. Negligible heat is dissipated ($H \\propto R$), so it remains cool and does not glow.</li>\n  <li><strong>Heating Element:</strong> Made of high-resistance alloy (Nichrome) with high resistivity. Because its resistance ($R_{\\text{element}}$) is very large, an enormous quantity of Joule heat is developed, causing the element to heat up to glowing incandescence (red-hot, $>800^\\circ\\text{C}$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Current is identical in both series elements</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cord has low resistance (negligible heat); element has high resistance (intense heat glowing red-hot)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 209 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Compute the heat generated while transferring 96000 coulomb of charge in one hour through a potential difference of 50 V.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>Heat generated is equal to the electrical work done in moving charge $Q$ across potential difference $V$:</p>\n$$H = W = Q \\times V$$\n<p>Given: $Q = 96000\\text{ C}$, $V = 50\\text{ V}$.</p>\n$$H = 96000\\text{ C} \\times 50\\text{ V} = 4,800,000\\text{ J} = \\mathbf{4.8 \\times 10^6\\text{ Joules}}$$\n<p>The heat generated is <strong>$4.8 \\times 10^6\\text{ J}$</strong> (or $4.8\\text{ MJ}$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula: H = Q x V</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculation: 96000 x 50 = 4.8 x 10^6 J</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A piece of wire of resistance R is cut into five equal parts. These parts are then connected in parallel. If the equivalent resistance of this combination is R', then the ratio R/R' is: (a) 1/25 (b) 1/5 (c) 5 (d) 25.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) 25</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Derivation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>When a wire of resistance $R$ is cut into 5 equal parts, the resistance of each part becomes:\n    $$r = \\frac{R}{5}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>When these 5 equal resistors are connected in parallel, the equivalent resistance $R'$ is:\n    $$\\frac{1}{R'} = \\frac{1}{r} + \\frac{1}{r} + \\frac{1}{r} + \\frac{1}{r} + \\frac{1}{r} = \\frac{5}{r} = \\frac{5}{R/5} = \\frac{25}{R}$$\n    $$R' = \\frac{R}{25}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Therefore, the ratio is:\n    $$\\frac{R}{R'} = \\frac{R}{R/25} = \\mathbf{25}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Resistance of each piece is r = R / 5</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Parallel equivalent R' = R / 25 yielding ratio R / R' = 25 [Option (d)]</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following terms does not represent electrical power in a circuit? (a) $I^2 R$ (b) $I R^2$ (c) $V I$ (d) $V^2 / R$.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) $I R^2$</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Electric power is $P = V I$. Using Ohm's law ($V = I R$), $P = (I R) I = I^2 R$, or $P = V (V/R) = V^2 / R$. The formula $I R^2$ is dimensionally incorrect and does not represent power.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) IR^2 does not represent power</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An electric bulb is rated 220 V and 100 W. When it is operated on 110 V, the power consumed will be: (a) 100 W (b) 75 W (c) 50 W (d) 25 W.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) 25 W</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Calculation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Resistance of the bulb filament is constant:\n    $$R = \\frac{V^2}{P} = \\frac{220^2}{100} = \\frac{48400}{100} = 484\\ \\Omega$$\n  </li>\n  <li>When operated on $V' = 110\\text{ V}$, power consumed is:\n    $$P' = \\frac{(V')^2}{R} = \\frac{110^2}{484} = \\frac{12100}{484} = \\mathbf{25\\text{ W}}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Filament resistance R = V^2 / P = 484 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Power at 110 V: P' = 110^2 / 484 = 25 W [Option (d)]</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Two conducting wires of the same material and of equal lengths and equal diameters are first connected in series and then parallel in a circuit across the same potential difference. The ratio of heat produced in series and parallel combinations would be: (a) 1:2 (b) 2:1 (c) 1:4 (d) 4:1.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) 1:4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Derivation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Let each wire have resistance $R$.\n    <ul>\n      <li>Series resistance: $R_s = R + R = 2R$.</li>\n      <li>Parallel resistance: $R_p = \\frac{R \\times R}{R + R} = \\frac{R}{2}$.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Since both are connected across the <em>same potential difference ($V$)</em>, heat generated per unit time is $H = \\frac{V^2}{R_{\\text{eq}}} t$:\n    $$H_s = \\frac{V^2}{2R} t, \\quad H_p = \\frac{V^2}{R/2} t = \\frac{2 V^2}{R} t$$\n    $$\\frac{H_s}{H_p} = \\frac{V^2 / (2R)}{2V^2 / R} = \\frac{1}{4} = \\mathbf{1 : 4}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formulas Rs = 2R and Rp = R / 2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Heat ratio for same potential difference: Hs / Hp = Rp / Rs = (R/2) / (2R) = 1:4 [Option (c)]</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is a voltmeter connected in the circuit to measure the potential difference between two points?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>A voltmeter is always connected in <strong>parallel</strong> across the two points between which the potential difference is to be measured. It has an extremely high electrical resistance so that it draws negligible current from the main circuit.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Connected in parallel with high internal resistance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A copper wire has diameter 0.5 mm and resistivity of $1.6 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega\\cdot\\text{m}$. What will be the length of this wire to make its resistance $10\\ \\Omega$? How much does the resistance change if the diameter is doubled?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Calculation of Length ($l$):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Diameter $d = 0.5\\text{ mm} = 0.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ m} \\implies r = 0.25 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ m}$.</li>\n      <li>Cross-sectional area: $A = \\pi r^2 = 3.14 \\times (0.25 \\times 10^{-3})^2 = 1.9635 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ m}^2$.</li>\n      <li>Given: $R = 10\\ \\Omega$, $\\rho = 1.6 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\Omega\\cdot\\text{m}$.</li>\n    </ul>\n    $$R = \\rho \\frac{l}{A} \\implies l = \\frac{R A}{\\rho} = \\frac{10 \\times 1.9635 \\times 10^{-7}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-8}} = \\frac{1.9635 \\times 10^{-6}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-8}} = \\mathbf{122.7\\text{ metres}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Effect of Doubling Diameter:</strong>\n    $$R = \\frac{\\rho l}{\\pi (d/2)^2} = \\frac{4 \\rho l}{\\pi d^2} \\implies R \\propto \\frac{1}{d^2}$$\n    If diameter is doubled ($d' = 2d$), the new resistance becomes:\n    $$R' = \\frac{R}{2^2} = \\frac{R}{4} = \\frac{10\\ \\Omega}{4} = \\mathbf{2.5\\ \\Omega}$$\n    The resistance <strong>decreases to one-fourth</strong> of its original value ($2.5\\ \\Omega$).\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Area calculation: A = pi * r^2 = 1.9635 x 10^-7 m^2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Length formula and result: l = R*A / rho = 122.7 metres</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Doubling diameter reduces resistance to 1/4th (R' = 2.5 ohm)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q27\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q27')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">When a 12 V battery is connected across an unknown resistor, there is a current of 2.5 mA in the circuit. Find the value of the resistance of the resistor.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Potential difference, $V = 12\\text{ V}$</li>\n  <li>Current, $I = 2.5\\text{ mA} = 2.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ A}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By Ohm's law:</p>\n$$R = \\frac{V}{I} = \\frac{12\\text{ V}}{2.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ A}} = \\frac{12000}{2.5} = \\mathbf{4800\\ \\Omega} = \\mathbf{4.8\\text{ k}\\Omega}$$\n<p>The resistance of the unknown resistor is <strong>$4800\\ \\Omega$</strong> (or $4.8\\text{ k}\\Omega$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Converting current: I = 2.5 x 10^-3 A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ohm's law calculation: R = V / I = 4800 ohm (4.8 k-ohm)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q28\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q28')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A battery of 9 V is connected in series with resistors of 0.2 ohm, 0.3 ohm, 0.4 ohm, 0.5 ohm and 12 ohm, respectively. How much current would flow through the 12 ohm resistor?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Total Series Resistance ($R_s$):</strong>\n    $$R_s = 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.4 + 0.5 + 12 = \\mathbf{13.4\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Circuit Current ($I$):</strong>\n    $$I = \\frac{V}{R_s} = \\frac{9\\text{ V}}{13.4\\ \\Omega} \\approx \\mathbf{0.67\\text{ A}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Current through 12 ohm Resistor:</strong> In a series circuit, current is identical through every component. Therefore, the current flowing through the $12\\ \\Omega$ resistor is exactly <strong>$0.67\\text{ A}$</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Total series resistance: Rs = 13.4 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Current calculation I = 9 / 13.4 = 0.67 A flowing equally through 12 ohm resistor</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q29\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q29')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How many 176 ohm resistors (in parallel) are required to carry 5 A on a 220 V line?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Let $n$ resistors of resistance $R = 176\\ \\Omega$ be connected in parallel.</li>\n  <li>The equivalent resistance of $n$ identical parallel resistors is $R_p = \\frac{R}{n} = \\frac{176}{n}$.</li>\n  <li>By Ohm's law for $V = 220\\text{ V}$ and $I = 5\\text{ A}$:\n    $$R_p = \\frac{V}{I} = \\frac{220\\text{ V}}{5\\text{ A}} = 44\\ \\Omega$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Equating both expressions:\n    $$\\frac{176}{n} = 44 \\implies n = \\frac{176}{44} = \\mathbf{4}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Therefore, exactly <strong>4 resistors</strong> must be connected in parallel.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Required equivalent resistance: Rp = 220 / 5 = 44 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Solving n = 176 / 44 = 4 resistors in parallel</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q30\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q30')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Show how you would connect three resistors, each of resistance 6 ohm, so that the combination has a resistance of (i) 9 ohm, (ii) 4 ohm.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Circuit Connections:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) To get $9\\ \\Omega$:</strong>\n    Connect two $6\\ \\Omega$ resistors in <strong>parallel</strong>, and connect this parallel pair in <strong>series with the third $6\\ \\Omega$ resistor</strong>:\n    $$R_p = \\frac{6 \\times 6}{6 + 6} = 3\\ \\Omega$$\n    $$R_{\\text{total}} = 6\\ \\Omega + R_p = 6 + 3 = \\mathbf{9\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) To get $4\\ \\Omega$:</strong>\n    Connect two $6\\ \\Omega$ resistors in <strong>series</strong> (giving $6 + 6 = 12\\ \\Omega$), and connect this series pair in <strong>parallel with the third $6\\ \\Omega$ resistor</strong>:\n    $$R_s = 6 + 6 = 12\\ \\Omega$$\n    $$R_{\\text{total}} = \\frac{12 \\times 6}{12 + 6} = \\frac{72}{18} = \\mathbf{4\\ \\Omega}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Two 6 ohm resistors in parallel (3 ohm) in series with third 6 ohm gives 9 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) Two 6 ohm resistors in series (12 ohm) in parallel with third 6 ohm gives 4 ohm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q31\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q31')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Several electric bulbs designed to be used on a 220 V electric supply line, are rated 10 W. How many lamps can be connected in parallel with each other across the two wires of 220 V line if the maximum allowable current is 5 A?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Total maximum power allowable on the supply line:\n    $$P_{\\text{total}} = V \\times I_{\\text{max}} = 220\\text{ V} \\times 5\\text{ A} = 1100\\text{ W}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Power of each individual bulb: $P_{\\text{bulb}} = 10\\text{ W}$.</li>\n  <li>Maximum number of bulbs that can be connected in parallel:\n    $$n = \\frac{P_{\\text{total}}}{P_{\\text{bulb}}} = \\frac{1100\\text{ W}}{10\\text{ W}} = \\mathbf{110\\text{ bulbs}}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Total allowable power: P = V * I = 220 * 5 = 1100 W</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Number of bulbs: n = 1100 / 10 = 110 bulbs in parallel</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q32\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q32')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A hot plate of an electric oven connected to a 220 V line has two resistance coils A and B, each of 24 ohm resistance, which may be used separately, in series, or in parallel. What are the currents in the three cases?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Three Cases of Operation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Case 1: Used Separately (single $24\\ \\Omega$ coil):</strong>\n    $$I_1 = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{220\\text{ V}}{24\\ \\Omega} = \\mathbf{9.17\\text{ A}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Case 2: Used in Series ($R_s = 24 + 24 = 48\\ \\Omega$):</strong>\n    $$I_2 = \\frac{V}{R_s} = \\frac{220\\text{ V}}{48\\ \\Omega} = \\mathbf{4.58\\text{ A}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Case 3: Used in Parallel ($R_p = \\frac{24}{2} = 12\\ \\Omega$):</strong>\n    $$I_3 = \\frac{V}{R_p} = \\frac{220\\text{ V}}{12\\ \\Omega} = \\mathbf{18.33\\text{ A}}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Separately: I = 220 / 24 = 9.17 A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">In Series: I = 220 / 48 = 4.58 A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">In Parallel: I = 220 / 12 = 18.33 A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q33\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q33')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Compare the power used in the 2 ohm resistor in each of the following circuits: (i) a 6 V battery in series with 1 ohm and 2 ohm resistors, and (ii) a 4 V battery in parallel with 12 ohm and 2 ohm resistors.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Power in $2\\ \\Omega$ Resistor:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Circuit (i) [Series Circuit]:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>$R_s = 1 + 2 = 3\\ \\Omega$, $V = 6\\text{ V}$.</li>\n      <li>Circuit current: $I = \\frac{6\\text{ V}}{3\\ \\Omega} = 2\\text{ A}$.</li>\n      <li>Power consumed in $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor:\n        $$P_1 = I^2 R = (2\\text{ A})^2 \\times 2\\ \\Omega = 4 \\times 2 = \\mathbf{8\\text{ W}}$$\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Circuit (ii) [Parallel Circuit]:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>In parallel, the full voltage of $4\\text{ V}$ appears directly across the $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor.</li>\n      <li>Power consumed in $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor:\n        $$P_2 = \\frac{V^2}{R} = \\frac{4^2}{2} = \\frac{16}{2} = \\mathbf{8\\text{ W}}$$\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Comparison:</strong> The power consumed by the $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor in both circuits is <strong>identical ($8\\text{ W}$ in each case)</strong>, so the ratio is $1 : 1$.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Circuit 1: I = 2 A, P1 = I^2 * R = 8 W</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Circuit 2: V = 4 V across 2 ohm, P2 = V^2 / R = 8 W (Equal power)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q34\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q34')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q14</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Two lamps, one rated 100 W at 220 V, and the other 60 W at 220 V, are connected in parallel to electric mains supply. What current is drawn from the line if the supply voltage is 220 V?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Current drawn by $100\\text{ W}$ lamp:\n    $$I_1 = \\frac{P_1}{V} = \\frac{100\\text{ W}}{220\\text{ V}} = \\frac{5}{11}\\text{ A} \\approx 0.455\\text{ A}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Current drawn by $60\\text{ W}$ lamp:\n    $$I_2 = \\frac{P_2}{V} = \\frac{60\\text{ W}}{220\\text{ V}} = \\frac{3}{11}\\text{ A} \\approx 0.273\\text{ A}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Total current drawn from the line in parallel:\n    $$I_{\\text{total}} = I_1 + I_2 = \\frac{5}{11} + \\frac{3}{11} = \\frac{8}{11}\\text{ A} \\approx \\mathbf{0.727\\text{ A}}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Current calculations: I1 = 100/220 A and I2 = 60/220 A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Total parallel current: Itotal = 8/11 A = 0.73 A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q35\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q35')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q15</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which uses more energy, a 250 W TV set in 1 hr, or a 1200 W toaster in 10 minutes?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Energy Comparison ($E = P \\times t$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>TV Set:</strong>\n    $$P_{\\text{TV}} = 250\\text{ W}, \\quad t = 1\\text{ hr} = 3600\\text{ s}$$\n    $$E_{\\text{TV}} = 250\\text{ W} \\times 3600\\text{ s} = \\mathbf{900,000\\text{ Joules}} = 900\\text{ kJ} \\quad (\\text{or } 0.25\\text{ kWh})$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Toaster:</strong>\n    $$P_{\\text{toaster}} = 1200\\text{ W}, \\quad t = 10\\text{ min} = 10 \\times 60 = 600\\text{ s}$$\n    $$E_{\\text{toaster}} = 1200\\text{ W} \\times 600\\text{ s} = \\mathbf{720,000\\text{ Joules}} = 720\\text{ kJ} \\quad (\\text{or } 0.20\\text{ kWh})$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The <strong>250 W TV set consumes more energy</strong> ($900\\text{ kJ}$) than the 1200 W toaster ($720\\text{ kJ}$).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Energy of TV set: E = 250 W * 3600 s = 900,000 J</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Energy of toaster: E = 1200 W * 600 s = 720,000 J (TV set uses more energy)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q36\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q36')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q16</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An electric heater of resistance 8 ohm draws 15 A from the service mains 2 hours. Calculate the rate at which heat is developed in the heater.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p>The \"rate at which heat is developed\" is defined as <strong>Electrical Power ($P$)</strong>:</p>\n$$P = \\frac{H}{t} = I^2 R$$\n<p>Given: $I = 15\\text{ A}$, $R = 8\\ \\Omega$. (Note: Operating time of 2 hours is redundant when calculating the instantaneous rate/power).</p>\n$$P = (15\\text{ A})^2 \\times 8\\ \\Omega = 225 \\times 8 = \\mathbf{1800\\text{ Watts}} = \\mathbf{1800\\text{ J/s}}$$\n<p>The rate at which heat is developed is <strong>$1800\\text{ Joules per second}$ ($1800\\text{ W}$)</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rate of heat generation equals electric power: P = I^2 * R</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculation: P = 15^2 * 8 = 1800 W (J/s)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q37\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q37')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q17</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the following: (a) Why is tungsten used almost exclusively for filament of electric lamps? (b) Why are conductors of electric heating devices made of an alloy rather than a pure metal?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reasons:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Tungsten for Lamp Filaments:</strong> Tungsten has an exceptionally <strong>high melting point ($3380^\\circ\\text{C}$)</strong> and very high electrical resistivity. It can be heated to high incandescence where it emits bright white light without melting, evaporating, or burning in the presence of inert argon/nitrogen filling gas.</li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Alloys for Heating Devices:</strong> Alloys (e.g., Nichrome) have much <strong>higher resistivity</strong> than their constituent pure metals and do not oxidise (burn) at red-hot temperatures ($>800^\\circ\\text{C}$), generating intense Joule heat.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tungsten: Extremely high melting point (3380°C) and emits white light without melting</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Alloys: Higher resistivity generating high Joule heat and does not oxidise at red-hot temperatures</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q38\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q38')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q18</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the following: (a) Why is the series arrangement not used for domestic circuits? (b) How does the resistance of a wire vary with its area of cross-section? (c) Why are copper and aluminium wires usually employed for electricity transmission?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Explanations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Series Arrangement in Domestic Circuits:</strong> In series, if any one appliance burns out or is switched off, the entire circuit is broken and all other appliances turn off. Furthermore, the voltage divides among appliances ($V = V_1 + V_2 + \\dots$), so each device receives inadequate voltage and cannot operate at its rated power.</li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Resistance vs Area of Cross-section:</strong> Resistance of a wire is <strong>inversely proportional</strong> to its cross-sectional area:\n    $$R \\propto \\frac{1}{A}$$\n    Doubling the cross-sectional area halves the resistance, while halving the area doubles resistance.</li>\n  <li><strong>(c) Copper and Aluminium for Transmission:</strong> They possess <strong>extremely low electrical resistivity</strong>, which minimizes $I^2 R$ transmission line power losses over long distances. They are also ductile, lightweight, and cost-effective.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Series circuit drawbacks: breakage turns off all appliances; voltage divides</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inverse variation of resistance with cross-sectional area (R proportional to 1/A)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Copper/Aluminium have very low resistivity minimizing I^2*R transmission losses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 11</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Commercial Electricity Billing & kWh Units</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A residential house uses 4 LED bulbs of 20 W each for 8 hours daily, two electric fans of 80 W each for 10 hours daily, and a 1.5 kW electric geyser for 2 hours daily. (i) Calculate the total electrical energy consumed in commercial units (kWh) during a 30-day month. (ii) If the tariff rate is ₹ 6.50 per unit, calculate the monthly electricity bill.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Daily Energy Consumption:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Bulbs: $E_1 = 4 \\times 20\\text{ W} \\times 8\\text{ h} = 640\\text{ Wh}$</li>\n      <li>Fans: $E_2 = 2 \\times 80\\text{ W} \\times 10\\text{ h} = 1600\\text{ Wh}$</li>\n      <li>Geyser: $E_3 = 1500\\text{ W} \\times 2\\text{ h} = 3000\\text{ Wh}$</li>\n      <li>Total daily energy = $640 + 1600 + 3000 = 5240\\text{ Wh} = 5.24\\text{ kWh}$</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Monthly Energy Consumption (30 days):</strong>\n    $$E_{\\text{monthly}} = 5.24\\text{ kWh/day} \\times 30\\text{ days} = \\mathbf{157.2\\text{ kWh (units)}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Total Monthly Bill:</strong>\n    $$\\text{Cost} = 157.2 \\times ₹\\ 6.50 = \\mathbf{₹\\ 1021.80}$$\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(10)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(12)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "12": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch12\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">12</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 12: Magnetic Effects of Electric Current</h2>\n      <p>Magnetic Field Lines, Right-Hand Thumb Rule, Solenoids, Force on Conductor & Fleming's Left-Hand Rule, Electric Motor, Domestic Circuits & Earthing — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 213, 217, 220, 222, 226) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 18)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 213 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does a compass needle get deflected when brought near a bar magnet?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> A compass needle is itself a tiny, pivoted permanent bar magnet with north and south magnetic poles. When brought near another bar magnet, the magnetic field of the bar magnet exerts mechanical magnetic forces (like poles repel, unlike poles attract) on the magnetic poles of the compass needle, producing a couple that turns and deflects the needle.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Compass needle is a small pivoted bar magnet experiencing magnetic force/torque</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 213 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Draw magnetic field lines around a bar magnet. What are their properties?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Properties of Magnetic Field Lines:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Closed Loops:</strong> Outside the magnet, field lines emerge from the <strong>North pole</strong> and enter the <strong>South pole</strong>. Inside the magnet, they travel from South pole to North pole, forming continuous unbroken closed loops.</li>\n  <li><strong>Crowding Indicates Strength:</strong> The degree of closeness (density) of field lines represents the strength of the magnetic field. They are crowded closest at the poles where the field is strongest.</li>\n  <li><strong>Never Intersect:</strong> Two magnetic field lines never cross or intersect each other.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Field lines emerge from N pole and enter S pole outside forming closed continuous loops</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Line crowding indicates magnetic field strength; lines never intersect</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 213 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why don't two magnetic field lines intersect each other?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scientific Reason:</strong></p>\n<p>The tangent drawn at any point on a magnetic field line indicates the unique direction of the net magnetic field at that point.</p>\n<p>If two field lines were to cross or intersect at a point, it would mean that at the single point of intersection, the magnetic field has <strong>two different directions simultaneously</strong>. If a compass needle were placed at that intersection, it would have to point in two opposite directions at the same instant, which is physically impossible. Therefore, two magnetic field lines can never cross each other.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tangent at a point indicates the unique direction of magnetic field</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Intersection implies two directions at the same point, which is physically impossible</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 217 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Consider a circular loop of wire lying in the plane of the table. Let the current pass through the loop clockwise. Apply the right-hand rule to find out the direction of the magnetic field inside and outside the loop.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Application of Right-Hand Thumb Rule:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Inside the Loop:</strong> Grasping the circular wire with the right hand such that the thumb points in the clockwise direction of current, the fingers curl into the loop perpendicular to the plane of the table. Therefore, the magnetic field lines <strong>point downwards (into the table)</strong> inside the loop.</li>\n  <li><strong>Outside the Loop:</strong> As the field lines complete their closed loops, they curl upwards. Hence, the magnetic field lines <strong>point upwards (out of the table)</strong> outside the loop.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inside the loop: Magnetic field directed downwards into the table</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Outside the loop: Magnetic field directed upwards out of the table</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 217 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The magnetic field in a given region is uniform. Draw a diagram to represent it.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>A uniform magnetic field is represented by a set of <strong>equidistant, parallel straight lines pointing in the same direction</strong>.</p>\n$$\\longrightarrow \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad \\longrightarrow$$\n$$\\longrightarrow \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad \\longrightarrow$$\n<p>(For example, inside a long current-carrying solenoid, the magnetic field lines are parallel and equidistant, indicating uniform field strength).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equidistant, parallel straight lines pointing in the same direction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 217 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Choose the correct option: The magnetic field inside a long straight solenoid-carrying current: (a) is zero (b) decreases as we move towards its end (c) increases as we move towards its end (d) is the same at all points.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) is the same at all points</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> The magnetic field lines inside a long current-carrying solenoid run parallel to its axis and are uniformly spaced, signifying that the magnetic field is uniform (constant in magnitude and direction) at all internal points.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) is the same at all points with uniform field explanation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 220 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following property of a proton can change while it moves freely in a magnetic field? (There may be more than one correct answer.) (a) mass (b) speed (c) velocity (d) momentum.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Options:</strong> <strong>(c) velocity</strong> and <strong>(d) momentum</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> When a charged proton moves perpendicular to a magnetic field, the magnetic Lorentz force ($F = q v B$) acts perpendicular to its direction of motion. Because the force is perpendicular to velocity, it does zero work on the proton; hence its kinetic energy and <strong>speed remain strictly constant</strong>. However, the force continuously deflects the direction of motion, changing its <strong>velocity vector</strong>. Since momentum is a vector ($\\vec{p} = m \\vec{v}$), the <strong>momentum changes</strong> continuously.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Options (c) velocity and (d) momentum</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Magnetic force changes direction of motion (velocity and momentum) while speed remains constant</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 220 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In Activity 12.7, how do we think the displacement of rod AB will be affected if (i) current in rod AB is increased; (ii) a stronger horse-shoe magnet is used; and (iii) length of the rod AB is increased?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Effect on Conductor Displacement:</strong></p>\n<p>The magnetic force acting on a current-carrying conductor of length $l$ carrying current $I$ in a magnetic field $B$ is given by:</p>\n$$F = I l B \\sin\\theta$$\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) If current ($I$) is increased:</strong> The magnetic force increases ($F \\propto I$), so the displacement of rod AB <strong>increases</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) If a stronger horse-shoe magnet ($B$) is used:</strong> Magnetic field $B$ increases ($F \\propto B$), so the displacement of rod AB <strong>increases</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) If length of the rod ($l$) is increased:</strong> Force increases ($F \\propto l$), so the displacement of rod AB <strong>increases</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Current increased: Displacement increases (F proportional to I)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) Stronger magnet: Displacement increases (F proportional to B)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(iii) Length increased: Displacement increases (F proportional to l)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 220 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A positively-charged particle (alpha-particle) projected towards west is deflected towards north by a magnetic field. The direction of magnetic field is: (a) towards south (b) towards east (c) downward (d) upward.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) upward</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Application of Fleming's Left-Hand Rule:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Direction of motion of positive charge (Current, Middle finger) = <strong>West</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Direction of magnetic force/deflection (Thumb) = <strong>North</strong>.</li>\n  <li>According to Fleming's Left-Hand Rule, when the thumb points North and middle finger points West, the forefinger (Magnetic field) points <strong>vertically upward (out of the page)</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) upward</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Applying Fleming's Left-Hand Rule: current West, force North -> field points upward</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 222 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State Fleming's left-hand rule.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Fleming's Left-Hand Rule:</strong></p>\n<p>Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger of your left hand mutually perpendicular to each other:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>If the <strong>Forefinger</strong> represents the direction of the <strong>Magnetic Field</strong>,</li>\n  <li>And the <strong>Middle finger</strong> represents the direction of the <strong>Electric Current</strong>,</li>\n  <li>Then the <strong>Thumb</strong> points in the direction of the <strong>Motion or Force</strong> acting on the conductor.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>(Mnemonic: Thumb = Force/Motion, Forefinger = Field, Middle finger = Current).</em></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mutual perpendicular arrangement of thumb, forefinger, middle finger of left hand</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Forefinger = Magnetic Field, Middle finger = Current, Thumb = Force/Motion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 222 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the principle of an electric motor? What is the role of the split ring in an electric motor?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Principle of an Electric Motor:</strong></p>\n<p>An electric motor operates on the principle of the <strong>magnetic force on a current-carrying conductor</strong>. When a rectangular coil carrying electric current is placed in a magnetic field, opposite sides experience equal and opposite magnetic forces according to Fleming's left-hand rule, creating a torque that rotates the coil continuously.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Role of the Split Ring (Commutator):</strong></p>\n<p>The split ring acts as a <strong>commutator</strong>. It reverses the direction of current flowing through the two arms of the rotating coil every half rotation. Reversing current direction ensures that the torque continues to act in the <strong>same rotational direction</strong>, sustaining continuous unidirectional rotation.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Principle: Current-carrying coil in magnetic field experiences couple rotating it continuously</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Split ring commutator reverses current every half-turn ensuring continuous unidirectional rotation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 226 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name two safety measures commonly used in electric circuits and appliances.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Two Essential Safety Measures:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Electric Fuse (or MCB):</strong> A safety device consisting of a low-melting-point wire connected in series with the live wire. When current exceeds safe limits (due to overloading or short-circuiting), Joule heating melts the fuse wire, breaking the circuit to protect wiring and appliances from fire.</li>\n  <li><strong>Earthing (Earth Wire):</strong> A green/yellow insulated grounding wire connected to the metallic casing of electrical appliances that routes leakage current harmlessly into the earth, preventing lethal electric shocks.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electric fuse / MCB breaking circuit during overcurrent</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Earthing metallic body of appliances to protect users from electric shock</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 226 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An electric oven of 2 kW power rating is operated in a domestic electric circuit (220 V) that has a current rating of 5 A. What result do you expect? Explain.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Calculation and Consequence:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Current Required by Oven:</strong>\n    $$P = V \\times I \\implies I = \\frac{P}{V} = \\frac{2000\\text{ W}}{220\\text{ V}} = \\mathbf{9.09\\text{ A}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Consequence:</strong> The electric oven requires a current of $9.09\\text{ A}$, which substantially exceeds the circuit's safe current rating of $5\\text{ A}$.</li>\n  <li>This causes severe <strong>overloading</strong> of the circuit wires. The 5 A fuse will overheat and blow (melt), cutting off electric supply to prevent wires from catching fire.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Current drawn by oven: I = 2000 / 220 = 9.09 A</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Current exceeds 5 A capacity causing overloading; fuse blows/circuit breaks</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 226 • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What precaution should be taken to avoid the overloading of domestic electric circuits?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Precautions Against Overloading:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Avoid Connecting Too Many High-Power Appliances Simultaneously:</strong> Do not connect multiple high-power electrical appliances (air conditioner, geyser, heater, iron) to the same power socket or circuit line at the same time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use Fuses with Appropriate Ratings:</strong> Always ensure electric fuses/MCBs of appropriate current ratings ($5\\text{ A}$ for lighting, $15\\text{ A}$ for heating) are connected in series with live wires.</li>\n  <li><strong>Inspect Insulation:</strong> Periodically check wires to ensure insulation is intact and avoid short circuits between live and neutral wires.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Do not operate too many heavy appliances on a single socket simultaneously</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Use proper capacity fuses/MCBs and ensure good insulation to prevent short circuits</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following correctly describes the magnetic field near a long straight wire? (a) The field consists of straight lines perpendicular to the wire (b) The field consists of straight lines parallel to the wire (c) The field consists of radial lines originating from the wire (d) The field consists of concentric circles centred on the wire.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) The field consists of concentric circles centred on the wire</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> By the Right-Hand Thumb Rule, the magnetic field lines surrounding a straight current-carrying conductor are concentric circular loops lying in planes perpendicular to the conductor axis.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) concentric circles centred on the wire</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q16\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q16')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The phenomenon of electromagnetic induction is: (a) the process of charging a body (b) the process of generating magnetic field due to a current passing through a coil (c) producing induced current in a coil due to relative motion between a magnet and the coil (d) the process of rotating a coil of an electric motor.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) producing induced current in a coil due to relative motion between a magnet and the coil</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> Discovered by Michael Faraday, electromagnetic induction is the generation of an induced electromotive force (and current) across an electrical conductor situated in a changing magnetic flux.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) producing induced current due to relative motion with magnet</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q17\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q17')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The device used for producing electric current is called a: (a) generator (b) galvanometer (c) ammeter (d) motor.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(a) generator</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> An electric generator (dynamo) converts mechanical rotational energy into electrical energy based on the principle of electromagnetic induction. (A motor converts electrical energy into mechanical energy; an ammeter measures current).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (a) generator</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q18\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q18')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">The essential difference between an AC generator and a DC generator is that: (a) AC generator has an electromagnet while a DC generator has permanent magnet (b) DC generator will generate a higher voltage (c) AC generator will generate a higher voltage (d) AC generator has slip rings while the DC generator has a commutator.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) AC generator has slip rings while the DC generator has a commutator</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> An AC generator uses two continuous circular slip rings to deliver periodically reversing alternating current, whereas a DC generator uses a split-ring commutator to produce unidirectional direct current.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) AC generator has slip rings while DC generator has a commutator</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q19\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q19')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">At the time of short circuit, the current in the circuit: (a) reduces substantially (b) does not change (c) increases heavily (d) vary continuously.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(c) increases heavily</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> During a short circuit, the live wire comes into direct physical contact with the neutral wire, reducing the circuit resistance to almost zero ($R \\approx 0$). By Ohm's law ($I = V/R$), the electric current abruptly surges to a massive magnitude.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (c) increases heavily due to zero circuit resistance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q20\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q20')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State whether the following statements are true or false: (a) An electric motor converts mechanical energy into electrical energy. (b) An electric generator works on the principle of electromagnetic induction. (c) The field at the centre of a long circular coil carrying current will be parallel straight lines. (d) A wire with a green insulation is usually the live wire of an electric supply.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>True / False Statements:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) False:</strong> An electric motor converts <em>electrical energy into mechanical energy</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(b) True:</strong> Electric generators operate on Michael Faraday's principle of electromagnetic induction.</li>\n  <li><strong>(c) True:</strong> At the center of a circular coil, circular field lines expand and become parallel straight lines, representing a uniform magnetic field.</li>\n  <li><strong>(d) False:</strong> In standard colour coding, the <strong>green wire is the Earth wire</strong>. The red (or brown) wire is the live wire.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(a) False (converts electrical to mechanical); (b) True</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(c) True; (d) False (green is earth wire, red is live)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q21\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q21')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">List two methods of producing magnetic fields.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Methods of Producing Magnetic Fields:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Using Permanent Magnets:</strong> Naturally occurring magnetic ores (lodestone) or manufactured permanent bar magnets and horse-shoe magnets made of ferromagnetic alloys (Alnico).</li>\n  <li><strong>Using Electric Current:</strong> Passing electric current through a conductor (straight wire, circular loop, or solenoid/electromagnet). Moving electric charges always generate an encircling magnetic field.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Using permanent magnets (bar magnets/Alnico)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Passing electric current through conductors/solenoids (electromagnets)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q22\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q22')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does a solenoid behave like a magnet? Can you determine the north and south poles of a current–carrying solenoid with the help of a bar magnet? Explain.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Magnetic Behaviour of a Solenoid:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>A solenoid is a long cylindrical helical coil of many insulated copper turns. When an electric current flows through it, magnetic field lines emerge from one end and enter the opposite end, forming an identical magnetic field pattern to that of a <strong>bar magnet</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Determining Polarity using a Bar Magnet:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Suspend a bar magnet with known North and South poles near one end of the active solenoid.</li>\n      <li>If the North pole of the bar magnet is <strong>repelled</strong> by that end of the solenoid, that end is the <strong>North pole</strong> (like poles repel).</li>\n      <li>If the North pole is <strong>attracted</strong>, that end is the <strong>South pole</strong> (unlike poles attract).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><em>Alternatively:</em> By the Clock Face Rule, an end where current flows anti-clockwise is a North pole; clockwise is a South pole.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Solenoid magnetic field pattern is identical to that of a bar magnet</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">End repelling the bar magnet's N-pole is the N-pole; end attracting is S-pole</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Confirmation with Clock Face Rule (anticlockwise = North, clockwise = South)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q23\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q23')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">When is the force experienced by a current–carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field largest?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Condition for Maximum Magnetic Force:</strong></p>\n<p>The magnetic force on a conductor of length $l$ carrying current $I$ in a magnetic field $B$ is given by:</p>\n$$F = I l B \\sin\\theta$$\n<p>Where $\\theta$ is the angle between the direction of current and the magnetic field. The force is maximum when $\\sin\\theta$ has its maximum value of $1$ ($\\theta = 90^\\circ$).</p>\n<p>Therefore, the force is largest when the <strong>direction of the current is perpendicular ($90^\\circ$) to the direction of the magnetic field</strong> ($F_{\\text{max}} = I l B$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula F = I * l * B * sin(theta)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Force is maximum when conductor is perpendicular (theta = 90°) to magnetic field</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q24\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q24')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q10</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Imagine that you are sitting in a chamber with your back to one wall. An electron beam, moving horizontally from back wall towards the front wall, is deflected by a strong magnetic field to your right side. What is the direction of magnetic field?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Application of Fleming's Left-Hand Rule:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Direction of Current:</strong> The electron beam travels horizontally from back wall to front wall. Since electrons carry negative charge, the conventional electric current flows in the <strong>opposite direction</strong>: from <strong>front wall to back wall</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Direction of Force (Deflection):</strong> The beam is deflected to the <strong>Right</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Applying Fleming's Left-Hand Rule:</strong> Align your left hand so that the middle finger (current) points backward (towards you) and the thumb (force) points to your right. The forefinger (magnetic field) points <strong>vertically downwards (towards the floor)</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Conventional current flows opposite to electrons (front to back wall)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fleming's left-hand rule: Forefinger points vertically downwards (towards floor)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q25\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q25')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q11</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Draw a labelled diagram of an electric motor. Explain its principle and working. What is the function of a split ring in an electric motor?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electric Motor:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Principle:</strong> Based on the magnetic effect of electric current; a current-carrying loop placed in a magnetic field experiences a mechanical couple (torque) that rotates it continuously.</li>\n  <li><strong>Working:</strong> When current flows through arm AB of the rectangular coil ABCD, Fleming's left-hand rule dictates that arm AB experiences a downward force and arm CD experiences an upward force. This creates an anticlockwise torque.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role of Split Ring (Commutator):</strong> After every half rotation, the split rings reverse contact with the carbon brushes, reversing current flow through the coil. This ensures that the torque remains in the same direction, sustaining continuous rotation.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Principle: Current-carrying loop in magnetic field experiences rotational torque</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Working with Fleming's left-hand rule creating equal and opposite forces on arms AB and CD</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Split ring reverses current every half rotation maintaining unidirectional rotation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q26\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q26')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q12</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name some devices in which electric motors are used.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Electric motors are widely used in: <strong>Electric fans, refrigerators, washing machines, water pumps, mixer-grinders, hair dryers, computer hard drives, and electric cars</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electric fans, refrigerators, washing machines, water pumps, mixers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q27\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q27')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q13</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A coil of insulated copper wire is connected to a galvanometer. What will happen if a bar magnet is (i) pushed into the coil, (ii) withdrawn from inside the coil, (iii) held stationary inside the coil?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Observations based on Faraday's Law of Electromagnetic Induction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Magnet pushed into the coil:</strong> The magnetic flux linked with the coil increases rapidly. An induced current is generated, causing the galvanometer needle to undergo a <strong>momentary deflection in one direction</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Magnet withdrawn from inside the coil:</strong> The magnetic flux decreases. An induced current is produced in the opposite direction, causing the galvanometer needle to show a <strong>momentary deflection in the opposite direction</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Magnet held stationary inside the coil:</strong> Since there is no relative motion, there is zero change in magnetic flux over time ($\\frac{d\\Phi}{dt} = 0$). No current is induced; the galvanometer needle shows <strong>zero deflection</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Momentary deflection of galvanometer in one direction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) Momentary deflection in the opposite direction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(iii) Zero deflection (no induced current when stationary)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q28\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q28')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q14</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Two circular coils A and B are placed close to each other. If the current in the coil A is changed, will some current be induced in the coil B? Give reason.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Explanation (Mutual Induction):</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Yes</strong>, an electric current will be induced in coil B.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> When the electric current in coil A is altered (by switching on, off, or adjusting a rheostat), the strength of the magnetic field generated around coil A changes. Because coil B is placed close to coil A, the magnetic field lines linking coil B also change. This changing magnetic flux induces an electromotive force (EMF) and an <strong>induced electric current</strong> in coil B according to Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Yes, current is induced in coil B</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Changing current in A changes magnetic flux linking B, inducing current via mutual induction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q29\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q29')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q15</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State the rule to determine the direction of a (i) magnetic field produced around a straight conductor-carrying current, (ii) force experienced by a current-carrying straight conductor placed in a magnetic field which is perpendicular to it, and (iii) electric current induced in a coil due to its rotation in a magnetic field.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Three Fundamental Rules:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Right-Hand Thumb Rule (Maxwell's Corkscrew Rule):</strong> Imagine holding the straight current-carrying wire in your right hand with the stretched thumb pointing in the direction of electric current; then the fingers curling around the wire indicate the circular direction of the magnetic field lines.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Fleming's Left-Hand Rule:</strong> Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger of the left hand mutually perpendicular. Forefinger = Field, Middle finger = Current, Thumb = Force/Motion acting on the conductor.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Fleming's Right-Hand Rule:</strong> Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger of the right hand mutually perpendicular. Forefinger = Magnetic Field, Thumb = Motion of conductor, Middle finger = Direction of <strong>Induced Current</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Right-Hand Thumb Rule for magnetic field lines around straight wire</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) Fleming's Left-Hand Rule for magnetic force on current-carrying conductor</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(iii) Fleming's Right-Hand Rule for induced current direction in generator</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q30\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q30')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q16</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the underlying principle and working of an electric generator by drawing a labelled diagram. What is the function of brushes?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electric Generator:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Principle:</strong> Works on the principle of <strong>electromagnetic induction</strong>. When a closed armature coil is mechanically rotated in a magnetic field, the magnetic flux linking the coil continuously changes, inducing an alternating electric current.</li>\n  <li><strong>Working:</strong> As coil ABCD rotates in the magnetic field, arm AB moves upwards and arm CD moves downwards. According to Fleming's right-hand rule, induced current flows in the direction $A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D$. After half a rotation, the arms swap positions, reversing the direction of induced current, producing an alternating current (AC).</li>\n  <li><strong>Function of Brushes:</strong> Flexible carbon brushes maintain constant sliding electrical contact with the rotating slip rings to draw out the induced current into the external load circuit without twisting the wires.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Principle: Electromagnetic induction generating induced EMF from changing flux</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Working with Fleming's right-hand rule generating alternating current</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Carbon brushes maintain sliding electrical contact to export current to external load</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q31\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q31')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q17</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">When does an electric short circuit occur?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Causes of Short Circuit:</strong></p>\n<p>An electric short circuit occurs when the <strong>live wire and the neutral wire come into direct physical contact</strong> with each other without any intervening resistance or electrical load.</p>\n<p>This typically happens due to:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Damage or wear-and-tear of the protective plastic insulation coating on wires.</li>\n  <li>A fault or internal loose connection inside an electrical appliance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When live and neutral wires touch directly, circuit resistance drops to near zero, causing a massive surge of current that produces intense sparking, heating, and fire hazards.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Direct contact between live and neutral wires without electrical load</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Caused by faulty insulation or appliance defect; drops resistance to zero causing huge current surge</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q32\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q32')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q18</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the function of an earth wire? Why is it necessary to earth metallic appliances?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Function and Necessity of Earth Wire:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Safety Function:</strong> The earth wire (covered with green insulation) is physically connected to the metallic casing of high-power electrical appliances (iron, toaster, refrigerator) and grounded into the earth via a buried metal plate.</li>\n  <li><strong>Preventing Electric Shocks:</strong> If live wire insulation becomes worn and touches the metal casing of the appliance, the metal body is energized to $220\\text{ V}$. Because the earth wire offers an <strong>almost zero-resistance path</strong> to ground, the leakage current flows directly into the earth rather than through the human body.</li>\n  <li>The surge in current instantly blows the circuit fuse or trips the MCB, safeguarding the user from fatal electric shocks.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Connects metallic appliance casing to earth providing a low-resistance path</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Safely routes leakage current to ground and trips fuse, preventing fatal electric shocks</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q33\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q33')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Board Benchmark • Q33</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is an electromagnet? How does it differ from a permanent magnet?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Electromagnet:</strong> A temporary magnet consisting of a coil of insulated wire wound around a soft iron core that exhibits strong magnetism only while an electric current passes through it.\n    <ul>\n      <li>Its polarity and field strength can be easily controlled by changing the current.</li>\n      <li>It can be easily demagnetised by switching off the current.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Permanent Magnet:</strong> Made of steel or alloys like Alnico that retain their magnetism permanently without an external current source. Its strength and polarity cannot be easily changed.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electromagnet: temporary magnet active only during current flow with adjustable strength</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Permanent magnet: retains permanent magnetic field regardless of external current</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q34\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q34')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Board Benchmark • Q34</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the difference between Direct Current (DC) and Alternating Current (AC). Why is AC preferred for long distance power transmission?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>DC vs AC:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Direct Current (DC):</strong> Electric current that flows strictly in one constant direction over time (e.g., from dry cells and batteries). Frequency is $0\\text{ Hz}$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Alternating Current (AC):</strong> Electric current that periodically reverses its direction of flow at regular intervals of time. In India, AC reverses direction every $1/100\\text{ second}$, having a frequency of $50\\text{ Hz}$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why AC is Preferred for Transmission:</strong> AC voltage can be stepped up to very high voltages ($132\\text{ kV}, 400\\text{ kV}$) using step-up transformers for long-distance transmission, which drastically reduces current ($I$) and minimizes Joule heat losses ($I^2 R$) over hundreds of kilometres. It is then safely stepped down to $220\\text{ V}$ for domestic consumption.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">DC flows in one direction (0 Hz); AC periodically reverses direction (50 Hz in India)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">AC can be stepped up to high voltage using transformers, reducing transmission heat loss</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 12</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Domestic Wiring Architecture</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">In a standard domestic electric circuit, three distinct wires enter a household from the municipal poles: Live wire, Neutral wire, and Earth wire. (i) What is the potential difference between the live wire and the neutral wire in Indian households? (ii) In which wire is the main electric fuse and switch always connected and why? (iii) What color insulation is standardly used for live, neutral, and earth wires?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Potential Difference:</strong> The potential difference between the live wire ($220\\text{ V}$) and the neutral wire ($0\\text{ V}$) is <strong>$220\\text{ Volts}$</strong> at an AC frequency of $50\\text{ Hz}$.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Connection of Switch and Fuse:</strong> The switch and fuse are <strong>always connected in the Live wire</strong>. When the switch is turned off or the fuse blows, the appliance is completely isolated from the high voltage ($220\\text{ V}$) supply. If connected in the neutral wire, the appliance would remain live even when turned off, posing a lethal electrocution risk to anyone touching it.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Color Insulation Standards:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Live wire:</strong> Red (or Brown)</li>\n      <li><strong>Neutral wire:</strong> Black (or Light Blue)</li>\n      <li><strong>Earth wire:</strong> Green (or Green with yellow stripes)</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(11)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(13)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "13": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch13\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">13</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 13: Our Environment</h2>\n      <p>Ecosystem Components, Food Chains & Webs, 10% Energy Flow Rule, Biomagnification, Ozone Layer Depletion & Waste Management — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Pages 242, 245) &amp; Chapter-End Exercises (Questions 1 to 9)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 242 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are trophic levels? Give an example of a food chain and state the different trophic levels in it.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>Each step, stage, or feeding level in a food chain where transfer of energy and organic matter takes place from one group of organisms to another is called a <strong>trophic level</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Example of a Terrestrial Grassland Food Chain:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>First Trophic Level ($T_1$):</strong> <strong>Producers</strong> (Green Grass) &rarr; captures solar energy via photosynthesis.</li>\n  <li><strong>Second Trophic Level ($T_2$):</strong> <strong>Primary Consumers / Herbivores</strong> (Grasshopper) &rarr; feeds on grass.</li>\n  <li><strong>Third Trophic Level ($T_3$):</strong> <strong>Secondary Consumers / Primary Carnivores</strong> (Frog) &rarr; feeds on grasshopper.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fourth Trophic Level ($T_4$):</strong> <strong>Tertiary Consumers / Top Carnivores</strong> (Snake / Hawk) &rarr; feeds on frog.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of trophic levels as sequential steps of energy transfer in a food chain</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Food chain example with 4 trophic levels (Grass -> Grasshopper -> Frog -> Snake)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 242 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the role of decomposers in the ecosystem?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Crucial Roles of Decomposers (Bacteria and Fungi):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Nutrient Recycling (Biogeochemical Cycling):</strong> Decomposers secrete extracellular digestive enzymes that break down complex organic remains of dead plants and animal wastes into simple inorganic substances (carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus). These nutrients are returned to the soil, water, and air, where they are reabsorbed by green plants.</li>\n  <li><strong>Natural Scavenging and Cleansing:</strong> They decompose organic carcasses and agricultural residues, preventing environmental fouling and the piling up of dead matter on Earth.</li>\n  <li><strong>Maintaining Ecological Equilibrium:</strong> Without decomposers, all essential mineral nutrients would remain locked inside dead biomass, leading to exhaustion of soil fertility and collapse of the ecosystem.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mineral recycling: breaking down dead biomass into simple inorganic soil nutrients</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Natural scavengers preventing dead organic matter accumulation and replenishing soil fertility</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 245 • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is ozone and how does it affect any ecosystem?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Ozone ($\\text{O}_3$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Structure:</strong> Ozone is an allotrope of oxygen consisting of three oxygen atoms bonded together ($\\text{O}_3$). While it is a deadly poison at ground level, high up in the stratosphere it forms a protective <strong>ozone layer</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Protective Ecological Role:</strong> The stratospheric ozone layer acts as a celestial shield, absorbing more than $99\\%$ of harmful <strong>ultraviolet (UV-B) radiation</strong> emitted by the Sun.</li>\n  <li><strong>Consequences of UV Penetration:</strong> If the ozone layer is depleted, excessive UV radiation reaching the Earth's surface causes skin cancer, cataract blindness, immune suppression in humans, and damages photosynthetic phytoplankton, crippling marine aquatic food chains.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ozone is O3 forming a stratospheric layer shielding Earth from harmful solar UV radiation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Depletion causes skin cancer, cataracts, immune damage, and destroys phytoplankton food chains</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">In-Text Page 245 • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How can you help in reducing the problem of waste disposal? Give any two methods.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Methods to Reduce Waste Disposal Problems:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Segregation and Composting of Biodegradable Waste:</strong> Separate domestic garbage into biodegradable (vegetable peels, leftover food, dry leaves) and non-biodegradable waste at the source. Biodegradable waste can be converted into rich organic compost or biogas in local pits, reducing landfill pressure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Adopting the 3R Principles (Reduce, Reuse, Recycle):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Recycle non-biodegradable materials such as plastic bottles, glass, and metal scrap.</li>\n      <li>Replace single-use polythene bags with reusable cloth or jute bags.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Waste segregation at source and composting biodegradable kitchen wastes into manure</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Practicing 3Rs (Reduce, Reuse, Recycle) especially for plastics, glass, and metals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following groups contain only biodegradable items? (a) Grass, flowers and leather (b) Grass, wood and plastic (c) Fruit-peels, cake and lime-juice (d) Cake, wood and grass.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Options:</strong> <strong>(a), (c), and (d)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>(a) Grass, flowers, and leather are all natural organic items decomposed by bacteria.</li>\n  <li>(c) Fruit-peels, cake, and lime-juice are organic foodstuffs that are readily biodegradable.</li>\n  <li>(d) Cake, wood, and grass are also entirely biodegradable.</li>\n  <li>Group (b) contains <strong>plastic</strong>, which is synthetic and non-biodegradable.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Groups (a), (c), and (d) contain only biodegradable items; plastic in (b) is non-biodegradable</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following constitute a food-chain? (a) Grass, wheat and mango (b) Grass, goat and human (c) Goat, cow and elephant (d) Grass, fish and goat.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(b) Grass, goat and human</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> A food chain represents the sequential transfer of energy from producer to consumer. Grass (producer) is eaten by a goat (primary consumer/herbivore), which in turn is consumed by a human (secondary consumer/omnivore).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (b) Grass, goat and human with producer-consumer sequence</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Which of the following are environment-friendly practices? (a) Carrying cloth-bags to put purchases in while shopping (b) Switching off unnecessary lights and fans (c) Walking to school instead of getting your mother to drop you on her scooter (d) All of the above.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[1 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong> <strong>(d) All of the above</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reason:</strong> All three practices conserve fossil fuels, reduce plastic waste, conserve electrical energy, and minimize environmental pollution.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Option (d) All of the above</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What will happen if we kill all the organisms in one trophic level?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Ecological Consequences:</strong></p>\n<p>If all organisms at a specific trophic level are eliminated, it triggers a devastating ecological imbalance throughout the entire food web:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Starvation of Higher Trophic Levels:</strong> The predators in the next higher trophic level will face an immediate food crisis, leading to mass starvation, population collapse, or migration.</li>\n  <li><strong>Overpopulation of Lower Trophic Levels:</strong> The prey organisms in the preceding lower trophic level, freed from predatory control, will multiply uncontrollably. For example, if all carnivores (lions) are removed, herbivorous deer will multiply excessively, overgrazing and destroying all grassland vegetation and causing desertification.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Higher trophic levels starve and decline due to absence of food source</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lower trophic levels multiply uncontrollably causing overgrazing/destruction of producers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Will the impact of removing all the organisms in a trophic level be different for different trophic levels? Can the organisms of any trophic level be removed without causing any damage to the ecosystem?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Analysis:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Impact at Different Levels:</strong> Yes, the impact will differ in nature depending on the level removed:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Removing <strong>producers ($T_1$)</strong> is catastrophic and immediately collapses the entire ecosystem, as all life directly or indirectly depends on primary photosynthetic energy.</li>\n      <li>Removing herbivores ($T_2$) causes producers to overgrow and carnivores to starve.</li>\n      <li>Removing decomposers leads to accumulation of dead biomass and arrest of nutrient cycling.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Can any trophic level be removed without damage?</strong> <strong>No</strong>, organisms of no trophic level can be removed without causing significant damage. In an ecosystem, every organism is interlinked in complex food webs; removing any level destabilizes ecological balance.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Yes, impact varies (removing producers is most fatal to all life)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">No trophic level can be removed without causing damage due to interdependent food web links</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q10\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q10')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is biological magnification? Will the levels of this magnification be different at different levels of the ecosystem?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Biological Magnification (Biomagnification):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Definition:</strong> The progressive increase in the concentration of toxic, non-biodegradable chemical pollutants (such as DDT, mercury, cadmium) in the tissues of living organisms at each successive trophic level of a food chain is called <strong>biological magnification</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Variation Across Trophic Levels:</strong> Yes, the levels of magnification are drastically different at different trophic levels. Because non-biodegradable chemicals cannot be metabolized or excreted, they accumulate in body fat. Each predator consumes many prey organisms, concentrating the toxin.</li>\n  <li>Consequently, the concentration of the chemical increases exponentially with each higher trophic level, reaching the <strong>maximum concentration in top carnivores / humans</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition: Progressive accumulation and concentration of non-biodegradable toxins along a food chain</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Levels increase at each successive trophic level, reaching maximum concentration in top trophic level (humans)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q11\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q11')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the problems caused by the non-biodegradable wastes that we generate?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Problems Caused by Non-biodegradable Wastes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Soil and Water Pollution:</strong> Synthetic plastics, electronic waste, and toxic chemicals persist in the environment for centuries without decomposing, leaching heavy metals into ground water and contaminating soils.</li>\n  <li><strong>Biological Magnification:</strong> Toxic chemicals enter food chains and accumulate in tissues of animals and humans, causing organ failure, neurological damage, and cancer.</li>\n  <li><strong>Blockage of Drainage & Stray Animal Death:</strong> Discarded plastic bags choke municipal sewage drains causing floods, and when ingested by stray cattle, cause fatal intestinal blockage.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Long persistence polluting soil and groundwater; entering food chains causing biomagnification</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Choking municipal drains and ingestion by stray cattle causing fatal intestinal blockages</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q12\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q12')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">If all the waste we generate is biodegradable, will this have no impact on the environment?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Critical Evaluation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>No, even biodegradable waste will have serious negative impacts if generated in excessive amounts or handled improperly.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reasons:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Foul Odour and Air Pollution:</strong> Large mounds of decomposing organic waste emit noxious, foul-smelling gases like hydrogen sulphide ($\\text{H}_2\\text{S}$) and methane ($\\text{CH}_4$), which is also a potent greenhouse gas driving global warming.</li>\n  <li><strong>Breeding Ground for Pathogens:</strong> Rotting organic matter serves as an ideal breeding ground for flies, mosquitoes, and rodents, spreading epidemic diseases like cholera, typhoid, and dengue.</li>\n  <li><strong>Eutrophication of Water Bodies:</strong> If excessive biodegradable matter enters rivers and lakes, decomposer microbes consume all dissolved oxygen (high Biological Oxygen Demand, BOD), causing aquatic hypoxia and mass fish mortality.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Excessive biodegradable waste still causes foul gases (H2S, methane greenhouse gas)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Breeds disease vectors (flies/mosquitoes) and causes aquatic eutrophication/fish mortality</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q13\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q13')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">NCERT Exercise • Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is damage to the ozone layer a cause for concern? What steps are being taken to limit this damage?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Concerns and Mitigation Measures:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Why It Is a Concern:</strong> The ozone layer shields Earth from harmful solar ultraviolet-B (UV-B) radiation. Depletion of this protective layer allows dangerous UV rays to penetrate the atmosphere, leading to:\n    <ul>\n      <li>High incidence of skin cancers and melanoma in humans.</li>\n      <li>Cataracts and damage to human eyes and immune systems.</li>\n      <li>Destruction of young marine phytoplankton and reduction in crop yields.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Steps Taken:</strong> In 1987, the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) successfully forged an international agreement called the <strong>Montreal Protocol</strong> to freeze and phase out the production of <strong>Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)</strong> used in refrigerators, aerosols, and air-conditioners. Today, all manufacturing companies produce CFC-free appliances worldwide.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Concerns: Increased UV radiation causing skin cancer, cataracts, immune decline, and phytoplankton loss</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Remedial steps: Montreal Protocol (UNEP 1987) banning/freezing CFCs and adopting CFC-free alternatives</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q14\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q14')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Board Benchmark • Q14</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State the 10 percent law of energy transfer in a food chain. If 20,000 J of solar energy is available to plants, how much energy will be transferred to a snake in the food chain: Plants -> Mice -> Snake -> Peacock?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>10 Percent Law (Raymond Lindeman, 1942):</strong></p>\n<p>Only about <strong>$10\\%$ of the energy</strong> entering a particular trophic level is incorporated into its biomass and made available for transfer to the next higher trophic level. The remaining $90\\%$ is lost as metabolic heat, respiration, and excretion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Energy Calculation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Plants capture only about $1\\%$ of incident solar energy:\n    $$E_{\\text{Plants}} = 1\\% \\text{ of } 20,000\\text{ J} = \\mathbf{200\\text{ J}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Energy transferred to Mice (Herbivore, $T_2$):\n    $$E_{\\text{Mice}} = 10\\% \\text{ of } 200\\text{ J} = \\mathbf{20\\text{ J}}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Energy transferred to Snake (Secondary Consumer, $T_3$):\n    $$E_{\\text{Snake}} = 10\\% \\text{ of } 20\\text{ J} = \\mathbf{2\\text{ Joules}}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Therefore, exactly <strong>2 Joules</strong> of energy is available to the snake.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">10% law definition: only 10% energy moves to next trophic level</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculation: Plants (200 J) -> Mice (20 J) -> Snake (2 J)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q15\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q15')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Board Benchmark • Q15</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Differentiate between biodegradable and non-biodegradable substances. Give two examples of each.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Parameter</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Biodegradable Substances</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Non-biodegradable Substances</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Biological Breakdown</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Broken down into simple harmless substances by the action of biological decomposers (bacteria/fungi).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Cannot be broken down by saprophytic microorganisms and persist indefinitely.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Environmental Impact</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Do not accumulate or cause chronic ecological toxicity.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Accumulate in environment, causing soil/water pollution and biomagnification.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Vegetable peels, paper, cattle dung, cotton cloth.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Plastics, polythene, DDT, glass, aluminium cans.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Biodegradable: broken down by decomposers (e.g. paper, vegetable peels)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Non-biodegradable: cannot be broken down by microbes (e.g. plastics, DDT)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 13</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Kulhads vs Disposable Plastic Cups</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">At Indian railway stations, trains previously served tea in reusable plastic cups, then shifted briefly to earthen clay cups (kulhads), and now use disposable paper cups. (i) What was the environmental problem caused by mass usage of disposable plastic cups? (ii) Why was mass use of earthen kulhads abandoned? (iii) Why are paper cups considered the best eco-friendly alternative?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Plastic Cups Issue:</strong> Disposable plastic cups are non-biodegradable, choke municipal drainage lines, litter railway tracks, and when incinerated, release highly toxic dioxin fumes.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Kulhad Drawback:</strong> Producing billions of earthen kulhads on a nationwide scale required stripping millions of tons of fertile agricultural topsoil. Firing them in kilns also consumed large quantities of wood fuel, causing topsoil depletion and deforestation.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Advantage of Paper Cups:</strong> Disposable paper cups are biodegradable, decompose naturally in compost without generating toxic residues, do not deplete topsoil, and can be recycled.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(12)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <div></div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n"
};
